&lt;p&gt;AWS Transform for mainframe now discovers business functions with field-level precision and generates a new requirements coverage report in the assess and reimagine workflow. Previously, business functions that used the same table or file were grouped together, and you had to build your own traceability reporting from AWS Transform artifacts. With this launch, you can start modernizing z/OS COBOL and PL/I workloads from a more accurate catalog of business functions and verify requirements coverage before you build.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Business functions are now grouped only when they affect the same data fields. They also include screens and queues and carry clearer names and descriptions. The new requirements coverage report shows how every part of your application is accounted for in the generated requirements. Every line of source code is classified by how it was handled, every business rule links to the exact lines it came from, and every requirement is backed by the business rules that support it. You can review coverage from the full portfolio down to a single line before moving into forward engineering in Kiro and other IDEs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These capabilities are available in all AWS Regions where AWS Transform for mainframe is available. For more information, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/transform/mainframe/"&gt;AWS Transform for mainframe&lt;/a&gt; or see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/"&gt;AWS Transform for mainframe documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/"&gt;Amazon CloudWatch&lt;/a&gt; now supports bearer token authentication, using API keys (bearer tokens), as an additional option for sending OpenTelemetry traces to the AWS X-Ray OpenTelemetry Protocol (OTLP) traces endpoint. Any OTLP/HTTP client that can set an Authorization header, including the OpenTelemetry Collector and OpenTelemetry SDK exporters, can now authenticate with a bearer token instead of signing requests with AWS Signature Version 4 (SigV4). This helps teams send traces from workloads that can't use the AWS SDK or short-term credentials, such as applications running on premises or in other clouds, CI/CD pipelines, and third-party tools that support only a static Authorization header.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;SigV4 remains the recommended authentication method for sending traces to CloudWatch, and the AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry (ADOT) SDKs continue to use SigV4 by default with no changes required. Traces sent with either method get the same CloudWatch capabilities, including Transaction Search, Amazon CloudWatch Application Signals, and Amazon CloudWatch Omni.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Bearer token&amp;nbsp;authentication for traces is available in all commercial AWS Regions where the AWS X-Ray OTLP traces endpoint is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/xray/latest/devguide/xray-opentelemetry-bearer-token.html"&gt;Setting up bearer token authentication for traces&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS X-Ray Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) Capability for Argo CD now supports Argo CD Notifications, providing notifications when your applications sync, fail, or change health. You define triggers and message templates in your cluster and subscribe individual Applications with an annotation, the same way you do in upstream Argo CD.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, you can deliver notifications to 16 notification services, including Slack, PagerDuty, Opsgenie, Amazon SQS, and Amazon EventBridge. The Amazon SQS and Amazon EventBridge services authenticate with your IAM Capability Role, so you can route deployment events into your own event-driven workflows without storing credentials in your cluster. For a service that requires third-party credentials such as a Slack token, you can specify an AWS Secrets Manager ARN in place of the secret and EKS will automatically use it on your behalf.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Notifications are available in all AWS Regions where the EKS Capability for Argo CD is available, at no additional charge. Standard charges apply for resources related to the services your notifications use, for example Amazon SQS or Amazon EventBridge. To learn more, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/eks/features/"&gt;Amazon EKS&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;and refer to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/argocd-notifications.html"&gt;Amazon EKS User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/oracle/"&gt;Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for Oracle&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;now supports M8i and R8i instances in additional AWS Regions. Both M8i and R8i instances are now available in Asia Pacific (New Zealand) and Canada West (Calgary), and R8i instances are now available in Europe (Milan, Stockholm, and Zurich) and Israel (Tel Aviv).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These instances are powered by custom Intel Xeon 6 processors, available only on AWS, delivering the highest performance and fastest memory bandwidth among comparable Intel processors in the cloud. The M8i and R8i instances offer up to 15% better price-performance, and 2.5x more memory bandwidth compared to previous generation Intel-based instances.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;M8i and R8i instances are available for Amazon RDS for Oracle in Bring Your Own License model for Oracle Database Enterprise Edition (EE) and Oracle Database Standard Edition 2 (SE2). For EE, instances are available in sizes from large to 48xlarge, as well as bare metal sizes metal-48xl and metal-96xl. For SE2, instances are available in sizes from large to 4xlarge and bare metal sizes metal-48xl and metal-96xl. Refer to &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/oracle/pricing/"&gt;Amazon RDS for Oracle Pricing&lt;/a&gt; for available instance configurations, pricing, and region availability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A new Anthropic model is now available in the Kiro IDE and CLI for the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. With Haiku 5.5, all three Claude 5.5 models are now available in Kiro in AWS GovCloud (US).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Claude Haiku 5.5 is the fastest model in the Claude 5.5 family. It's a clear step up from Claude Haiku 4.5, built for work that runs in large volumes and needs quick responses. Developers can use it to summarize and compact long context, classify and route requests, process text in bulk, and make small, targeted edits across many files. It also works well as a review subagent that runs in parallel, while Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5 take on harder planning and coding work. Haiku 5.5 uses adaptive thinking across five effort levels from low to max, with medium as the default. It's available with a 1M context window and a 0.06x credit multiplier. Requests over 100K tokens are billed at a 0.3x multiplier.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Ensure your IDE or CLI is updated to the latest version, then restart it to access the new model from the model selector. For more details, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/govcloud-us/latest/UserGuide/govcloud-kiro.html"&gt;GovCloud documentation&lt;/a&gt;, the &lt;a href="https://kiro.dev/docs/enterprise/monitor-and-track/"&gt;monitoring and tracking guide&lt;/a&gt;, or contact your AWS account team. To learn more about Kiro, visit the &lt;a href="https://kiro.dev/"&gt;Kiro product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, Amazon RDS supports per snapshot billing for Amazon RDS snapshots. Per snapshot billing allows you to get the cost of each individual snapshot.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, RDS snapshot charges were aggregated at the database instance level on the Cost and Usage report as well as the Cost Explorer. This meant that while understanding the snapshot charges, you could only see the aggregate view at the database instance instead of drilling down to each individual snapshot. Now, you can use cost allocation tags on your snapshots to see exactly how much each snapshot costs, giving you a better understanding of your backups spend and budgets. Per snapshot billing is enabled by default on all RDS snapshots for no additional charge.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Per snapshot billing is available for all Amazon RDS engines in all commercial AWS Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. To learn more, please visit our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_WorkingWithAutomatedBackups.html" target="_blank"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For organizations that use Amazon Quick, the desktop application now signs users in with the same sign-in method the organization already uses for Quick on the web. Administrators no longer need to register an OIDC application in their identity provider or set up separate desktop sign-in before users can get started. If a user can sign in to Quick on the web, that user can sign in to the desktop application.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To turn on desktop access, an administrator adds the desktop connector once in the Amazon Quick console. Users then enter their Quick account name, choose Sign In, and finish signing in through their browser. This works for accounts that use IAM Identity Center, IAM federation, or native Amazon Quick users. If your organization wants to manage desktop sign-in separately, you can still set up a dedicated identity provider configuration with providers such as Microsoft Entra ID, Google Workspace, Okta, or Ping Identity.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Quick on desktop is available in &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/regions.html#regions-qs" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Regions that support the full Amazon Quick feature set&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/amazon-quick-desktop.html"&gt;Amazon Quick on desktop&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Quick User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Aurora MySQL-Compatible Edition&lt;/a&gt; 8.4 supports MySQL 8.4.10. We recommend upgrading to the newer minor versions to accept fixes for Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs) in prior versions of MySQL and to benefit from bug fixes, performance improvements, and new functionality added by the MySQL community. For more details, refer to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraMySQLReleaseNotes/AuroraMySQL.Updates.84Updates.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Aurora MySQL 8.4&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/relnotes/mysql/8.4/en/news-8-4-10.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MySQL 8.4.10&lt;/a&gt; release notes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Aurora MySQL 8.4.10 adds support for global transaction identifiers (GTID) tags, which lets you assign a user-defined tag to&amp;nbsp;GTIDs&amp;nbsp;to help you&amp;nbsp;identify and group related transactions in binary log replication. This release also adds&amp;nbsp;support for a maximum of 50,000 simultaneous connections per DB instance, which allows connection-intensive applications to scale without adding a connection pooling layer. Learn more about maximum connections best practices in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraMySQL.Managing.Performance.html#AuroraMySQL.Managing.MaxConnections" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Aurora MySQL User Guide&lt;/a&gt;. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can upgrade your databases during scheduled maintenance windows using automatic minor version upgrades. To simplify operations at scale, enable &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraMySQL.Updates.AMVU.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;automatic minor version upgrades&lt;/a&gt; and use the AWS Organizations &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/Aurora.Maintenance.AMVU.UpgradeRollout.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Upgrade Rollout Policy&lt;/a&gt; to orchestrate upgrades across your clusters in phases, validating on lower-priority environments before upgrading your most critical ones. You can perform minor version upgrades in-place or via snapshot restore. Learn more about performing version upgrades in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraMySQL.Updates.Upgrading.html" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon Aurora User Guide&lt;/a&gt;. This release is supported in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/Concepts.RegionsAndAvailabilityZones.html#Aurora.Overview.Availability.MySQL" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Aurora MySQL is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Aurora&lt;/a&gt; is designed for high performance and availability at global scale with full MySQL compatibility. It provides scale-to-zero serverless compute, Aurora Global Database for multi-Region resilience, Aurora I/O-Optimized for improved price performance on I/O-intensive workloads, and built-in security and continuous backups. To get started, take a look at Aurora's &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/CHAP_AuroraOverview.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;getting started page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Lambda now supports &lt;a href="https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/spaces/KAFKA/pages/255070434/KIP-932+Queues+for+Kafka"&gt;queues for Apache Kafka&lt;/a&gt; in &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kafka.html"&gt;self-managed Kafka event source mappings&lt;/a&gt; (ESM), for Kafka clusters that customers run themselves and for managed offerings such as Confluent Cloud.&amp;nbsp;Customers can now scale processing for Kafka workloads beyond the partition count of a topic, increasing throughput during traffic spikes and improving processing resilience.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers building event-driven Kafka workloads for use cases such as image and video encoding, fraud detection, notification delivery, and telemetry ingestion treat each event as an independent unit of work, where processing speed matters more than strict ordering. Previously, Kafka ESMs used the Kafka consumer group protocol, which assigns each partition to exactly one consumer within a consumer group. Customers needing higher parallelism had to over-partition topics or move traffic to a separate queuing system, adding infrastructure to operate and producer code to maintain. Queue support for self-managed Kafka ESMs is built on Kafka share groups, which are generally available in Apache Kafka 4.2. This launch enables multiple event pollers to consume concurrently from the same partition, with each event poller supporting up to 10 concurrent invocations. This decouples parallelism from partition count with no changes to producers, topic configuration, or function code. As a result, customers get higher throughput during traffic spikes and more resilient processing, because a slow or failed event no longer holds up other events in the same partition. Queue mode does not preserve partition ordering and is intended for workloads that do not require it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability requires a Kafka cluster with share groups enabled, such as Apache Kafka 4.2 or later, and is available in all &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities"&gt;AWS commercial Regions&lt;/a&gt; where self-managed Kafka ESM is available. To get started, create a self-managed&amp;nbsp;Kafka ESM with ConsumptionMode set to Queue through the AWS Management Console, Lambda API, AWS CLI, AWS CloudFormation, AWS SAM, or AWS CDK. Standard Lambda pricing applies. Customers who configure &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/kafka-scaling-modes.html#kafka-provisioned-mode"&gt;provisioned mode&lt;/a&gt; for their self-managed Kafka ESM also pay for Event Poller Unit (EPU) usage. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/polling-kafka-queue.html"&gt;AWS Lambda Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/pricing/"&gt;AWS Lambda pricing&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, Amazon Aurora MySQL-Compatible Edition 3 (compatible with MySQL 8.0) supports MySQL 8.0.46, which includes community MySQL fixes and Aurora-specific improvements. In addition to operational improvements, Aurora MySQL 3.14 introduces support for post-quantum TLS (PQ-TLS) key exchange, providing you with post-quantum cryptography options for encrypting your data in-transit. For detailed information on this release, refer to &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraMySQLReleaseNotes/AuroraMySQL.Updates.30Updates.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Aurora MySQL 3&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/relnotes/mysql/8.0/en/news-8-0-46.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MySQL 8.0.46&lt;/a&gt; release notes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can upgrade your databases during scheduled maintenance windows using automatic minor version upgrades. To simplify operations at scale, enable &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraMySQL.Updates.AMVU.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;automatic minor version upgrades&lt;/a&gt; and use the AWS Organizations &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/Aurora.Maintenance.AMVU.UpgradeRollout.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Upgrade Rollout Policy&lt;/a&gt; to orchestrate upgrades across your clusters in phases. You can perform minor version upgrades in-place or via snapshot restore. This release is supported in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/Concepts.RegionsAndAvailabilityZones.html#Aurora.Overview.Availability.MySQL" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Aurora MySQL is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Aurora&lt;/a&gt; is designed for high performance and availability at global scale with full MySQL compatibility. It provides scale-to-zero serverless compute, Aurora Global Database for multi-Region resilience, Aurora I/O-Optimized for improved price performance on I/O-intensive workloads, and built-in security and continuous backups. To get started, take a look at Aurora’s &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/CHAP_AuroraOverview.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;getting started page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Inspector now extends its vulnerability scanning to EC2 Amazon Machine Images (AMIs), enabling you to identify software vulnerabilities in your AMIs without launching instances or installing additional software. This helps you fix issues at the source before AMIs are used to launch instances across your fleet.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this capability, you can run continuous evaluations to monitor your AMI catalog for newly discovered vulnerabilities, or perform targeted one-time scans for specific AMIs directly from the EC2 Console. You can enable AMI scanning for an account or across your entire organization in Amazon Inspector. For AWS Security Hub customers, you can enable AMI scanning as an add-on across your organization, and findings are also delivered to Security Hub. Based on the scan results, you can enable the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-allowed-amis.html"&gt;Allowed AMIs&lt;/a&gt; setting and specify security criteria that AMIs must meet to be available within your account and enforce this setting across the entire organization using Declarative Policies. Customers building AMIs with &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/imagebuilder/latest/userguide/integ-inspector.html#integ-inspector-ami-scan"&gt;EC2 Image Builder&lt;/a&gt; can integrate scanning directly into their pipelines and restrict distribution of AMIs that do not meet their security standards.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AMI Scanning helps you detect and remediate vulnerabilities earlier in your deployment pipeline, reducing the risk of deploying compromised compute resources. Security teams can now establish a consistent vulnerability scanning process across all AMIs improving their security posture before instances are launched.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all commercial AWS Regions where Amazon Inspector is available. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ami-scanning.html"&gt;EC2 User Guide&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/inspector/latest/user/what-is-inspector.html"&gt;Amazon Inspector User Guide&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/inspector/pricing/"&gt;Amazon Inspector pricing page&lt;/a&gt;, and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/inspector/"&gt;Amazon Inspector product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Textract is now available in the Asia Pacific (Tokyo) and Europe (Zurich) Regions. Amazon Textract is a machine learning service that automatically extracts text, handwriting, and data from scanned documents, going beyond simple optical character recognition (OCR) to identify and extract text and structured data.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At launch in these Regions, Amazon Textract supports text detection and document analysis. This includes the DetectDocumentText, AnalyzeDocument, StartDocumentTextDetection, GetDocumentTextDetection, StartDocumentAnalysis, and GetDocumentAnalysis operations. The AnalyzeExpense, AnalyzeID, and AnalyzeLending operations, and Custom Queries (adapters), are not available in these Regions at this time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, Amazon Textract is available in a total of 18 AWS Regions: US East (Ohio), US East (N. Virginia), US West (N. California), US West (Oregon), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Canada (Central), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (Ireland), Europe (London), Europe (Paris), Europe (Spain), the AWS GovCloud (US-East) and AWS GovCloud (US-West) Regions, and the newly added Asia Pacific (Tokyo) and Europe (Zurich) Regions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/textract/"&gt;Amazon Textract product page&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/textract/"&gt;Amazon Textract Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing and Region availability, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/textract/pricing/"&gt;Amazon Textract pricing page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/textract.html"&gt;Amazon Textract endpoints and quotas&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS for Fluent Bit announces version &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/aws-for-fluent-bit/releases/tag/v3.0.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;3.0.0&lt;/a&gt;, based on Fluent Bit version 4.1.1 and Amazon Linux 2023. Container logging using AWS for Fluent Bit is now more performant and more feature-rich for AWS customers, including those using &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ecs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Elastic Container Services&lt;/a&gt; (Amazon ECS) and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/eks/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service&lt;/a&gt; (Amazon EKS).&lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; AWS for Fluent Bit enables Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS customers to collect, process, and route container logs to destinations including Amazon CloudWatch Logs, Amazon Data Firehose, Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, and Amazon S3 without changing application code. AWS for Fluent Bit 3.0.0 upgrades the Fluent Bit version to 4.1.1, and upgrades the base image to Amazon Linux 2023. These updates deliver access to the latest Fluent Bit features, significant performance improvements, and enhanced security. New features include native OpenTelemetry (OTel) support for ingesting and forwarding OTLP logs, metrics, and traces with AWS SigV4 authentication. Performance improvements include faster JSON parsing, processing more logs per vCPU with lower latency. Security enhancements include TLS min version and cipher controls, which enforce your TLS policy on outputs from AWS for Fluent Bit for stronger protocol posture.&lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; You can use AWS for Fluent Bit 3.0.0 on both ECS and EKS. On ECS, update the FireLens log-router container image in your task definition to the 3.0.0 tag from the Amazon ECR Public Gallery. On EKS, upgrade by either updating the Helm release or setting the DaemonSet image to the 3.0.0 version.&lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; The &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/developerguide/firelens-using-fluentbit.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS for Fluent Bit image&lt;/a&gt; is available in the &lt;a href="https://gallery.ecr.aws/aws-observability/aws-for-fluent-bit" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon ECR Public Gallery&lt;/a&gt; and in the Amazon ECR repository. You can also find it on &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/aws-for-fluent-bit" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt; for source code and additional guidance.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;i&gt;10/29/2025 - This post has been updated to accurately reflect the Fluent Bit version at launch.&lt;br&gt; 11/02/2026 - This post has been updated to clarify the scope of OpenTelemetry support.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Textract now provides an updated text detection model that improves accuracy, expands language support, and enhances multilingual table extraction. The updated model applies to the DetectDocumentText and AnalyzeDocument APIs and requires no changes to your Amazon Textract API requests or response schema. Your applications are served by the updated model automatically, so you can continue calling Amazon Textract exactly as before.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The updated model expands language coverage to more than 40 languages, including Albanian, Arabic, Basque, Belarusian, Bosnian, Bulgarian, Chinese (Simplified and Traditional), Croatian, Czech, Danish, Dutch, English, Estonian, Finnish, French, German, Hebrew, Hindi, Hungarian, Icelandic, Italian, Japanese, Korean, Latvian, Lithuanian, Luxembourgish, Norwegian, Polish, Portuguese, Romanian, Russian, Serbian Cyrillic, Serbian Latin, Slovak, Slovenian, Spanish, Swedish, Turkish, Turkmen, and Ukrainian, enabling reliable processing of previously unsupported scripts. Accuracy improvements include enhanced long-word recognition and handwriting detection. Additionally, multilingual table extraction is more robust on merged cells, skewed or rotated tables, and partial or corrupted tables. These improvements help you accurately process invoices, forms, identity documents, and other multilingual or complex documents, including those with tables, at scale.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The updated Amazon Textract model is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Textract is available. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/textract/"&gt;Amazon Textract product page&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/textract/"&gt;Amazon Textract Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon CloudFront now supports AWS Service Quotas, so you can view, monitor, and request increases for 28 CloudFront quotas from the Service Quotas console or API.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Until now, increasing a quota on resources such as distributions, cache policies, origins, or alternate domain names meant opening a support case and waiting for manual review. If you have AWS Business Support or higher, requests for limit increases within published thresholds are now approved automatically. For example, you can raise your distribution quota from 500 to 1,000, or the number of alternate domain names per distribution from 100 to 500, without opening a support case. Some quotas also publish usage metrics to Amazon CloudWatch every 5 minutes, so you can set alarms and request an increase before you reach a limit..&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon CloudFront is supported.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/servicequotas/home#!/services/cloudfront/quotas"&gt;the Service Quotas console&lt;/a&gt; to view and request increases for your CloudFront quotas. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/cloudfront-limits.html"&gt;Amazon CloudFront quotas&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon CloudFront Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Compute Optimizer now provides rightsizing recommendations for 389 additional Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) instance types in AWS GovCloud (US-West) and 276 in AWS GovCloud (US-East) Regions. Customers running regulated and government workloads can find the same cost and performance opportunities on current-generation instance types without manual analysis.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Compute Optimizer has expanded support to include the latest generation families in every workload category. The additions include Compute Optimized (C7i, C7i-flex, C8g, C8gn), General Purpose (M7i, M7i-flex, M8g, M8gd, M8i, M8i-flex), Memory Optimized (R7i, R8g, R8gd, R8i, R8i-flex), Memory Intensive (X2iedn, X8i), and Storage Optimized (I7i, I7ie, I8g, I8ge) instance types. With this expansion, Compute Optimizer supports 724 instance types across 102 families in AWS GovCloud (US-West) and 542 instance types across 69 families in AWS GovCloud (US-East), in both EC2 instance and EC2 Auto Scaling group recommendations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This expanded coverage is available today at no additional cost in the AWS GovCloud (US-West) and AWS GovCloud (US-East) Regions. You can start using Compute Optimizer through the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, or AWS SDK. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/compute-optimizer/"&gt;AWS Compute Optimizer product page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/govcloud-us/latest/UserGuide/govcloud-compute-optimizer.html"&gt;AWS Compute Optimizer in the AWS GovCloud (US) User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock now supports the OpenAI GPT-5.6 models (Terra and Luna) on the bedrock-runtime endpoint, with support for the Responses, Converse, and Chat Completions APIs in AWS GovCloud (US). It also adds support for Cross-Region Inference, allowing customers to use Cross-Region Inference within AWS GovCloud (US) regions to access higher throughput and increased redundancy.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Cross-Region Inference automatically routes inference requests across both AWS GovCloud (US) Regions to give you higher throughput, without you needing to manage capacity across multiple Regions. Cross-Region Inference within AWS GovCloud (US) routes requests across both regions so you can scale while maintaining US data sovereignty, giving you the broadest access to Bedrock capacity and the highest throughput during demand spikes. This launch also expands API support; you can use OpenAI GPT models with the Responses API, Chat Completions API, and the Converse API on the bedrock-runtime endpoint. Because these native OpenAI APIs now run on bedrock-runtime, the models work with the same account-level controls you already use for other models on Bedrock: usage appears in Bedrock model invocation logging (deliverable to Amazon S3 or Amazon CloudWatch Logs) and in Amazon CloudWatch metrics covering invocation counts, token counts, latency, throttles, and errors, and it is itemized in AWS Cost Explorer and the AWS Cost and Usage Report so you can attribute spend by model.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Cross-Region Inference for OpenAI models is available in all AWS Regions where OpenAI models on Amazon Bedrock are offered. To get started, review the model cards for GPT-5.6 (Terra and Luna) in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/"&gt;Amazon Bedrock User Guide.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Kiro web search gives the agent real-time access to current information on the internet, so its answers draw on up-to-date search results and page content. The agent uses web search automatically when a question involves recent changes or topics beyond the model's training data. Answers that use web results include citations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Kiro enterprise administrators can turn off web search and web fetch for their organization from the Kiro console under Settings. Teams can also remove web tools from individual custom agents, or block them with capability-based permission rules.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For more details, visit the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/govcloud-us/latest/UserGuide/govcloud-kiro.html"&gt;AWS GovCloud (US) documentation&lt;/a&gt;, the &lt;a href="https://kiro.dev/docs/enterprise/monitor-and-track/"&gt;monitoring and tracking guide&lt;/a&gt;, or contact your AWS account team. To learn more about Kiro, visit the &lt;a href="https://kiro.dev/"&gt;Kiro product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security teams need powerful tools to find vulnerabilities, test exploits, and build defenses. Daybreak Red from OpenAI is now available to eligible customers on Amazon Bedrock in AWS GovCloud (US-East), bringing advanced AI to this work.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Part of OpenAI’s Daybreak cyber defense initiative, Daybreak Red: GPT-5.6 Cyber is built for authorized vulnerability research, exploit reproduction, and mitigation development. It gives verified defenders more room to investigate complex security problems, with fewer refusals for this work and stronger identity verification, monitoring, and access controls.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Daybreak Red runs on Bedrock’s next-generation inference engine, with zero-operator access enforced at the chip. Your inference data is not used to train models, and you do not need to share your Daybreak Red data with OpenAI.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The model is available in US East (Ohio) and AWS GovCloud (US-East). Access to the model requires &lt;a href="https://openai.com/form/enterprise-trusted-access-for-cyber/"&gt;enrollment in Daybreak access&lt;/a&gt; from OpenAI.&amp;nbsp;To get started, contact OpenAI or your AWS account team about Daybreak eligibility. Once approved, work with your AWS account team to enable access.&amp;nbsp;Learn more in the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/accelerate-cyber-defense-with-openai-and-aws-daybreak-red-daybreak-blue-now-available-to-eligible-customers-on-amazon-bedrock/"&gt;blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon CloudFront can now forward two new optional headers to your origin. The CloudFront-Viewer-ISP header contains the name of the viewer's internet service provider (ISP), so you can see which network a request came from. The CloudFront-Viewer-TLS-Details header describes the cryptography used on each viewer's TLS connection, so you can see whether a connection used post-quantum cryptography (PQC).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With CloudFront-Viewer-ISP, you can break down traffic and performance by network. You can also isolate unusual traffic from a single provider. CloudFront-Viewer-TLS-Details includes the key exchange, signature scheme, and signature public key type for each connection. You can use it to measure the share of viewers that actually connect with PQC and identify clients that do not yet do so. To forward either header to your origin, add it to an origin request policy and attach the policy to your distribution.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/adding-cloudfront-headers.html"&gt;adding CloudFront headers&lt;/a&gt; and&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/controlling-origin-requests.html"&gt;controlling origin requests with a policy&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon CloudFront Developer Guide&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon MSK now supports version 1.3 of Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol for MSK Provisioned clusters through encryption policies. TLS 1.3 delivers faster, more secure encryption for streaming data and is increasingly required by compliance frameworks and enterprise security standards. It is also one of the prerequisites for post-quantum cryptography for protecting data against attacks that collect encrypted traffic now with the intent to decrypt it later.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers can opt in to TLS 1.3 by selecting a named security policy in their MSK cluster configuration, with no change to existing clusters until a policy is applied. A named security policy defines which TLS version and cipher suites a cluster can accept. With this launch, Amazon MSK provides a set of policies customers can select and any connection that does not meet the selected policy requirements is rejected. This allows customers to stay in control of their security and compliance posture. Policies that support both TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 allow connections to negotiate the highest version the client supports allowing clients that support TLS 1.3 to connect on it immediately while clients on TLS 1.2 continue to connect on TLS 1.2. This lets customers upgrade their clients at their pace before applying a stricter policy. Customers can also select stricter policies that align closer with their compliance requirements to restrict connections to TLS 1.3 only.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where Amazon MSK Provisioned is available. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/security.html"&gt;Amazon MSK documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/oracle/"&gt;Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for Oracle&lt;/a&gt; now lets you turn off automatic minor version upgrades on new and existing DB instances that use the JVM, Spatial, Locator, or OLAP option. Previously, these options required automatic minor version upgrades to be enabled.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this change, you decide when to apply minor version upgrades to DB instances using these options. If you turn off automatic minor version upgrades, we recommend that you apply new minor versions regularly so your DB instances keep the latest Oracle security fixes. To keep automatic minor version upgrades turned on and still control the order in which your environments are upgraded, you can use &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/RDS.Maintenance.AMVU.UpgradeRollout.html"&gt;AWS Organizations upgrade rollout policy&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This change is available in &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;all commercial AWS Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon RDS for Oracle is available. To learn more about options available in Amazon RDS for Oracle, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Appendix.Oracle.Options.html"&gt;Adding options to Oracle DB instances&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon RDS User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Connect Customer now supports versioning and aliasing for flows and flow modules, simplifying how you build, test, and deploy customer experience flows while minimizing impact to live end-customer traffic. With this launch, you can now create immutable versions of a flow and attach one or more friendly names (i.e., aliases) to a version, then point resources such as queues, quick connects, and phone numbers at an alias instead of a specific version.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Rolling out a new version takes a single update, with no need to republish or relink each resource. For example, you can publish an updated customer experience flow as a new version and validate it in a test environment while live traffic stays on the current version, then repoint the production alias to move every phone number to the new version in a single update.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Connect Customer is offered. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/flows-versions-aliases.html"&gt;Flow versioning and aliasing&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Connect Customer Administrator Guide. To learn more about Amazon Connect Customer, an agentic AI solution that helps enterprises deliver exceptional customer experiences, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/products/connect/customer/"&gt;Amazon Connect Customer website.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Managed Prometheus collectors now support Amazon MSK consumer-group metrics for &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/prometheus/"&gt;Amazon Managed Service for Prometheus&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/"&gt;Amazon CloudWatch&lt;/a&gt;. The integration collects Kafka exporter metrics and consumer-group lag metrics from your Amazon MSK clusters.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Managed Prometheus collectors discover and collect Prometheus metrics from your workloads, so you don't run and operate collection agents yourself. You ingest the collected metrics into an Amazon Managed Service for Prometheus workspace or into Amazon CloudWatch, where you query and analyze them. You monitor Kafka stream health and consumer performance with consumer-group metrics. This helps you detect processing bottlenecks in your streaming applications.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where managed Prometheus collectors are generally available. To learn more, see the documentation for &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prometheus/latest/userguide/prom-msk-kafka-exporter.html"&gt;Amazon Managed Service for Prometheus&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/managed-prometheus-collectors-msk-kafka-exporter.html"&gt;Amazon CloudWatch&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Glue Data Catalog now supports automatic table optimization for Apache Iceberg tables in the US West (N. California) Region. Apache Iceberg is an open table format that delivers fast query performance over large tables in data lakes. With this launch, you can enable table optimization for individual tables or for all tables in your Data Catalog.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Apache Iceberg tables accumulate small files, snapshots, and unreferenced files with every write, which slows queries and increases storage costs over time. Table optimization continuously monitors your tables to compact small files in the background, remove snapshots that are past your retention settings, and delete unreferenced files that are no longer needed. You can monitor each run from the AWS Glue console, Amazon CloudWatch metrics, and logs.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can enable table optimization using the AWS Glue console, AWS CLI, or AWS SDKs. For the full list of Regions where table optimization is available, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/regions-optimizers.html"&gt;Supported Regions for table optimizers&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/table-optimizers.html"&gt;Optimizing Iceberg tables&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Glue Developer Guide. To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/glue/"&gt;AWS Glue product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker AI inference now supports GPU partitioning, enabling you to run several models on a single GPU instead of dedicating a whole accelerator to each one. Using NVIDIA Multi-Instance GPU (MIG) technology, SageMaker AI divides a GPU into as many as seven isolated partitions, each with its own memory and compute. Customers serving small and mid-size models, such as fine-tuned variants, embedding models, rerankers, and classifiers, previously had to give every model an entire accelerator, so an instance hosted no more models than it had GPUs and paid for GPU capacity those models could never use.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With GPU partitioning, you choose how to divide the GPUs behind your endpoint and place each model on a partition sized to fit it. Several models then share one accelerator with hardware-enforced isolation, so a busy model cannot take memory or throughput from the models beside it. Right-sizing an accelerator to a workload also opens up newer instance generations that would otherwise be more capacity than a single model needs. You configure GPU partitioning when you create an endpoint that hosts inference components, and you continue to invoke each model by name, so your application code doesn't change.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;GPU partitioning is available on P4d, P5, G7, and G7e instances in US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (Oregon), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Europe (Frankfurt), and Europe (Ireland). To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/ai/deploy/"&gt;Amazon SageMaker AI deployment page&lt;/a&gt; and see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/inference-components-accelerator-partitioning.html"&gt;Amazon SageMaker AI Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Quick introduces Quick Essentials for enterprises. The plan makes it easier for organizations to get started with all the features of Quick such as AI-powered chat, Research, Flows, and Apps, and lets users view and interact with Quick Sight dashboards and reports and then manage adoption at the desired pace.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;In AWS Regions where Amazon Quick is available, Quick Sight Reader users are upgraded to Quick Essentials at no additional per-user cost. With Quick Essentials, Quick Sight Readers can ask their dashboards questions in plain language, like what drove a revenue dip in March, and dig into the details with follow-up questions. They can turn the answer into a document, presentation, or spreadsheet for their team. When they need more context, Research pulls from their data, files, and the web into a detailed report with citations. If they need the same update every week, Flows can build it and send it automatically. When they need a tool that doesn't exist yet, like a request tracker, they can describe it and Apps builds it, without writing code.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Existing dashboards, Favorites, and Recents stay in place. Row-level and column-level security apply to every answer, and data is not shared across customers or used to train shared models. Administrators continue to manage the capabilities with &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/custom-permissions.html" target="_blank"&gt;custom permission&lt;/a&gt; profiles that can be applied to any user or a role.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Quick Essentials is enabled in a phased rollout across &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/regions.html#regions-qs" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;AWS Regions where Quick AI features are available&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, read the &lt;a href="https://community.amazonquick.com/t/every-reader-gets-professional-capabilities/53958" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Amazon Quick Community blog post&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing details, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/quick/pricing/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon Quick pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces that AWS partners who operate multiple legal entities can publish a product listing once in their primary account and enable their local subsidiaries to sell it through direct private offers — eliminating the need to recreate and maintain duplicate listings across every account.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;From the primary account, which retains ownership of the listing, partners authorize which products each subsidiary can sell and which buyer countries it can extend private offers to. Each subsidiary transacts under its own local tax identity, selects the local currencies it supports, and sets its own disbursement preferences and bank account. The AWS Marketplace insights report in the primary account provides a consolidated view of offers, agreements, and renewals across all connected subsidiary accounts. Partners can revoke a subsidiary authorization or a subsidiary connection at any time, which immediately stops that subsidiary from creating new offers — agreements and offers already extended to buyers remain unchanged.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available today at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where AWS Marketplace is available. To get started, see &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/partner-central/latest/getting-started/manage-subsidiary.html"&gt;Managing subsidiary account connections&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; and &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/marketplace/latest/userguide/subsidiary-offers.html"&gt;Creating private offers as a subsidiary account&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/sqlserver/" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Amazon RDS for SQL Server&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/oracle/" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Amazon RDS for Oracle&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; now support RDS storage autoscaling on &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PIOPS.ModifyingExisting.AdditionalVolumes.html" target="_blank"&gt;additional storage volumes&lt;/a&gt;. RDS storage autoscaling automatically scales storage capacity in response to growing database workloads, with zero downtime.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Additional storage volumes allow you to add up to three additional volumes to increase your storage capacity up to 256 TiB. Previously, you had to manually provision storage capacity on the additional storage volumes based on anticipated application capacity demands while autoscaling could be enabled on the primary storage volume. Under-provisioning could result in application downtime, and over-provisioning could result in under-utilized resources and&amp;nbsp;higher costs. With RDS storage autoscaling, you simply set your desired maximum storage limit for each additional storage volume, and autoscaling takes care of the rest.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;RDS storage autoscaling continuously monitors actual storage consumption, and scales capacity up automatically when actual utilization approaches provisioned storage capacity. Autoscaling works with new and existing database instances. Autoscaling has no additional charges and you only pay for the provisioned storage resources. You can enable it by using the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, or Amazon RDS API.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;RDS storage autoscaling for additional storage volumes is available for Amazon RDS for SQL Server and Amazon RDS for Oracle in all commercial AWS Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. To learn more, please visit&amp;nbsp;the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PIOPS.Autoscaling.html"&gt;Amazon RDS storage autoscaling&lt;/a&gt; documentation.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible Edition&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;introduces support for post-quantum TLS (PQ-TLS) key exchange, providing you with post-quantum cryptography options for encrypting your data in transit.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL versions 18 and higher support modification of the ssl_groups parameter. You can &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraPostgreSQL.Security.html#PostgreSQL.Concepts.General.SSL.Groups" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;select cryptographic groups&lt;/a&gt; from the&amp;nbsp;Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL allow list to align with your organization's security standards.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Aurora is designed for high performance and availability at global scale with full PostgreSQL compatibility. It provides scale-to-zero serverless compute, Aurora Global Database for multi-Region resilience, Aurora I/O-Optimized for improved price performance on I/O-intensive workloads, and built-in security and continuous backups. To get started, take a look at our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/CHAP_GettingStartedAurora.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;getting started page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/msk/features/msk-connect/"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Amazon MSK Connect&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka Connect) now lets you update the custom plugin and worker configuration of an existing connector. This is useful when you need to update the custom plugin to run a new version of your connector code, for example one with updated dependencies. You can also update the worker configuration to change worker-level settings, such as switching key and value converters or tuning producer and consumer properties for throughput and latency.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon MSK Connect is a fully managed service that allows you to deploy, monitor, and scale Apache Kafka Connect connectors with &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/msk/"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Amazon MSK&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka). Previously, updating a connector's plugin or worker configuration meant deleting the connector and recreating it - a disruptive operation that added risk and operational overhead. With this launch, you can update a connector's plugin and worker configuration in place. The connector keeps its committed offsets, so its tasks continue processing from where they left off once the update completes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where Amazon MSK Connect is supported. To learn more, visit &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-connect.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Amazon MSK Connect developer guide&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can now use your AWS Step Functions workflows as tools for AI agents through Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway. Your agent decides when to run a workflow and what input to pass, and Step Functions runs each step exactly as you defined it. Agents can use your existing Standard and Express workflows as tools.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AWS Step Functions is a visual workflow service capable of orchestrating over 220 AWS services to help customers build distributed applications at scale. AgentCore Gateway connects AI agents to tools through the Model Context Protocol (MCP). With this launch, your agents can run multi-step business processes in the same order every time. For example, a customer support agent can start your refund workflow, and Step Functions runs the eligibility check, manager approval, and credit steps, so every refund follows your policy. Agents can also look up workflow runs, so an operations agent can tell your on-call engineer which order runs failed overnight and where, letting them go straight to fixing the step that broke.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway is available. You pay standard rates for Step Functions and AgentCore Gateway.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To get started, follow the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/tutorial-agentcore-gateway.html"&gt;tutorial&lt;/a&gt; in the Step Functions Developer Guide. For all supported operations and setup options, see Step Functions connector targets in the AgentCore Gateway &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/gateway-target-connector-step-functions.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;. If you build with an AI coding assistant such as Kiro, the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws"&gt;Agent Toolkit for AWS&lt;/a&gt; gives it skills and guidance for building on AWS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) is now available in two additional AWS Regions: Canada (Central) and Africa (Cape Town). DAX is a fully managed, highly available, in-memory cache for Amazon DynamoDB that delivers up to ten times performance improvement, from single-digit milliseconds to microseconds, even at millions of requests per second.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With DAX, you can accelerate read-intensive and bursty workloads such as real-time bidding, gaming leaderboards, and retail product catalogs, while offloading read traffic from your DynamoDB tables. DAX is API-compatible with DynamoDB, so you can add microsecond-latency caching to your applications with minimal code changes. Customers can now keep cached data within the Region and meet low-latency requirements while benefiting from a fully managed service, including automated patching and failover.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/dax/"&gt;Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator page&lt;/a&gt;. For the full list of Regions where DAX is available, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Regional Services page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Managed Grafana now supports in-place upgrade from Grafana version 12.4 to 13.2. Upgrading to version 13.2 brings features launched as part of open-source Grafana versions 13.0 to 13.2. These include Git Sync for dashboards, dynamic dashboards, and PromQL query support in the Amazon CloudWatch data source plugin.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With Git Sync, you can treat dashboards as code by linking a workspace to a Git repository. Dashboard modifications can be tracked, reviewed, and reverted through version control. Dynamic dashboards bring a more adaptable approach to layout and paneling. You can construct responsive views driven by conditions that respond to the data and variables in scope. With Amazon CloudWatch data source plugin support for Prometheus Query Language, you can use PromQL to query metrics that CloudWatch takes in via its OpenTelemetry (OTLP) endpoint, in addition to the existing Metric Search and Metrics Insights query types.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In-place upgrade to Grafana 13.2 is supported in all &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon Managed Grafana is generally available. You can upgrade with just a few clicks from the AWS Console, AWS SDK or AWS CLI. For a complete list of new features, refer to &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/grafana/latest/userguide/version-differences.html#version-diff-v13"&gt;Differences between Grafana versions&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Managed Grafana User Guide. For upgrade instructions, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/grafana/latest/userguide/AMG-workspace-version-update.html"&gt;Update your workspace version&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about Amazon Managed Grafana features and its pricing, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/grafana/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/grafana/pricing/"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS DataSync Enhanced mode now supports Amazon FSx for Windows File Server and Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP as source or destination locations. You can now run Windows to Windows transfers, such as from an on-premises file server to FSx for Windows File Server. Additionally, Enhanced mode agents can now run on Red Hat OpenShift Virtualization version 4.20 or later.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, you can use the same location options in Enhanced mode as in Basic mode. Enhanced mode processes data in parallel for higher performance, removes file count limitations, and provides detailed data transfer metrics. OpenShift Virtualization expands your agent deployment options alongside VMware ESXi, KVM, Microsoft Hyper-V, and Amazon EC2.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can get started by choosing Enhanced mode when you create a task in the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/datasync/home"&gt;AWS DataSync console&lt;/a&gt;. These capabilities are available in all AWS Regions where DataSync Enhanced mode is supported. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/what-is-datasync.html"&gt;AWS DataSync documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, Amazon SageMaker AI announces support for Seekable OCI (SOCI)-indexed container images for training and processing jobs, so your jobs start running your code sooner. SOCI-indexed images start Amazon SageMaker AI training containers in seconds, cutting GPU idle time during image pulls by up to 90%, with no impact on training performance. Before a training or processing job can run, SageMaker AI downloads its container image to the instance. For deep learning images, which routinely exceed 10 GB, that download adds minutes to the start of every job, and you pay for that time. Now, when your image includes a SOCI index, SageMaker AI lazy-loads the image, fetching only the files needed to start the container and streaming the rest in the background as your code runs.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To use this feature, reference a SOCI-indexed image from a private Amazon ECR repository in your training or processing job. You can use a pre-indexed AWS Deep Learning Container by changing the image tag to its -soci variant, or add a SOCI index to your own image in Amazon ECR with open-source tools, without rebuilding the image. No changes to your job definition, entry point, or data channels are needed. SageMaker AI checks for an index automatically and images without an index download as before. If an index can't be used, the job falls back to a standard download and continues running.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;SOCI-indexed images for SageMaker AI training and processing jobs are available today in all AWS Regions where SageMaker AI is available, through the SageMaker AI APIs, CLIs, SDKs, and Console. To learn more, see the documentation for &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/train-soci-indexed-images.html"&gt;training jobs&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/processing-job-soci-indexed-images.html"&gt;processing jobs&lt;/a&gt;, or read the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/announcing-soci-indexed-images-for-faster-sagemaker-ai-jobs/"&gt;launch blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Backup now supports cross-account management, cross-account backup, and delegated administrators in the AWS European Sovereign Cloud (Germany) Region. These capabilities help you centrally manage, monitor, and copy backups across the accounts in your organization using AWS Organizations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With cross-account management, you can deploy organization-wide backup policies from your AWS Organizations management account or a delegated administrator account. You can also monitor backup, copy, and restore jobs across all of your organization accounts from one place. With cross-account backup, you can copy recovery points to a backup vault in another account in your organization, which adds protection against accidental or malicious deletion in the source account. You can also register a member account as a delegated administrator, so your teams can manage backups across your organization without needing access to your management account.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;There is no additional charge to use these capabilities; standard AWS Backup pricing applies. To learn more, see the AWS Backup documentation at &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/"&gt;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/&lt;/a&gt;. To get started, visit the AWS Backup console at &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/backup"&gt;https://console.aws.amazon.com/backup&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/"&gt;Amazon Redshift &lt;/a&gt;announces automated cross-region snapshot copy for Amazon Redshift. You can now have your Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster or serverless workgroup automatically backed up to the target AWS region(s) you choose. The automated cross-region snapshot copy feature will automatically, always copy the most recent snapshot with no manual intervention, continuously and incrementally and ensure your cluster is backed-up to the destination region(s), giving you additional options for business continuity.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The benefit to enabling the new automated cross-region snapshot copy feature is that you do not need to manually intervene to initiate a cross-region snapshot copy when an unplanned outage event occurs. Also, this feature takes the most recent snapshot available on your system to copy to the destination region, so you don't have to wait for the next snapshot to be taken, making it independent of your snapshot schedule on your local cluster. To configure cross-region snapshot copy, you need to enable the automated cross-region snapshot copy feature for each cluster and configure the destination region(s) to copy snapshots and how long to keep copied automated snapshots in the destination Region. You can choose the retention period for automated snapshots in the destination region. User snapshots are retained until you explicitly delete them. To restore the snapshot in the destination region, use the snapshot restore feature to a new provisioned cluster or serverless workgroup. Snapshots copied with the automated cross-region snapshot copy feature have names prefixed with `copy` in the destination region.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all regions AWS Commercial and GovCloud (US) regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For more details on configuration and usage see the Amazon Redshift documentation sections for cross-region snapshot copy: &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/working-with-snapshots.html"&gt;Amazon Redshift snapshots and backups&lt;/a&gt;. For details on restoring a cluster from a snapshot, see the documentation for &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/working-with-snapshot-restore-cluster-from-snapshot.html"&gt;Restoring a cluster from a snapshot&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing information, please review the 'Backup and storage' and 'Data transfer' sections of the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/pricing/"&gt;Amazon Redshift pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio now supports custom designations, named permission sets that control what actions individual project members can perform. Previously, project members could only be assigned Owner or Contributor designations, which provided nearly identical permissions. With custom designations, administrators define permission sets that align with their organization's personas and assign them to project members. Designations govern all actions within a project, providing administrators with a single permission model for separation of duties.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Administrators create designations in the SageMaker Unified Studio administration portal by selecting permissions grouped into categories such as Catalog, Subscriptions, Connections, Notebooks, and Data Agent. For example, a "Data Steward" designation might include permissions to approve subscription requests and publish assets, while an "Analyst" designation is limited to querying data and running notebooks. Each designation is defined at the domain level and assigned across any project in that domain. When a member performs an action, SageMaker Unified Studio evaluates their assigned designation and permits or denies the request. Data engineers, stewards, analysts, and automated pipelines can now work within the same project with differentiated access, removing the need to create separate projects to isolate permissions. Every domain includes two managed designations, Owner and Contributor, and administrators can create up to 10 additional designations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/supported-regions.html"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio is available. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/designations.html"&gt;Designations&lt;/a&gt; documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/products/connect/customer/"&gt;Amazon Connect Customer&lt;/a&gt; Outbound Campaigns now provides flow designer analytics for journeys, so you can see directly on a journey flow how many customers reached each step and which branch they took. A journey is a multi-step outbound campaign that reaches your customers by voice, email, SMS, or WhatsApp, and branches based on customer behavior. Campaign managers can use these metrics to find the steps in a journey that need changes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A collections team running a payment reminder journey can see how many customers moved from the first SMS reminder to a follow-up call. If the SMS step shows a high error rate, the team can add an email step to its error branch so future customers on that path still get a reminder.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can use flow designer analytics for journeys in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), Africa (Cape Town), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Canada (Central), Europe (Frankfurt), and Europe (London).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/monitor-flow-performance.html"&gt;Monitor flow performance with metrics in flow designer analytics mode&lt;/a&gt; in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/what-is-amazon-connect.html"&gt;Amazon Connect Customer Administrator Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/oracle/"&gt;Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for Oracle&lt;/a&gt; now supports the September 2026 Monthly Recommended Patch (MRP) for Oracle Database version 19c and 26ai. An MRP is a minor engine version that builds on a quarterly Release Update (RU), bundling Oracle's Critical Security Patch Update (CSPU) with additional Oracle-recommended fixes. The September 2026 MRP is applied on top of the July 2026 RU and includes the &lt;a href="https://www.oracle.com/security-alerts/cspusep2026.html#AppendixDB"&gt;Oracle September CSPU&lt;/a&gt;. We recommend upgrading to the September 2026 MRP as it includes the latest security updates for Oracle Database.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This release also includes the updated Oracle time zone file (DST version 46), which reflects the latest daylight saving time rule changes in several regions, including British Columbia and Alberta in Canada (effective November 1, 2026) and Morocco and Western Sahara (effective September 20, 2026), which are moving to permanent UTC offsets. If you store timestamps that include time zone information for these areas, the updated time zone file keeps that data aligned with the new rules.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can apply the September 2026 MRP for new database instances, or upgrade existing instances to engine version 19.0.0.0.ru-2026-07.mrp-2026-09.r2 or 26.0.0.0.ru-2026-07.mrp-2026-09.r2 from the Amazon RDS Management Console, or by using the AWS SDK or CLI. To learn more about Oracle MRPs supported on Amazon RDS for each engine version, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/OracleReleaseNotes/Welcome.html"&gt;Amazon RDS for Oracle Release notes&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS announces the general availability of Amazon Bedrock AgentCore CLI version 1.0 (@aws/agentcore 1.0.0), rebuilt with faster startup, a consistent command structure, and support for more AgentCore resources. The CLI helps developers create, develop, deploy, and operate AI agents and supporting resources from the terminal.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With version 1.0, developers can use the project workflow or work directly with harnesses, runtimes, memory, identity, gateways, and payments without first adding them to a project. Developers can invoke existing resources by ID or ARN, open an interactive shell, export a harness, and use searchable menus, resource pickers, and guided workflows. Consistent global flags provide region selection, JSON output, debugging, and version information across commands.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Existing projects can be migrated to AgentCore version 1 using the agentcore migrate command.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Install AgentCore CLI version 1.0 from &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@aws/agentcore"&gt;npm&lt;/a&gt; or explore the source on &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/agentcore-cli"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/agentcore/"&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore product page&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/agentcore-cli-reference.html"&gt;AgentCore CLI reference&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/agentcore-get-started-cli.html"&gt;Get started with Amazon Bedrock AgentCore&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon ElastiCache Serverless for Valkey now supports public endpoints in the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. A public endpoint lets you connect to your cache from a serverless function or any application running outside a VPC, without setting up a VPN, a bastion host, or an SSH tunnel.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With a public endpoint, ElastiCache Serverless gives you a fully managed cache with no VPC to configure and no infrastructure to provision, and you can create one in under a minute. Every connection uses IAM authentication over TLS 1.3, so there's no password to store or rotate. Connect using Valkey GLIDE 2.2 or later, which has built-in IAM support, or another Valkey client paired with the Developer Toolkit for ElastiCache, an open-source library that generates and refreshes IAM authentication tokens for you.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this expansion, public endpoints for ElastiCache Serverless are now available in all commercial AWS Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and the China Regions. There is no additional charge for using public endpoints beyond standard ElastiCache Serverless pricing.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, create a Valkey 9.0 or later serverless cache with a public endpoint using the AWS Management Console, AWS SDK, or AWS CLI. To learn more about public endpoints, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/serverless-public-endpoints-chapter.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Create a Valkey serverless cache with a public endpoint&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Lambda now supports &lt;a href="https://github.com/awslabs/soci-snapshotter"&gt;Seekable OCI (SOCI)&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;for lazy loading &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/lambda-functions/"&gt;Event Functions&lt;/a&gt; packaged as Open Container Initiative (OCI) images running on &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/lambda-managed-instances/"&gt;Lambda Managed Instances&lt;/a&gt;. With SOCI support, Lambda fetches only the files needed at each stage of the execution environment startup rather than downloading the entire image upfront, reducing container startup times by up to 70%.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers building compute-intensive applications such as data processing pipelines, high-throughput API backends, and batch computation workloads require substantial memory and CPU resources to process large datasets, serve low-latency responses at scale, and run complex computations efficiently. Container images for these workloads can reach several gigabytes, and downloading and preparing the full image can take several minutes when provisioning new execution environments. SOCI support on Lambda Managed Instances eliminates this bottleneck by streaming only the required files at each stage of the execution environment startup, so your functions start faster as you scale.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, create a &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-managed-instances-seekable-oci.html#lambda-managed-instances-seekable-oci-create-index"&gt;SOCI index&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;(a table of contents for the files in your image layers) for your container image and push it to &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ecr/"&gt;Amazon ECR&lt;/a&gt;. When creating or updating your function, set the image URI to the SOCI-indexed tag in Amazon ECR (for example, my-function:v1-soci) and publish a function version. Lambda automatically detects the SOCI index and enables lazy loading with no additional configuration. If you use a coding assistant such as Kiro, Claude Code, or Codex, install the Lambda Managed Instances skill from the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/agent-setup-guide.html"&gt;Agent Toolkit for AWS&lt;/a&gt; to configure and deploy functions through your agent.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The feature is available in all &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities"&gt;AWS commercial Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Lambda Managed Instances is available. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-managed-instances-seekable-oci.html"&gt;AWS Lambda Managed Instances developer guide&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, we're announcing support for time series collections on the next generation of Amazon OpenSearch Serverless. With time series collections, you can ingest, store, and analyze logs, metrics, traces, and event data without provisioning or managing clusters. They absorb ingest spikes automatically, keep recent data fast to query, and make months or years of history searchable at low cost, all in the same collection.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Time series collections add a new, lower-cost warm storage tier in addition to the existing hot storage tier, so customers can retain their infrequently accessed data without paying hot storage prices. Customers can choose how long data stays hot before transitiong to warm storage.&amp;nbsp; With up to 100 TB of hot storage per collection, time series collections can handle high-volume observability workloads such as application monitoring, security analytics, and IoT telemetry. Alerting and anomaly detection are also now available, so customers can get notified about issues and spot unusual patterns in their log and event data without separate tooling.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Time series collections are available today in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/opensearch-service.html" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where OpenSearch Serverless is available. To learn more about the pricing for the new warm storage,&amp;nbsp; see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenSearch Serverless pricing&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about time series collections on Amazon OpenSearch Serverless, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/features/serverless/" target="_blank"&gt;OpenSearch Serverless feature page&lt;/a&gt;, the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-the-next-generation-of-serverless-time-series-for-amazon-opensearch-service/" target="_blank"&gt;launch blog&lt;/a&gt;, and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/serverless.html" target="_blank"&gt;AWS documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/pricing/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Quick now supports smart approvals, a review layer that reduces unnecessary approval prompts while preserving human oversight of the actions that matter most. As agents take more actions on a user's behalf, such as sending email, updating records, and creating tickets, asking a person to approve every step leads to approval fatigue and weaker oversight over time. With smart approvals, a separate review model called the classifier evaluates each eligible action against the user's request. It approves routine actions automatically and refers consequential actions to the user for review.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Your existing policies always come first. Smart approvals only reviews actions where the user's setting is "ask" each time. Denied actions stay denied, and smart approvals never grants new access. The classifier sends higher-impact actions to the user for review. These include sending content outside the organization, changing permissions, deleting content, making payments, and actions that go beyond what the user asked for. Each automatic approval covers only the exact action that was reviewed. If a review fails or times out, the action goes to the user.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Smart approvals is on for all users by default. Administrators control who can use it with custom permissions, and users can turn it off for their conversations and scheduled runs in chat options. AWS CloudTrail records each action review made through smart approvals and each change to a user's smart approvals setting.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Smart approvals is available in Amazon Quick in all AWS Regions where Amazon Quick agentic capabilities are supported. For more information, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about smart approvals, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/smart-approvals.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;smart approvals&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Quick User Guide. For more information about Amazon Quick, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/quick/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Quick product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) announces certificate authority (CA) rotation visibility in the Amazon EKS Dashboard, giving you a consolidated view of CA rotation across your clusters. The Amazon EKS Dashboard already provides centralized visibility into your clusters across AWS Regions and accounts, and it now includes CA rotation, so you can see where action is needed without checking each cluster individually.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Each Amazon EKS cluster has its own CA, and clusters created since 2018 have CAs with a 10-year validity period that are now approaching the point where rotation activities should begin. In the EKS Dashboard, you can now see your clusters and their CA rotation status, which clusters have CAs approaching expiration, and upcoming automated rotation events, so platform teams who operate many clusters can prioritize action across their fleet from one place.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The Amazon EKS Dashboard aggregates data across your accounts and Regions through AWS Organizations, so you enable trusted access for the dashboard in your organization’s management account to use this capability. You view the dashboard from the US East region (us-east-1), and it is available at no additional cost. CA rotation support extends to all commercial AWS Regions, and the dashboard brings that rotation status together in one fleet-wide view. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/certificate-authority-rotation.html"&gt;Amazon EKS documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ecs/managed-instances/"&gt;Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) Managed Instances&lt;/a&gt; introduces two infrastructure optimization updates: cross-instance and Spot consolidation. Cross-instance consolidation evaluates tasks running across multiple underutilized instances together, packing them onto fewer instances to raise utilization and lower costs. Spot consolidation extends this capability to Spot capacity, consolidating underutilized Spot Instances after first verifying replacement capacity is available.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Amazon ECS Managed Instances provisions, configures, and operates right-sized Amazon EC2 instances for your workloads, balancing availability and cost. Its infrastructure optimization continuously monitors your fleet, reclaiming idle capacity and consolidating underutilized instances with graceful start-before-stop task replacement. Until now, optimization evaluated each instance in isolation for replacement, which worked well when a capacity provider was configured with diverse instance types. However, when a capacity provider was configured with a narrow range of instance types, there were fewer opportunities to consolidate multiple underutilized instances, because no single replacement could yield savings. With cross-instance consolidation, ECS now evaluates and repacks tasks across multiple instances simultaneously. For example, in our testing with a capacity provider configured for c9g instances with a minimum size of 4xlarge, running 261 tasks (each 1 vCPU, 1.94 GiB), cross-instance consolidation reduced the fleet from 19 instances (seven 4xlarge, seven 8xlarge, four 12xlarge, and one 24xlarge) to 13 instances (eight 4xlarge and five 8xlarge), while raising CPU utilization from 42% to 91% and memory utilization from 41% to 88%. Spot consolidation applies the same approach to Spot capacity, first verifying replacement capacity is available. With these enhancements, ECS Managed Instances delivers deeper cost optimization for both your On-Demand and Spot workloads.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cross-instance and Spot consolidation are now available at no additional cost in all AWS Regions, and apply automatically to all existing and new ECS Managed Instances capacity providers. To learn more, see our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/developerguide/managed-instances-infrastructure-optimization.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Lambda announces the Durable Execution Telemetry Exporter plugin for Lambda durable functions. This new observability plugin captures the step-by-step history of your durable executions and sends them to one or more destinations of your choice. You can use the exported data to gather operational and business insights and keep history beyond the standard retention period. We are also releasing a companion Model Context Protocol (MCP) server that lets AI agents such as Kiro query your exported telemetry data using natural language.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Lambda durable functions helps you build resilient multi-step applications and AI workflows that run for up to one year. The new Telemetry Exporter sends data such as execution status, operation timing, step input and output, and errors to 13 built-in destinations. Destinations include Amazon CloudWatch Logs, Amazon S3 for Amazon Athena, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon Aurora, and Amazon OpenSearch Service. You can redact sensitive fields before export and set a sampling rate to control data volume. With the MCP server, an on-call engineer can ask an AI agent to list recent failed executions and find the cause of a failure, and a business analyst can find all transactions for a specific customer.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The exporter is available for TypeScript, Python, Java, and Go. To get started, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/durable-execution/sdk-reference/observability/telemetry/exporter/"&gt;install&lt;/a&gt; the exporter, register the plugin in your function handler, then &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/durable-execution/sdk-reference/observability/telemetry/mcp/"&gt;add&lt;/a&gt; the MCP server to your agent.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The Durable Execution Telemetry Exporter is available at no additional charge in all AWS Regions that support Lambda durable functions. To learn more, see the Durable Execution SDK &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/durable-execution/sdk-reference/observability/telemetry/"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/observing-aws-lambda-durable-functions-with-the-durable-execution-telemetry-exporter/"&gt;blog post&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon EKS Auto Mode now supports the Kubernetes Gateway API, giving customers a standard, portable way to define how traffic reaches their applications. Customers can configure application load balancing using Gateway and HTTPRoute resources, and EKS Auto Mode provisions and manages the underlying AWS infrastructure automatically.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, EKS Auto Mode customers can adopt the next-generation Kubernetes traffic management standard without installing or configuring a separate controller. Capabilities include HTTP and gRPC routing, cross-namespace traffic routing for multi-team environments, weighted traffic splitting, header-based routing, and automatic TLS certificate discovery through AWS Certificate Manager. Existing workloads using Ingress resources continue to work alongside Gateway API resources.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/auto-configure-gateway-api.html"&gt;Amazon EKS documentation&lt;/a&gt;. Gateway API support is included as part of EKS Auto Mode and is available in all AWS Regions where EKS Auto Mode is supported. Standard charges apply for the AWS load balancing resources provisioned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Backup restore testing now supports Amazon Redshift. Restore testing helps you automate and periodically run restore tests of your backups, so you can confirm that your Amazon Redshift backups restore successfully and measure how long the restores take.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can now include Amazon Redshift in your restore testing plans alongside other supported AWS resources across storage, compute, and database services. Restore testing plans automatically select recovery points, run restores on a schedule, and measure restore job completion time against your recovery time objectives (RTO). This helps you evaluate recovery readiness and meet regulatory and compliance requirements for disaster recovery and business continuity.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, create a restore testing plan and assign your Amazon Redshift resources by Amazon Resource Name (ARN) or tag. Choose whether each test restores the latest or a random eligible recovery point. Optionally, use restore testing validation to confirm the restored resource before AWS Backup deletes the test resources.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This support is available in all AWS Regions where both restore testing and Amazon Redshift are supported by AWS Backup. You can use the AWS Backup console, AWS Command Line Interface (CLI), or AWS SDKs. For a complete list of supported Regions and features, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/backup-feature-availability.html#features-by-region" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Backup documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about restore testing, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/restore-testing.html" target="_blank"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/backup/pricing/" target="_blank"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS Marketplace and Amazon Business announced the unified storefront. Organizations can now give their teams one curated catalog that puts software, data, and AI tools from AWS Marketplace alongside physical goods and supplies from Amazon Business. Many enterprises already buy from both, but they manage two catalogs with duplicated approval rules and separate reporting. The unified storefront can now give procurement teams one front door for both.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Built on AWS Marketplace Storefront, the unified storefront lets administrators connect their organization's Amazon Business account and import Amazon Business products into their branded storefront. Administrators set governance rules once, and they apply across both catalogs. Buyers can browse AWS Marketplace listings and Amazon Business products in one place. Purchases of AWS Marketplace listings complete directly in the storefront and appear on the customer's AWS invoice. When a buyer selects an Amazon Business product, single sign-on takes them to an authenticated Amazon Business cart, so they don't need a second login. Each storefront connects only to the accounts its administrator sets up.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Amazon Business connector is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Marketplace operates to AWS Marketplace Storefront customers who have access to both the AWS Marketplace and Amazon Business APIs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/partners/storefront"&gt;AWS Marketplace Storefront product page&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/marketplace/latest/storefrontguide/what-is-marketplace-storefront.html"&gt;AWS Marketplace Storefront User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS IAM Identity Center's Identity Store now delivers real-time events to Amazon EventBridge when users, groups, or group memberships or the identity store itself is created, updated, or deleted. AWS IAM Identity Center Identity Store is a central repository that allows you to manage identities and access across AWS accounts and applications, serving as the identity source for AWS IAM Identity Center. This new capability helps you automatically respond to identity lifecycle events and support your security and compliance processes by detecting changes as they happen.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With Identity Store change notifications, you can build automated workflows that respond to identity changes in your organization. For example, security teams can create EventBridge rules that trigger AWS Lambda functions to audit user provisioning activity, send alerts to security information and event management (SIEM) systems, or update access in downstream applications when users or groups change. Organizations that manage large workforces can also use this data to automate onboarding and offboarding when users are added to or removed from specific groups.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Change notifications are available in all AWS Regions where IAM Identity Center is available, and events are sent in each enabled Region of your instance. For the list of Regions, see &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities/explore?tab=service-feature&amp;amp;f=eJwdi0EKhDAUQ68yZK2L2fYAc4DZ-ouIZqQgtrRfQaR3909XeeElNwo3zsrlyzXEvcBhEByl51S0fws8OqQcE7Oen7Ap8_jjpEemTW9BEjhBzIJOoFYGb7D-wWxs1jV5NggLdw16vWZL2q36ivoAQn8tDw"&gt;AWS capabilities by Regions&lt;/a&gt;. Change notifications are available at no additional cost beyond standard Amazon EventBridge usage charges. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/event-notifications.html"&gt;Event notifications&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS IAM Identity Center User Guide and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/ref/events-ref-identitystore.html"&gt;Identity Store events&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon EventBridge Events Reference.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Agent Registry, available through Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, is now available in four additional AWS Regions: Europe (London) eu-west-2, Europe (Frankfurt) eu-central-1, US East (Ohio) us-east-2, and Canada (Central) ca-central-1.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AWS Agent Registry is a fully managed discovery service that provides a centralized catalog for organizing, curating, and discovering resources across your organization. With AWS Agent Registry, you can publish MCP servers, tools, agents, agent skills, and custom resources into a searchable registry, control access through an approval workflow, and enable both human users and AI agents to discover the right tools and agents through hybrid search, catalog browsing, and a native MCP endpoint.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AWS Agent Registry is now available in nine AWS Regions. For a complete list of Region availability, see the AWS Regional Services List.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see the AWS Agent Registry documentation: &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/registry.html"&gt;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/registry.html&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ecs/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon Elastic Container Service&lt;/a&gt; (Amazon ECS) now supports GPU sharing with NVIDIA Multi-Process Service (MPS), enabling you to run multiple Amazon ECS tasks concurrently on a single NVIDIA GPU. GPU sharing helps you increase GPU utilization and reduce infrastructure costs for workloads that use only part of a GPU's capacity, such as small-model inference, AV/ADAS simulations, embedding generation, and multi-model serving.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With GPU sharing, you can pack more workloads onto each GPU instance without changing your application code. You define the GPU memory each container needs and can optionally set a maximum share of GPU compute, and Amazon ECS places tasks on GPUs with available capacity as your services scale and deploy. GPU sharing is supported on Amazon ECS Managed Instances and Amazon ECS on Amazon EC2. On Amazon ECS Managed Instances, Amazon ECS also monitors the MPS control daemon that enables sharing on each instance. If the daemon stops or becomes unhealthy, Amazon ECS automatically replaces the instance and moves your tasks to healthy capacity, so your GPU workloads keep running without manual intervention.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, use the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, AWS SDKs, AWS CloudFormation, or other infrastructure-as-code tools to create a capacity provider with NVIDIA GPU instances, set the GPU sharing strategy and memory in the container definition of your task definition, and create a service on that capacity provider. This capability is available in all AWS commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/" target="_blank"&gt;Regions&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, visit the Amazon ECS GPU sharing &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/developerguide/ecs-gpu-sharing.html" target="_blank"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker AI MLflow Apps now support MLflow 3.16, bringing a redesigned tracing experience and faster experiment tracking to your generative AI and machine learning workflows.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;MLflow 3.16 improves debugging and analysis of generative AI applications and agents. The redesigned trace explorer is now the default, with adjustable row height, reorderable columns, custom columns from any trace tag or metadata field. This release also accelerates metric charts for experiments with large metric histories.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;New MLflow Apps are created on MLflow 3.16 by default. Existing MLflow Apps will be upgraded to MLflow 3.16 during an upcoming maintenance window, with no action required.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;MLflow 3.16 is available in all AWS Regions where MLflow Apps are available. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/mlflow.html"&gt;SageMaker AI MLflow developer guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Connect Customer lets agents preview attachments directly in the agent workspace, reducing contact handling time and limiting the need to download sensitive customer information. Agents can preview PDFs, images, and text files across email, chat, and cases, navigate between attachments, and view multi-page documents without leaving the conversation.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Connect Customer attachment previews is available in the US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), Africa (Cape Town), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Canada (Central), Europe (Frankfurt), and Europe (London) &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;regions&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more and get started, please refer to the help &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/preview-attachments.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; or visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/connect/"&gt;Amazon Connect Customer&lt;/a&gt; website.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/serverless/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon Aurora serverless&lt;/a&gt; now supports database capacity of up to 384 Aurora Capacity Units (ACUs) for Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible Edition, a 50% increase from the previous maximum of 256 ACUs. Each ACU provides approximately 2 gibibytes (GiB) of memory with corresponding CPU and networking, so a single Aurora serverless database can now scale up to 768 GiB of memory. When the workload completes, Aurora serverless automatically scales down to zero. This makes Aurora serverless especially well-suited for agentic AI applications, which typically have bursts of activity, long idle windows, and unpredictable traffic patterns. Aurora serverless handles all of it automatically, scaling capacity with your agents, so you only pay for what you use.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With 384 ACU support, customers can now use Aurora serverless for even more demanding workloads while continuing to scale down to zero when not in use, paying only for the capacity consumed. For new clusters, select a supported Aurora PostgreSQL engine version and set your maximum capacity up to 384 ACUs. For existing clusters on Platform Version 4, simply modify the cluster to update the maximum capacity setting (and the engine version if needed). There will be no downtime if your database is already on Platform Version 4. For existing clusters on older platform versions, upgrade to Platform Version 4 through a pending maintenance action. 384 ACU support is available for Aurora PostgreSQL versions 18.4, 17.10, 16.14, 15.18, and 14.23, running on Platform Version 4.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/aurora-serverless-v2.how-it-works.html#aurora-serverless-v2.how-it-works.capacity" target="_blank"&gt;Aurora serverless capacity&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Aurora User Guide. For pricing details and Region availability, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/pricing/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon Aurora Pricing&lt;/a&gt;. &amp;nbsp;To get started, create or modify an Aurora serverless database in the &lt;a href="https://signin.aws.amazon.com/signin?redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fconsole.aws.amazon.com%2Fconsole%2Fhome%3FhashArgs%3D%2523%26isauthcode%3Dtrue%26state%3DhashArgsFromTB_us-east-2_a53ff6382623e9a3&amp;amp;client_id=arn%3Aaws%3Asignin%3A%3A%3Aconsole%2Fcanvas&amp;amp;forceMobileApp=0&amp;amp;code_challenge=SCavOinSDkGpz8021yaDgWGy_E5aIbgB7HoRsIR47N0&amp;amp;code_challenge_method=SHA-256" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Management Console&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey data tiering is now available on Graviton4-based R8gd nodes in the AWS Europe (Spain) Region, and on Graviton3-based R7gd nodes in the Europe (Spain) and Asia Pacific (Hyderabad) Regions. Data tiering is a lower-cost way to scale your Valkey clusters to hundreds of terabytes of capacity. It uses solid state drives (SSDs) on each node alongside memory. When memory fills up, ElastiCache automatically moves the least recently used items to SSD. When your application reads an item stored on SSD, ElastiCache moves it back into memory before serving the request. Data tiering is a good fit for workloads that regularly access up to 20% of their dataset, and for applications that can tolerate some extra latency when they read data from SSD.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;R8gd nodes are powered by AWS Graviton4 processors. They deliver up to 47% higher throughput, up to 43% lower P99 latency, and up to 31% better price-performance than Graviton3-based nodes of the same size. R7gd nodes are powered by AWS Graviton3 processors. They deliver up to 28% more throughput (read and write operations per second), up to 21% better P99 latency, and up to 25% more network bandwidth than Graviton2-based nodes. Actual performance varies by node size and workload.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can use data tiering on R8gd and R7gd nodes with Valkey 7.2 and later. To get started, create a new cluster or upgrade an existing one with the AWS Management Console, the AWS CLI, or the AWS SDKs. For pricing, see the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/pricing/"&gt;Amazon ElastiCache pricing page&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, see &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/data-tiering.html"&gt;Data tiering&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; and &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/CacheNodes.SupportedTypes.html"&gt;Supported node types&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; in the ElastiCache documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey now lets you enable or disable durability on your existing node-based clusters. Previously, you couldn't change the durability configuration of an existing cluster in place. This made it harder to move from caching to persistent data store use cases, or to adapt as your application requirements change. Now you can change durability at any point in your application's lifecycle.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can choose between two durability options: synchronous and asynchronous writes. Synchronous writes persist data across at least two AZs before responding to the client, designed for zero data loss at single-digit millisecond write latency. Asynchronous writes persist data after responding to the client, maintaining microsecond write latency at no additional cost. However, up to 10 seconds of uncommitted data could be lost in the rare event of a failure. Both options maintain microsecond read latency. You can now use ElastiCache for a broader set of use cases beyond caching where data loss is unacceptable such as AI agent long-term memory, AI agent workflow state, knowledge bases for RAG applications, payment tokenization, and real-time inventory management.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where ElastiCache for Valkey 9.0 and later is available. To get started, use the AWS Management Console, AWS SDK, or AWS CLI. For pricing, see the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/pricing/"&gt;Amazon ElastiCache pricing page&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, see &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/durability.html"&gt;Durability&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;in the ElastiCache documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On October 26, 2026 Amazon announced a patch update for the following Amazon Corretto Long-Term Support (LTS) and Feature Release (FR) versions of OpenJDK. Corretto 27.0.1.0.11, 25.0.5.0.10, 21.0.13.0.11, 17.0.21.0.11, 11.0.33.0.10, and 8u512 are now available for &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/corretto/"&gt;download&lt;/a&gt;. Amazon Corretto is a no-cost, multi-platform, production-ready distribution of OpenJDK.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Visit the Corretto &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/corretto"&gt;home page&lt;/a&gt; to download Corretto 27, Corretto 25, Corretto 21, Corretto 17, Corretto 11, or Corretto 8. You can also get the updates on your Linux system by configuring a &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/corretto/latest/corretto-25-ug/generic-linux-install.html"&gt;Corretto Apt, Yum, or Apk repo&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Feedback is &lt;a href="https://github.com/corretto"&gt;welcomed&lt;/a&gt;!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/neptune/"&gt;Amazon Neptune&lt;/a&gt; Database now supports online index rebuild, a new capability that helps database administrators and application teams defragment Neptune indexes and reclaim unused storage space without taking their database offline. Over time, insert, update, and delete operations create gaps of unused space within data pages, keeping the &lt;code&gt;VolumeBytesUsed&lt;/code&gt; metric artificially elevated even after data is deleted. This fragmentation increases I/O costs and degrades query performance for teams running Gremlin, openCypher, or SPARQL workloads at scale.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With online index rebuild, read and write queries continue uninterrupted on both the writer instance and read replicas throughout the entire rebuild process. You can target all indexes, only quad indexes, or only dictionary indexes, making this feature well-suited for post-deletion cleanup, periodic maintenance, storage limit management, and performance tuning on heavily fragmented databases. Tunable concurrency and write buffer size settings let you balance rebuild speed against workload impact, while built-in storage safety mechanisms protect against storage exhaustion during the operation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Online index rebuild is available starting with Amazon Neptune engine release 1.5.0.0 on provisioned writer instances, in all regions where Amazon Neptune Database is supported. To learn more about configuring and running online index rebuild, including API references and tuning guidance, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/neptune/latest/userguide/storage-index-rebuild.html"&gt;Amazon Neptune documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock now offers Canada cross-Region inference (CRIS) for Anthropic's Claude Haiku 5.5. Customers with strict data residency requirements can now keep inference processing entirely within Canada. Canada CRIS routes requests only between ca-central-1 (Canada Central) and ca-west-1 (Canada West, Calgary). This helps organizations in financial services, healthcare, the public sector, and other regulated industries meet federal and provincial data residency requirements.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Canada CRIS adds to the inference options already available to customers in Canada. US CRIS and global CRIS remain fully supported for Claude Haiku 5.5. Both route requests across a broader set of AWS Regions, and global CRIS also offers cost savings, which makes these options a good fit for workloads that don't require inference processing to stay in Canada.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Canada CRIS is designated as an inference profile with limited model selection. Future models and model versions might not be released for this profile. We recommend Canada CRIS for workloads that must keep inference processing within Canada. For more details, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/inference-profiles-limited-model-selection.html"&gt;Inference profiles with limited model selection&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Bedrock User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For the most current information about model availability in each Region, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/models-region-compatibility.html"&gt;Regional availability&lt;/a&gt; by models in the Amazon Bedrock User Guide. To get started with Claude models on Amazon Bedrock, visit the Amazon Bedrock console.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) now supports replication backfill, enabling you to replicate existing images in your private registry to your configured replication registry destinations. Previously, replication only applied to images pushed after the replication configuration was enabled, requiring customers to manually retag images to trigger replication of preexisting images. With replication backfill, you can now replicate your full image catalog to new or existing destinations with a single API call.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Replication backfill is useful when expanding to a new AWS Region, validating disaster recovery readiness, updating replication rules, or ensuring complete image catalogs exist across Regions for compliance purposes. You can run up to ten backfill jobs concurrently, monitor progress and per-image results through new APIs, and stop a job at any time without deleting images that have already been replicated.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Replication backfill is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon ECR is available. To learn more, refer to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/replication-backfill.html"&gt;Amazon ECR user guide&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces the general availability of OpenAI GPT-6.1 Terra on Amazon Bedrock. GPT-6.1 Terra expands the GPT-6 family with a balance of capability, speed, and cost efficiency for everyday workloads.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;As the newest model in the Terra line, GPT-6.1 Terra combines detailed visual reasoning, accurate computer use, and improved factuality and natural writing over GPT-5.6 Terra, for complex workflows. In OpenAI’s Agents’ Last Exam evaluation, it scored 15 percentage points higher than GPT-5.6 Terra, performed on par with GPT-6 Astra, and completed tasks more than three times faster than GPT-6 Astra and GPT-6.1 Sol. GPT-6.1 Terra offers balanced performance with input and output token prices at least 50% lower than those of GPT-6.1 Sol and GPT-5.6 Terra. It supports a context window of up to 1 million input tokens and accepts both text and image inputs. The Amazon Bedrock inference engine delivers the performance, security, and scale required for enterprise workloads.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can call GPT-6.1 Terra directly through Amazon Bedrock APIs or configure ChatGPT Work and Codex to use the model on Amazon Bedrock. Use it to draft proposals, interpret technical drawings, investigate codebases, and build agents that operate across business systems.&amp;nbsp; Established AWS controls help you secure workloads, govern access, and audit model invocation activity.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can get started in the Amazon Bedrock console or programmatically through supported Amazon Bedrock APIs. For information about supported AWS Regions, endpoints, APIs, features, inference profiles and pricing, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-cards-openai.html"&gt;Amazon Bedrock documentation.&lt;/a&gt; &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon WorkSpaces Core Managed Instances now supports the ModifyVolume API, which lets you change volume size, volume type, IOPS, or throughput in place, without detaching the volume or restarting the instance. Previously, changing storage capacity or performance on a deployed managed instance required rebuilding it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With ModifyVolume, you can increase volume size up to 16,384 GiB, switch between supported Amazon EBS volume types, adjust IOPS for gp3, io1, and io2 volumes, and modify throughput for gp3 volumes. The API supports idempotency tokens to prevent duplicate changes, and you can track progress with the Amazon EC2 DescribeVolumesModifications operation.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;ModifyVolume is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon WorkSpaces Core Managed Instances is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, call ModifyVolume directly, or use it through your WorkSpaces Core partner solution once available. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/workspaces-instances/latest/api/API_ModifyVolume.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ModifyVolume&lt;/a&gt; in the WorkSpaces Instances API Reference and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/ebs-modify-volume.html#elastic-volumes-limitations" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Limitations&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon EBS User Guide, or visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/workspaces/vdi-partners/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WorkSpaces Core&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Interactive Video Service (Amazon IVS) Low-Latency Streaming now provides a failover slate, an animated image that IVS displays to viewers when a broadcaster's stream is unexpectedly interrupted. Instead of a buffering wheel or frozen frame, viewers see the slate until the broadcaster reconnects. Live playback then resumes automatically, without viewers needing to refresh their player.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The failover slate helps you retain your audience during momentary disruptions and is ideal for broadcasters streaming from mobile devices or from locations with unreliable connectivity. You can enable the failover slate on new or existing channels and monitor slate activity with Amazon EventBridge events and Amazon CloudWatch metrics.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ivs/"&gt;Amazon IVS&lt;/a&gt; is a managed live streaming solution designed to make low-latency or real-time video available to viewers around the world. Visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt; for a full list of AWS Regions where the Amazon IVS console and APIs for control and creation of video streams are available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ivs/latest/LowLatencyUserGuide/failover-slate.html"&gt;Amazon IVS Failover Slate documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces light mode support in Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio for Identity Center (IDC) based domains. Customers can now configure the visual interface mode to match their preference, choosing between dark and light themes. With this launch, customers can set this visual preference in both IAM-based and IDC-based domains.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Light mode helps improve readability in bright environments and provides a familiar visual experience for customers who prefer lighter interfaces. Combined with the existing dark mode, this update gives you full control over the appearance of your development environment, improving accessibility and reducing eye strain across varying lighting conditions. In SageMaker Unified Studio settings, you can choose 'Customize appearance' under your Profile settings to select between visual modes including dark mode, light mode, and device setting, which matches your operating system's setting. The setting persists across browsers and devices.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio is available. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/navigating-sagemaker-unified-studio.html#display-mode"&gt;Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio User Guide.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Trusted identity propagation carries a user's identity with their request, so AWS services can grant permissions based on who the user is and their underlying group associations, and log that user's access for auditing. With cross-Region support, this identity context can now be propagated to AWS services running in different AWS Regions, giving you greater flexibility in how you architect and deploy across AWS Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers often distribute workloads across AWS Regions for many reasons including separating the compute from the data it queries, to running workloads where capacity is available, or for disaster recovery and global scale. Previously, trusted identity propagation required all of the AWS services accessing a user's identity to reside in the same AWS Region. To reach services in another AWS Region, customers had to either grant access through broad IAM roles that cannot distinguish individual users or consolidate all their services into a single AWS Region. With cross-Region trusted identity propagation, a user's identity travels with the request across AWS Regions automatically, so you can grant access based on who the user is and their underlying group associations, and audit who accessed which resources, while preserving fine-grained access control for every individual user.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Cross-Region trusted identity propagation is available at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where IAM Identity Center is available. For the list of Regions where IAM Identity Center is available, see &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities/explore?tab=service-feature&amp;amp;f=eJxtks1OwzAQhF8F7bk-kFKQKnFF4sCFHusIWck2jTCx5Z8CqvLuGKeOHTu3nW_Wu_LYV9DIsTHYvmPXi0HDHo4UrCbItCH3FDZRVUF9Y-p5NXnsRLSw5nwzmUzHBFUFNQhlzsuGiFa6tgFlOyZZLeRybEQrXdsSPRRoV6LHEj15hFY3pMUsw05cVkiSZcNIg4NRjEeQ2GgzOwFVAD6t6KdJzXJuXg5P3jGoKZqeZ4u_fnKAJUhXazbfvIYNSCUkKvP70nOD6uOEzFiF7utdKUgKewpC-XPGiWPtiu6_cO6nd28HXlvfJDx79vXF12-Wm55MP_ruYKV0oVAY6xHGP20D8yw"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region.&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp; For more information, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/trustedidentitypropagation-overview.html"&gt;Trusted identity propagation&lt;/a&gt; in the IAM Identity Center User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Key Management Service (KMS) now supports Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism (ML-KEM), a post-quantum cryptography standard under &lt;a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final"&gt;NIST FIPS 203&lt;/a&gt;, which&amp;nbsp;enables two parties to establish a shared secret.&amp;nbsp;NIST designed ML-KEM to replace the classical key-establishment schemes, like DH/ECDH key agreement, and RSA-based key transport, in their strategic cryptographic roadmap&amp;nbsp;(see &lt;a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8547/ipd"&gt;NIST IR 8547&lt;/a&gt;).&amp;nbsp;ML-KEM addresses the "harvest now, decrypt later" threat to those classical schemes, where an adversary records the key-establishment exchange today and recovers the shared secret once sufficiently powerful quantum computers become available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;KMS supports all three ML-KEM key specs defined in NIST FIPS 203: ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768, and ML-KEM-1024. The Encapsulate API uses the public key to produce a random 32-byte shared secret and its ciphertext form. The Decapsulate API recovers the shared secret from ciphertext using the corresponding private key. KMS protects the private key of an ML-KEM key pair in FIPS 140-3 Level 3 validated hardware security modules (HSMs). You can use the GetPublicKey API to export the public key and distribute it to partners, client applications, or external systems for performing the encapsulate operation locally using cryptographic libraries like AWS-LC, OpenSSL, or Bouncy Castle.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;ML-KEM is available in all AWS Regions where KMS is available, except the the Middle East (UAE), Middle East (Bahrain), and China Regions. For more information, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/pqkeys.html#mlkem"&gt;ML-KEM keys in AWS KMS&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/symm-asymm-choose-key-spec.html#key-spec-mlkem"&gt;Key spec reference&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;in the AWS KMS Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS EC2 Auto Scaling now lets you enable Launch Before Terminate as the default replacement behavior across all Auto Scaling groups in your account. When enabled, Auto Scaling launches a replacement instance and waits for it to pass health checks before terminating the instance it replaces, helping your applications maintain full capacity and availability during instance replacement events. This applies to replacement events such as instance refreshes, rebalancing, health checks, and maximum-instance-lifetime replacements.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can enable Launch Before Terminate through AWS EC2 Auto Scaling account settings, which let you configure the behavior once at the account level instead of on each Auto Scaling group individually. You can manage the setting with the new `PutAccountSetting` and `DescribeAccountSettings` APIs, available through the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, or AWS SDKs.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Launch Before Terminate is available in all AWS Regions at no additional cost.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see Launch Before Terminate in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/ec2/userguide/ec2-auto-scaling-account-settings.html"&gt;AWS EC2 Auto Scaling User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/ec2/APIReference/API_Operations.html"&gt;AWS EC2 Auto Scaling API Reference Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/mediaconnect/features/router/"&gt;AWS Elemental MediaConnect Router&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;now supports managing connections through a new endpoint registry, which allows you to register devices or software external to MediaConnect and connect those endpoints to your router inputs and outputs. You can share registered endpoints directly with other AWS accounts, eliminating the need to manually chain router inputs and outputs or coordinate IP addresses and encryption settings out of band.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Cross-account endpoint sharing lowers costs by reducing required compute instances and decreases latency through fewer network hops. Sharing is managed through AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM), with Connection Manager providing visibility and control over each shared connection. Common use cases include content distributors sharing live feeds with downstream partners, broadcasters collaborating across organizational boundaries, and media workflows that require secure cross-account video transport.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;MediaConnect endpoint registry is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Elemental MediaConnect Router is available, at no additional cost. For more information, see the AWS Elemental MediaConnect &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mediaconnect/latest/ug/working-with-endpoints.html"&gt;User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Glue Data Catalog now supports OAuth 2.0 bearer token authentication for Apache Iceberg REST Catalog (IRC) APIs using AWS Sign-In. The Apache Iceberg REST Catalog specification defines OAuth 2.0 as its standard authentication mechanism, and query engines such as Apache Spark, Trino, and Apache Flink implement it natively. These engines and external integration partners can now authenticate to the Glue Data Catalog Iceberg endpoint using standard OAuth 2.0 bearer tokens, in addition to AWS Signature Version 4 (SigV4).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can now grant partners federated access to your Iceberg tables without distributing AWS credentials. Using OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange (RFC 8693), you exchange an OpenID Connect (OIDC) identity token for a short-lived, Glue-scoped bearer token and include it in IRC requests following the same standards-based pattern used across the Iceberg ecosystem. Access remains governed by AWS IAM and AWS Lake Formation permissions. Common use cases include enabling third-party analytics and data cloud platforms to query tables in the Glue Data Catalog through a single, consistent authentication path.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Glue and Lake Formation are available. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connect-glu-iceberg-rest.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Glue User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/signin/latest/userguide/configure-oauth-glue-iceberg-rest.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Sign-In User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Web Search on&amp;nbsp;Amazon Bedrock now supports two new controls that give you more precision over the results that ground a model's response: domain filtering and user location. Both are configured directly on the web_search tool in your existing OpenAI Responses API request.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With domain filtering, you can restrict Web Search results to a set of domains you choose. Using the filters parameter, you specify up to 100 allowed_domains to limit results to trusted sources, or up to 100 blocked_domains to exclude sources you don't want. This is useful when you want responses grounded only in specific sites, or when you need to keep certain domains out of results.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With user location, you can optionally specify location parameters so that Web Search can return results relevant to that geography. This helps with location-dependent questions, such as regional availability or local services.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Domain filtering and user location are available in all AWS Regions where Web Search is supported: US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (Oregon), and AWS GovCloud (US-West). To get started, see the Web Search section in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/web-search.html"&gt;Amazon Bedrock User Guide&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/pricing/"&gt;Amazon Bedrock pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Connect Customer Global Resiliency now provides customers a mechanism to deliver long, persistent chat experiences across linked AWS Regions. With this capability, end users can resume previous conversations with full context, metadata, and transcripts carried over across Regions, eliminating the need for end users to repeat information and allowing agents to provide personalized service with access to the entire conversation history across AWS Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For example, an end user can start a chat in US-East (N. Virginia) and later come back to it in the linked US-West (Oregon) Region, continuing the same conversation with an agent with full conversation history&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;chat attachment. To adopt this capability, onboard to &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/global-routing-across-acgr-regions.html"&gt;Global routing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions where &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/get-started-connect-global-resiliency.html"&gt;Amazon Connect Customer Global Resiliency&lt;/a&gt; is supported. To learn more, visit &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/setup-connect-global-resiliency.html"&gt;Amazon Connect Customer Admin Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transit Gateway and AWS Cloud WAN now provide managed one-to-one network address translation (NAT) directly on their attachments, through integration with Regional Private NAT Gateway. This enables customers to connect private networks that share the same IP address ranges without renumbering either network, deploying a NAT appliance, or writing return-traffic rules.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Networks with overlapping Classless Inter-Domain Routing (CIDR) blocks have long been unable to communicate, a common blocker during mergers and acquisitions, when connecting partner networks over AWS Site-to-Site VPN, in multicloud connectivity, and when independently provisioned VPCs need to interconnect. Using this capability, customers can define address-translation rules on a AWS Transit Gateway or AWS Cloud WAN attachment, specifying the private IP range each source CIDR translates to, across Amazon VPC, AWS Site-to-Site VPN, AWS Direct Connect, and SD-WAN appliances connected through Transit Gateway Connect. Both services apply translation automatically in both directions, with no separate return-traffic rule and no NAT appliance to deploy. AWS operates, scales, and manages the NAT capability as part of the attachment. For example, if a customer acquires a business and both VPCs use 10.0.0.0/16, they translate one VPC's addresses to a non-overlapping range on its attachment and connect the networks immediately. Customers set it up in minutes using the AWS Management Console, AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI), AWS SDKs, and AWS CloudFormation, and monitor translated traffic through Amazon CloudWatch metrics and Amazon EventBridge events.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Transit Gateway and AWS Cloud WAN offer 1:1 NAT through Regional Private NAT Gateway in all AWS commercial&amp;nbsp;regions where they operate. For full pricing details, see &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/vpc/pricing/"&gt;Amazon VPC pricing&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, see the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/transit-gateway/"&gt;AWS Transit Gateway&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; and &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloud-wan/"&gt;AWS Cloud WAN&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; product pages, and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/tgw/"&gt;AWS Transit Gateway&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-manager/latest/cloudwan/what-is-cloudwan.html"&gt;AWS Cloud WAN&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On October 22, 2026 Amazon announced quarterly security and critical updates for Amazon Corretto Long-Term Supported (LTS) versions of OpenJDK. Corretto 27.0.1.0.10, 25.0.5.0.9, 21.0.13.0.10, 17.0.21.0.10, 11.0.33.0.9, and 8u512 are now available for &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/corretto/"&gt;download&lt;/a&gt;. Amazon Corretto is a no-cost, multi-platform, production-ready distribution of OpenJDK.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Visit the Corretto &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/corretto"&gt;home page&lt;/a&gt; to download Corretto 27, Corretto 25, Corretto 21, Corretto 17, Corretto 11, or Corretto 8. You can also get the updates on your Linux system by configuring a &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/corretto/latest/corretto-25-ug/generic-linux-install.html"&gt;Corretto Apt, Yum, or Apk repo&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Feedback is &lt;a href="https://github.com/corretto"&gt;welcomed&lt;/a&gt;!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces the general availability of AWS Continuum for code vulnerabilities. Continuum for code vulnerabilities&amp;nbsp;discovers, validates, prioritizes, and remediates code vulnerabilities at machine speed.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Continuum for code vulnerabilities builds a context graph from your AWS environment, including resource topology, identity relationships, and network reachability. It knows which resources are part of your application, and which findings carry real business risk. It ingests your existing vulnerability findings, scans your code, and then confirms which findings are exploitable by testing them in an isolated sandbox. The findings are prioritized by business impact. And finally, it provides remediation patches as pull requests directly to developer workflows with exploitability proof and blast radius assessment attached.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Continuum for code vulnerabilities picks the right AI model for each task. Continuum starts in Learn mode where it proposes the remediation fixes that you approve. When it builds sufficient trust in its precision, you switch to the Enforce mode and let it act autonomous within the guardrails you set.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Continuum works alongside your existing AWS security services, including Amazon GuardDuty and AWS Security Hub. For more information about the AWS Regions where AWS Continuum is available, see the &lt;a title="AWS Regional Table" href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Region table.&lt;/a&gt; To learn more, see the AWS Continuum &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/continuum/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon OpenSearch Serverless now offers intelligent ingestion, a fully managed feature that turns logs and documents stored in Amazon S3 into searchable content in Amazon OpenSearch Serverless (NextGen) collections . With intelligent ingestion, you can set up ingestion with a few clicks in the AWS Management Console or a single API call, with no ingestion infrastructure to manage. Intelligent ingestion supports observability logs and structured data in JSON, NDJSON, and JSON array formats, and unstructured files in PDF, Word, plain text, and Markdown formats.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You choose an S3 source and a collection index, and intelligent ingestion handles the rest. It automatically detects data types, parses documents, describes images, chunks text, generates vector embeddings, and indexes the results. You don't need to manage ingestion or document-processing infrastructure, or size capacity in advance. Use ingestion jobs for one-time loads such as migrations and backfills, or ingestion streams to keep an index in sync as new objects arrive in your bucket. Capacity scales with your workload up to the collection's configured limit, and you pay only for active processing. Source S3 buckets can be in the same AWS account as the collection or in a different account.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For logs and structured data, intelligent ingestion applies transformations and optionally adds embeddings, with no pipeline code. For unstructured data, it parses PDF, Word, plain text, and Markdown files while keeping headings, reading order, and tables intact, so the content lands in an index ready for semantic and hybrid search. When documents contain charts, diagrams, screenshots, or photos, a vision-language model generates text descriptions that are indexed with the surrounding content, making visual information searchable too.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Intelligent ingestion is available today for Amazon OpenSearch Serverless (NextGen) collections, with Amazon S3 as the source, in seven AWS Regions. To get started, open the Amazon OpenSearch Service console, choose your collection, and select the Ingestion tab. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/serverless-intelligent-ingestion.html"&gt;Intelligent ingestion&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon OpenSearch Service Developer Guide. For pricing, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/pricing/"&gt;Amazon OpenSearch Service pricing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, Amazon OpenSearch Serverless reduces the default refresh interval to 5 seconds for both search and vector collections. The refresh interval controls how quickly newly ingested documents become available for search. By lowering the default from 10 seconds to 5 seconds, newly written data becomes searchable faster, giving applications quicker read-after-write visibility automatically, with no configuration changes and no action required on your part.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This improvement applies to the next generation of Amazon OpenSearch Serverless and takes effect by default on both search and vector collections. Faster refresh is valuable for workloads that continuously ingest data and need near real-time retrieval, including full-text search over frequently updated content as well as vector use cases such as retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), semantic search, and recommendation and personalization systems. This means applications and AI agents can retrieve and act on the most recently ingested data sooner, improving the relevance and accuracy of search results and RAG responses for datasets that change frequently.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The 5-second default refresh interval is available in all&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/opensearch-service.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;24 regions&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;where Amazon OpenSearch Serverless is available today. To learn more, see the Amazon OpenSearch Serverless &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/serverless.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cloudformation/"&gt;AWS CloudFormation&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;now makes it simpler to complete an update rollback and get back to deploying. An update rollback returns your stack to a known good state. Starting today, if some resources cannot roll back automatically, force rollback enables you to complete the rollback in a single operation. It works across every CloudFormation workflow, from the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/cloudformation/"&gt;AWS CLI&lt;/a&gt;, and SDKs to &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cdk/v2/guide/"&gt;AWS CDK&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;and AI agents.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With force rollback, CloudFormation completes the update rollback and returns your stack to a deployable state in a single operation, instead of requiring you to identify, list, and retry the operation for each affected resource. Any resource that still needs attention, such as one affected by a change outside CloudFormation, is recorded for you to review and resolve it before your next deployment.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, run continue update rollback with the force rollback option, then create a &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/drift-aware-change-sets.html"&gt;drift-aware change set&lt;/a&gt; to review any configuration differences before your next deployment. To learn more, see &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/using-cfn-updating-stacks-continueupdaterollback.html"&gt;Continue rolling back an update&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS CloudFormation User Guide.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Force rollback is available in all Regions at no additional cost. Refer to the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/cfn.html#cfn_region"&gt;AWS Region table&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; for service availability details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announced the expansion of 100 Gbps dedicated connections at the existing &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect"&gt;AWS Direct Connect&lt;/a&gt; location in the Netmagic DC2 data center near Bangalore, India. You can now establish private, direct network access to all public AWS Regions (except those in China), AWS GovCloud Regions, and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/localzones/"&gt;AWS Local Zones&lt;/a&gt; from this location.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The Direct Connect service enables you to establish a private, physical network connection between AWS and your data center, office, or colocation environment. These private connections can provide a more consistent network experience than those made over the public internet.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For more information on the over 150 Direct Connect locations worldwide, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect/locations/"&gt;locations&lt;/a&gt; section of the Direct Connect &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect"&gt;product detail pages&lt;/a&gt;. Or, visit our &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect/getting-started/"&gt;getting started&lt;/a&gt; page to learn more about how to purchase and deploy Direct Connect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/msk/features/msk-connect/"&gt;Amazon MSK Connect&lt;/a&gt; (Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka Connect) now supports pausing and resuming connectors, letting you temporarily pause a connector from processing records and resume it later. This is useful when you need to stop data flow during maintenance on a source database or a sink target, or to pause processing while you investigate an upstream or downstream issue.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon MSK Connect is a fully managed service that allows you to deploy, monitor, and scale Apache Kafka Connect connectors with &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/msk/"&gt;Amazon MSK&lt;/a&gt; (Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka). Previously, pausing a connector meant deleting it and recreating it - a more disruptive operation, adding risk and operational overhead. With this launch, you can pause a running connector and resume it in place when you are ready — a paused connector keeps its configuration, capacity settings, and committed offsets, and its tasks continue processing from the offsets they last committed when you resume.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available at no additional cost through the Amazon MSK Console, AWS CLI, AWS SDK, and AWS CDK in all AWS Regions where Amazon MSK Connect is available. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/mskconnect.html"&gt;Amazon MSK Connect documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/msk"&gt;Amazon MSK Console&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS launches AWS Interconnect - last mile preview with Megaport, a fully managed connectivity offering that allows customers to connect their branch offices, data centers, and remote locations to AWS with just a few clicks, eliminating the friction and complexity of network setup. As a milestone collaboration between AWS and Megaport, AWS Interconnect - last mile combines AWS cloud innovation with Megaport operating one of the world's largest Software Defined Networks (SDN), with 1,200+ enabled data centers across 31 countries.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Through the AWS Management Console, customers can now instantly establish private, high-speed connections to AWS by simply choosing their preferred AWS Region, bandwidth speed, Direct Connect Gateway ID and partner subscriber ID. Once initiated, AWS generates an activation key to complete connectivity provisioning with Megaport. The launch simplifies the connectivity experience by pre-provisioning capacity and automating complex network configuration including BGP peering, VLAN configuration, and ASN assignment. At GA, customers will also be able to dynamically scale bandwidth from 1 Gbps to 100 Gbps through the AWS Console. The service is designed for high availability, with zero down-time maintenance, backed by a 99.99% SLA.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Interconnect - last mile is available in the San Jose (us-west-1) and N. Virginia (us-east-1) metros through Megaport. You can create a preview Interconnect using the AWS Management Console, Command Line Interface (CLI), or API. Partners can also easily adopt via a published open API package on &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/Interconnect" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;. For more information, see the AWS Interconnect - last mile &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/interconnect/"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/interconnect/lastmile/pricing/"&gt;pricing pages&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker AI now supports serverless model customization for Muse Glimmer, Qwen3.8 27B, and Ministral 3 3B, 8B, and 14B models. In addition to deploying these models on SageMaker AI, you can now adapt them to your specific domains and workflows, using supervised fine-tuning (SFT), direct preference optimization (DPO), and reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT) techniques. You can also adapt these models with either low-rank adaptation (LoRA) or full fine-tuning techniques. This launch adds these models to a wide variety of models available for serverless customization on SageMaker AI, including models from the Nova, Gemma 4, Nemotron 3.5, Qwen, Llama, gpt-oss, and DeepSeek families.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Model customization lets you tailor foundation models with your proprietary data so a smaller, right-sized model can match frontier-model quality on your tasks, while also reducing cost and latency. You can use labeled data with SFT to improve accuracy on domain-specific tasks, preference data with DPO to align outputs with your organization's tone, or reward signals with RFT to improve performance on new tasks. With serverless customization, SageMaker AI handles all infrastructure provisioning and training orchestration, so you can focus on your data and evaluation rather than cluster management, and only pay for what you use.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Serverless model customization on SageMaker AI is available in US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), and Europe (Ireland). To get started, navigate to the Models page in Amazon SageMaker Studio to launch a customization job, use the &lt;a href="https://sagemaker.readthedocs.io/en/stable/model_customization/index.html"&gt;SageMaker Python SDK&lt;/a&gt; or use our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/model-customize-agent-skills.html"&gt;Agent skills&lt;/a&gt; with your choice of coding assistant. To learn more and see the full supported list of models, see the Amazon SageMaker AI model customization &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/customizing-models.html#customizing-models-supported"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway now supports the Amazon Bedrock InvokeModel and InvokeModelWithResponseStream APIs on its inference endpoint, along with model fallback routing for inference traffic. With InvokeModel support, you can point your existing Amazon Bedrock SDK integration at the gateway and reach any model provider through one governed endpoint, without rewriting client code for each provider. With model fallback, the gateway automatically routes a request to an alternative model or provider when the primary destination is throttled, out of capacity, or unreachable, so your agents stay available without each client implementing its own retry logic.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For fallback routing, you define an ordered chain of alternative destinations in a gateway rule, along with the conditions that trigger a switch, such as throttling, server errors, or response timeouts. A built-in circuit breaker skips destinations that are currently failing, so requests reach a healthy destination faster and you avoid added latency for failed attempts. Fallback routing happens behind the scenes, which means you send one request and receive one response, regardless of the internal trial and error on a given request.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These features are available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway is available (except those in China). To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/gateway-rules-fallback.html"&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Lambda MicroVMs is now available in 4 additional AWS Regions: Asia Pacific (Hong Kong), Asia Pacific (Malaysia), Asia Pacific (Thailand), and Europe (Spain). With this launch, you can use Lambda MicroVMs in a total of 14 AWS Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Lambda MicroVMs is a serverless compute primitive that provides VM-level isolation, near-instant launch and resume speeds, and state preservation for executing user or AI generated code.&amp;nbsp; Developers can use Lambda MicroVMs to give each user or job their own isolated compute environment without managing virtualization infrastructure or choosing between isolation, speed, and state retention. MicroVMs enable secure, stateful, and responsive code execution for applications including AI coding assistants, interactive development environments, and vulnerability scanners.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With today's launch, developers can run Lambda MicroVMs closer to their end-users in 4 additional Regions, helping meet latency and data residency requirements. To get started, create a MicroVM image from your Dockerfile, then launch MicroVMs from that image. Give each user or job their own MicroVM with a dedicated HTTPS URL that supports popular connectivity protocols such as HTTP/2, gRPC, and WebSockets. You can use MicroVMs through the AWS Lambda console, AWS CloudFormation, AWS Cloud Development Kit, or with your preferred Agentic development tools using the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/tree/main/skills/specialized-skills/serverless-skills/aws-lambda-microvms"&gt;Agent Toolkit for AWS&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-microvms-guide.html"&gt;AWS Lambda MicroVMs developer guide&lt;/a&gt;. For information on pricing, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/pricing/"&gt;Lambda MicroVMs pricing&lt;/a&gt;. Visit the &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region page&lt;/a&gt; for the latest region availability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Quick is now available in the AWS Asia Pacific (Singapore) region (ap-southeast-1). This launch allows customers in Singapore to access the full power of Amazon Quick while meeting local requirements for data sovereignty.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Quick provides business users an agentic teammate that quickly answers questions at work and turns those answers into actions. With Amazon Quick, every user is empowered to make better decisions faster and take actions without switching applications using AI they can trust. Today’s launch allows customers to take advantage of Amazon Quick’s capabilities including AI-powered chat, Research, Spaces, and QuickSight dashboards — with their data stored and processed locally within the AWS Singapore region.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For a full list of AWS regions where Amazon Quick is available, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/regions.html"&gt;Quick regional availability page.&lt;/a&gt; To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/"&gt;Amazon Quick documentation&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/quick/"&gt;product detail page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announced the expansion of 10 Gbps and 100 Gbps dedicated connections with MACsec encryption capabilities at the existing &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect"&gt;AWS Direct Connect&lt;/a&gt; location in the Equinix MA3 data center near Manchester, England. You can now establish private, direct network access to all public AWS Regions (except those in China), AWS GovCloud Regions, and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/localzones/"&gt;AWS Local Zones&lt;/a&gt; from this location. This is the third AWS Direct Connect location in England to provide 100 Gbps connections with MACsec encryption capabilities.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The Direct Connect service enables you to establish a private, physical network connection between AWS and your data center, office, or colocation environment. These private connections can provide a more consistent network experience than those made over the public internet.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For more information on the over 150 Direct Connect locations worldwide, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect/locations/"&gt;locations&lt;/a&gt; section of the Direct Connect &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect"&gt;product detail pages&lt;/a&gt;. Or, visit our &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/directconnect/getting-started/"&gt;getting started&lt;/a&gt; page to learn more about how to purchase and deploy Direct Connect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, you can reserve compute capacity for your Amazon Aurora and Amazon RDS DB instances in one or more Availability Zones for as long as you need it. Reserving capacity helps ensure that DB instance create, restore, scale-compute, and start operations don't fail because of insufficient capacity.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;On-Demand Capacity Reservations are designed for workloads that need assurance that specific DB instance capacity will be available when they need it for business-critical events, such as migrations, product launches, seasonal peaks, high-availability deployments, or disaster recovery. You choose the DB instance class, engine, Availability Zones, and reserved capacity per Availability Zone, and RDS holds that capacity for you. Capacity can be reserved in two or more Availability Zones to support Multi-AZ deployments. To use the reserved capacity, reference the reservation when you create, restore, modify, or start a DB instance.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can reserve capacity for immediate use with no commitment and cancel at any time, or schedule a reservation to begin 5 to 120 days in the future with a minimum commitment. Reserved capacity is billed at On-Demand rates while the reservation is active, whether or not DB instances within the reservation are running. Discounts from your existing Reserved DB Instances or Database Savings Plans apply automatically.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;On-Demand Capacity Reservations are available for &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Aurora&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon RDS&lt;/a&gt; in all AWS commercial Regions. To learn more about On-Demand Capacity Reservations, visit the documentation for &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/capacity-reservations.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Aurora&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/capacity-reservations.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon RDS&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Beam, the first open weight model offered by US-based Reflection, is now available in Amazon SageMaker JumpStart, the model hub in Amazon SageMaker AI. According to Beam, they operate at the frontier of open weight models in its class, with strong performance across reasoning, coding, and agentic benchmarks. Beam is for teams who build on and with an open weight model, not just call one. Customers who want to adapt a frontier model to their own domain can fine-tune it, inspect and modify its weights, and embed it deep in their agents and workflows, all while keeping full control of their data and infrastructure. Beam runs alongside other models that customers already use in SageMaker AI and scales to meet production demand with the same controls, audit trails, and monitoring built into every SageMaker AI deployment. Additionally, Beam was ported and optimized for AWS Trainium3, giving customers a high-performance, cost-efficient option for deploying and scaling the model from day one. “&lt;i&gt;Reflection is delivering frontier AI that developers can actually control, not just rent through an API. Bringing Beam to SageMaker AI puts that capability in front of millions of developers on infrastructure they already trust, running in their own AWS account, on their own data, fully in their control,&lt;/i&gt;" said Misha Laskin, co-founder and CEO, Reflection.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Teams building on Beam can take it well beyond calling an API: fine-tune it on proprietary code and data, specialize it for their own domains, and wire it into multi-step agentic workflows across their tools. In practice that means resolving real software issues, generating and reviewing code, automating operations work, and tackling advanced math and science reasoning on a model they can shape rather than one they simply consume. Because Beam is released under the Apache 2.0 license, customers can inspect and customize the model, while also running it in their own AWS accounts, so data stays private and under their control, protected by the same AWS security and compliance capabilities they already rely on. “&lt;i&gt;Advanced AI models like Beam, now available on Amazon SageMaker, let us run open weight frontier AI entirely inside our own secure environments, with full visibility into the weights, the data, and the pipeline. In the age of multi-model AI, the pairing of openness and managed infrastructure is an essential addition to the AI ecosystem. The freedom to deploy and adapt advanced AI, tailored to mission problems with client-specific security and controls, gives more agencies greater flexibility to deploy advanced AI across their enterprise,&lt;/i&gt;” said Garrett Berntsen, Chief AI Officer, Accenture Federal Services.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Beam is initially available in Amazon SageMaker JumpStart in US East (N. Virginia) and US East (Ohio). The model is available in BF16, FP8, and NVFP4 checkpoint variants, and can be deployed through a fully managed SageMaker JumpStart endpoint. To start, search for "Beam" in SageMaker JumpStart within Amazon SageMaker Studio, or deploy it directly on &lt;a href="http://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/ai/"&gt;Sagemaker AI&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp; To learn more, read the &lt;a href="http://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/ai/"&gt;AWS Machine Learning blog: Introducing Reflection Beam&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/"&gt;SageMaker AI documentation&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/studio-jumpstart.html"&gt;SageMaker JumpStart documentation&lt;/a&gt; for deployment guidance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Neuron 2.33 is now available, introducing TorchNeuron, the native PyTorch backend for AWS Trainium, in public beta. You can run models written with the standard PyTorch APIs on Amazon EC2 Trn2 and Trn3 instances, in eager mode or with torch.compile. This release also introduces Neuron Observer in public beta, which detects Trainium health issues in under a second and identifies the affected node or device.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The vLLM Neuron plugin upgrades to vLLM 0.29.0, and the vLLM Omni Neuron plugin for Wan2.2 video generation is now part of the Neuron release. Both support native PyTorch compilation through TorchNeuron. A new Neuron Deep Learning AMI and Deep Learning Container for PyTorch training include TorchNeuron, PyTorch 2.14, and TorchTitan. Neuron Explorer opens profiles captured with torch.profiler. From a PyTorch memory snapshot, it now attributes allocations to the Python source lines that made them, for debugging out-of-memory errors in eager mode.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Kernels written with the Neuron Kernel Interface (NKI) can now use the full Python language for metaprogramming, including generators and class inheritance. The NKI Library adds 25 new experimental kernels, including kernels for DeepSeek-V3.2, Qwen3.5, and Kimi Delta Attention.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see the following resources:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://awsdocs-neuron.readthedocs-hosted.com/en/latest/release-notes/index.html"&gt;AWS Neuron 2.33.0 Release Notes&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://awsdocs-neuron.readthedocs-hosted.com/en/latest/torch/index.html"&gt;AWS TorchNeuron documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://awsdocs-neuron.readthedocs-hosted.com/en/latest/tools/neuron-observer/index.html" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Neuron Observer documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://awsdocs-neuron.readthedocs-hosted.com/en/latest/nki/index.html" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Neuron Kernel Interface documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ai/machine-learning/neuron/"&gt;AWS Neuron&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Connect Customer now offers two different layouts that optimize screen space on the agent workspace. Contact center agents spend hours each day in the agent workspace, which includes all of the contact controls and applications they need to resolve customer issues. Agents who handle different channels and workflows want the workspace arranged differently, so that they can quickly read, respond, and access their tools. Now, administrators can choose the best layout for each team, maximizing agent efficiency and satisfaction.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;On the Modern layout, contact controls are at the top of the workspace, maximizing screen space for the applications beneath during voice calls. When agents handle chat, email, and task contacts on the Modern layout, they now have a taller, wider area to read and respond. The Classic layout offers a familiar experience for agents already using the agent workspace, now with contact controls that expand and contract by channel and a resizable agent assist application. Administrators can assign the Modern layout to a team migrating from another agent desktop with controls at the top, while teams already familiar with the agent workspace can use the Classic layout.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can use the new layouts for the Amazon Connect Customer agent workspace in the following AWS regions: US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), Africa (Cape Town), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Canada (Central), Europe (Frankfurt), and Europe (London).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more and get started, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/agent-workspace.html"&gt;agent workspace&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Connect Customer administrator guide.&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon S3 server access log delivery to Amazon CloudWatch Logs is now available in the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, AWS European Sovereign Cloud, and AWS China Regions. With your access logs in CloudWatch Logs, you can query them as they arrive, set alarms, and encrypt them with AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) keys. This new delivery path complements the existing delivery of server access logs to S3 general purpose buckets, giving you more flexibility in how you monitor and analyze access to your data.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With delivery to CloudWatch Logs, you can set alarms on error rates, monitor traffic patterns, investigate access incidents, and correlate S3 access activity with the rest of your operational data.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;S3 server access log delivery to CloudWatch Logs is now available in all AWS Regions. Amazon CloudWatch Logs charges for vended logs apply. For pricing details, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/pricing/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon CloudWatch pricing&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/ServerLogs.html" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon S3 User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, Amazon SageMaker AI announces the ability to configure the sequence length used during your serverless model customization job. Amazon SageMaker AI serverless model customization supports fine-tuning for many open-weight models, including models from popular families including gpt-oss, Gemma, Llama, Nemotron, and Qwen. Previously, each fine-tuning recipe ran at a fixed sequence length that customers could not modify. Customers sometimes need to modify these parameters to achieve better results for their use case. Customers also asked for longer sequence lengths than the static defaults we supported on some models.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Now, you can set sequence length parameters as part of the customization job's hyperparameters, anywhere up to the published maximum for your chosen model and technique. You can set the dataset_max_len hyperparameter for SFT and DPO jobs or set max_prompt_length and max_response_length hyperparameters for RFT jobs. This gives you more control to match your training data characteristics, whether you are working with short question-answer pairs or multi-page documents. Published maximums are up to 16K tokens for many models depending on model and technique.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Serverless model customization on SageMaker AI is available in US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), and Europe (Ireland). To get started, navigate to the Models page in Amazon SageMaker Studio to launch a customization job, use the &lt;a href="https://sagemaker.readthedocs.io/en/stable/model_customization/index.html"&gt;SageMaker Python SDK&lt;/a&gt; or use our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/model-customize-agent-skills.html"&gt;Agent skills&lt;/a&gt; with your choice of coding assistant. To learn more, and see the list of models and supported sequence lengths per technique, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/customizing-models-sequence-lengths.html"&gt;SageMaker AI documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Firewall now supports IP validation, extending domain-based filtering beyond HTTP/HTTPS to protocols such as SFTP and SSH. This new capability validates that a connection's destination IP address was resolved through your VPC's Amazon Route 53 VPC Resolver, closing security gaps where clients could bypass domain rules.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature enables Network Firewall customers to implement domain filtering rules for any protocol, not just HTTP/HTTPS, providing more flexible security policies than IP-based rules alone. It simplifies protection against Server Name Identifier (SNI) spoofing by validating the IPs resolved by the Route 53 VPC Resolver against the SNI, ensuring traffic only reaches legitimately resolved destinations. Organizations can now enforce consistent domain-based security policies across all outbound traffic types, from web applications to file transfer protocols, without complex workarounds. Additionally, Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall can be used with your VPC Resolver for extended DNS security.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Resolved IP validation is available in all AWS commercial regions where AWS Network Firewall and Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall are available, at no additional charge. For a full list of supported regions, visit the &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities/explore?f=eJxVirEKg0AQBX9FXq1FWiGFjZAmRVKkcC0uusji4cneqYj477lcEUg3b94c8Gy5C9w_eBA3eZRoCIsv2PhQXAgt8l9TrUaseYuVsMewmvbsT-WY1c2sYa_FBtbYHIRAKJs2J8wRCE4JcQxfG98xSc-6Sse3Pn0uuWviNXH1emZ3DpvTMatFeTPWEs72xPkBR_VAow&amp;amp;tab=service-feature" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit AWS Network Firewall &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt; and service &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/latest/developerguide/route53-resolved-ip-validation.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transform for migrations now gives you three ways to bring your network to AWS. In addition to migrating and modernizing your existing network, you can build a new network from scratch or convert your on-premises access rules into optimized security groups and deploy them into existing VPCs. Whichever path you choose, you arrive on AWS with a secure and scalable network foundation.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;On-premises networks accumulate years of definitions and access rules that you may not want to carry to AWS as-is. For a clean start, AWS Transform builds a new network aligned to AWS best practices. To bring your access rules with you, AWS Transform converts them into security groups and modernizes them as it goes, detecting stale rules and recommending optimizations, then deploys the groups into your existing VPCs without redeploying your network stack.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These capabilities&amp;nbsp;allow you migrate to any &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/latest/userguide/transform-vmware-connect-target-account.html#transform-vmware-cta-supported-regions"&gt;AWS commercial or GovCloud Region&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/latest/userguide/transform-vmware-migrate-network.html"&gt;Migrate networks&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/transform/migrations/"&gt;AWS Transform for migrations&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS announces Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) Gateway, a new resource that simplifies ingress networking for containerized applications by dynamically provisioning and managing Application Load Balancers (ALBs) and their supporting resources directly in your account.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With ECS Gateway, platform teams define organizational networking standards once on a gateway and associate it with one or more clusters, giving organizations a centralized way to govern how traffic reaches their ECS services. Application teams simply opt in to inherit those standards without requiring networking expertise. ECS Gateway offers host-based routing, allows up to 25 services to share an ALB to optimize infrastructure costs, and gives each service a unique Domain Name System (DNS) hostname. ECS manages the full lifecycle of the underlying infrastructure, provisioning the first ALB when your first service connects, adding more ALBs when capacity is reached, and removing them when no longer needed. You can inspect these resources and the gateway route to each service to see exactly how requests reach your applications. For traffic and health monitoring, you can use standard ALB metrics in Amazon CloudWatch and target health information from Elastic Load Balancing.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, create an ECS Gateway, associate it with a cluster, and opt in your ECS service by adding the gateway connector parameter using the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, API, AWS SDKs, or AWS CloudFormation. ECS Gateway is available for ECS services using the awsvpc network mode across all launch types. It is available now in all commercial AWS Regions at no additional charge. You pay only for the underlying infrastructure provisioned in your account when your services connect to the gateway. To learn more, refer to the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-ecs-gateway-a-fully-managed-ingress-solution-for-ecs-services"&gt;AWS News blog&lt;/a&gt; and our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/developerguide/gateway-overview.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html"&gt;Amazon DynamoDB&lt;/a&gt; global tables now support disassociating a replica without deleting its table or data. Global tables provide fully managed, multi-Region, multi-active replication. You can now stop replication to and from a replica and continue using the same table as a single-Region table.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An account can leave a shared multi-account global table and continue using its table independently. You can also stop replication to a Region while keeping its table for local workloads, validation, or audit, without creating a replacement table or copying data.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;DynamoDB supports disassociating replicas in all AWS Regions for same-account and multi-account global tables that use multi-Region eventual consistency (MREC).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, use the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, DynamoDB API, or AWS CloudFormation. For more information, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/globaltables-disassociate.html"&gt;Disassociating replicas&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon DynamoDB&amp;nbsp;Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;WorkSpaces for AI agents now handles authentication and desktop connections as part of the managed Model Context Protocol (MCP) service, so you can connect agents to governed cloud desktops, removing the need to write custom code. This makes it easy to use WorkSpaces with any client that supports MCP.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Connecting an agent to a desktop previously required you to manage presession requirements; generate, invoke, and rotate streaming URLs manually, adding complexity every time a session expired. You can now create sessions with your existing AWS credentials through OAuth 2.0, and the service handles the connection lifecycle. You configure once which desktop stack to use, and your agents reach the right desktop without ever seeing or handling the underlying streaming connection. You can also query session state to see which desktop stack, fleet, and session an agent is connected to, and generate a link for real-time observability of your agent. This gives you visibility and auditability over active agent work to know what your agents are doing and where.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To help your agents work with multiple applications, they can now discover and launch applications through a catalog&amp;nbsp;MCP Tool rather than navigating menus. Using these new tools, agents can list the available applications, launch one or several at runtime, and switch between them, replacing the start-menu and taskbar navigation with a programmatic, deterministic path that improves accuracy and reduces token cost.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;WorkSpaces for AI agents runs agent sessions inside the same network isolation and compliance boundaries that govern your human workforce, inheriting the VPC controls, data-loss prevention policies, and session-level security posture that enterprises have already configured. The service is also now available in four additional AWS Regions: South America (São Paulo), Europe (Milan), Europe (Spain), and Asia Pacific (Malaysia).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started operating AI agents on WorkSpaces, visit: &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appstream2/latest/developerguide/agent-access.html"&gt;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appstream2/latest/developerguide/agent-access.html&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, we are introducing a new model behind the Amazon Polly Generative engine that delivers more natural-sounding, accurate speech output with low latency. With this upgrade, you get access to an enhanced voice portfolio with higher overall quality, 14 new voices across nine locales, and two new languages—Hindi and Finnish—now generally available on the Generative engine for the first time. New voices include Kenneth and Nate (US English), Russell and Curtis (Australian English), Kiara and Arjun (Hindi and Indian English), Tina (German), Carlos (US Spanish), Leo and Carolina (Brazilian Portuguese), Hyunduk (Korean), and Suvi (Finnish).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/polly"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Amazon Polly&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; is a fully managed service that turns text into natural-sounding speech. This drop-in model upgrade makes Generative voices more natural and accurate. Complex content such as numbers, email addresses, and street addresses is designed to sound more reliable, reducing the need to fine-tune speech output. You can continue to use Speech Synthesis Markup Language (SSML) tags to control speech output on the Generative engine, just as before. The low-latency streaming API makes it easy to build contact center experiences, chatbots, and large language model (LLM) based agentic voice applications for real-time conversations. The richness and variety of voice options are also well suited for news, media, entertainment, e-learning, and accessibility use cases. This launch also expands the Amazon Polly Generative offering to AWS GovCloud, providing more options for public sector and regulated industries.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;All new Generative voices are now available in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (Zurich), Europe (London), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Canada (Central), and AWS GovCloud (US-West).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To hear how Polly voices sound, go to &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/polly/features/?nc=sn&amp;amp;loc=3"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Amazon Polly Features&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. For more details on the Polly offerings and use, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/polly/latest/dg/what-is.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Amazon Polly documentation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/polly/pricing/?nc=sn&amp;amp;loc=4"&gt;&lt;u&gt;pricing page&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS launched the public beta of TorchNeuron, an open source native PyTorch backend for AWS Trainium. Trainium is a portfolio of AI chips designed to deliver high performance and cost efficiency for deep learning AI training and inference workloads. TorchNeuron enables AI scientists and researchers to experiment and build models with PyTorch natively on Trainium.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With TorchNeuron, developers can enable their PyTorch code on Trainium simply by switching to .to('neuron') while maintaining full PyTorch compatibility with eager execution, torch.compile, and standard PyTorch APIs. Additionally, TorchNeuron integrates with Neuron Kernel Interface (NKI) to enable custom operators and performance optimization for Trainium hardware.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;TorchNeuron is available as open source through the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws-neuron/torch-neuronx"&gt;TorchNeuron github repo&lt;/a&gt; for Amazon EC2 Trainium2 instances and Amazon EC2 Trainium3 instances.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about TorchNeuron and AWS Trainium accelerators, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ai/machine-learning/neuron/"&gt;AWS Neuron page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock now supports structured outputs for all Anthropic Claude models across both the bedrock-runtime and the bedrock-mantle endpoint. Whether you call Claude through boto3 and the bedrock-runtime APIs or through the Messages API and the bedrock-mantle endpoint, you can now receive schema-compliant JSON responses that conform to a defined structure, without custom parsing, retry logic, or post-processing.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With structured outputs for Claude on Amazon Bedrock, you can automate reliable, schema-compliant tasks across industries. Examples include extracting every required field from financial earnings reports and compliance documents, parsing clinical notes into Electronic Health Records (EHR), and enriching product catalogs with consistent, validated results. You can also analyze contracts for key terms, parties, and obligations, or power intelligent support ticket routing where intents, sentiments, and entities always match your application's data model.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see the Amazon Bedrock &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/claude-messages-structured-outputs.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;. Structured outputs is available where claude models are available. For more information check the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/models-region-compatibility.html#model-regions-anthropic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;regional availability&lt;/a&gt; details page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Clean Rooms now supports &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/clean-rooms/latest/userguide/prepare-data-databricks.html" target="_blank"&gt;Databricks as a data source&lt;/a&gt;, so collaboration members and their partners can securely analyze data managed in Databricks without moving or revealing the source data. Members query a Databricks table shared through Delta Sharing directly in a collaboration. Databricks joins AWS Glue, Snowflake, and Amazon Athena as supported data sources in AWS Clean Rooms.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, members with data in Databricks had to export or replicate it into Amazon S3 to use it as a table in a collaboration, which added pipeline work and an extra copy of the data to govern. Now members can register a Databricks table directly by providing the workspace URL, schema, and table along with a Delta Sharing share, and make it available to a collaboration. For example, a retailer that manages its customer data in Databricks can collaborate with a media partner on campaign measurement in AWS Clean Rooms, running approved analyses against its Databricks data without first building and maintaining an export to S3.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Clean Rooms helps companies and their partners easily analyze and collaborate on their collective datasets without revealing or copying one another’s underlying data. For more information about the AWS Regions where AWS Clean Rooms is available, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/clean-rooms.html#clean-rooms_region" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; table. To learn more about collaborating with AWS Clean Rooms, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/clean-rooms/" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Clean Rooms&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS IAM Identity Center now retains your workforce identities and their assignments when you change your identity source from Active Directory to an external identity provider (IdP), such as Okta or Microsoft Entra ID, or to the Identity Center directory. Specifically, it preserves your users, groups, group memberships, permission set assignments, and application assignments. Previously, this change required you to recreate them in IAM Identity Center.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;IAM Identity Center helps you connect your workforce identities to AWS once and manage their access centrally across AWS applications and accounts. When you change your identity source from Active Directory, your existing identity store remains in place, so your users and groups keep the same IDs. As a result, users retain their access to AWS accounts and applications, and policies that reference your user IDs, group IDs, or identity store ID continue to work.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To make this change, configure your new identity source with the standard setup required for users to sign in. After the change, your workforce signs in through your dual-stack AWS access portal URL. Changing your identity source in the other direction, to Active Directory, works as before. This feature is available for organization instances of IAM Identity Center in all AWS Regions where IAM Identity Center is available. IAM Identity Center is provided at no additional cost.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, navigate to the IAM Identity Center console. For more information, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/manage-your-identity-source-change.html"&gt;Change your identity source&lt;/a&gt; in the IAM Identity Center User Guide. To learn more about IAM Identity Center, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/iam/identity-center/"&gt;product detail page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Partner Central now features Program Navigator, a unified experience that lets partners discover, apply for, and track AWS Specialization program applications in one place. Partners can browse specialization designations, view requirements and benefits, and submit applications through a guided, step-by-step workflow.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The new Case Study Generator in AWS Partner Central gives partners a redesigned creation experience with three starting paths: build a case study directly with AI assistance, import from an existing customer success story URL, or auto-populate from an opportunity. Case studies created for go-to-market purposes now follow a streamlined review path, a lightweight policy validation that lets partners publish directly to AWS domains without a specialization review. The deeper specialization-level review applies only to case studies associated with Program Navigator applications.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Additionally, AWS has streamlined the specialization application review process through Program Navigator. Partners submitting applications can expect faster review times and more detailed, requirement-level feedback throughout the process. Partners applying for non-specialization programs continue to access those programs through the existing experience.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Program Navigator and Case Study Generator are available to all AWS Partners in &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/partnercentral/home?region=us-east-2"&gt;AWS Partner Central&lt;/a&gt;. To get started, navigate to Program Navigator and Case Studies under the Go to Market section in the left navigation panel.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock Guardrails now offers a Standard tier for contextual grounding checks, which detect and filter hallucinations by checking whether a model response is grounded in your reference source and relevant to the user's query. The Standard tier is more robust than the Classic tier. It supports responses that include reasoning or conversational content, scores differently worded responses with the same meaning more consistently, and provides more comprehensive language support, matching the Standard tier for content filters and denied topics. Amazon Bedrock Guardrails provides configurable safeguards for generative AI applications on any foundation model through the ApplyGuardrail API.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Until now, contextual grounding checks applied strict grounding suited to summarization, paraphrasing, and question answering. With the Standard tier, you can also apply contextual grounding checks to conversational applications and to responses that reason over the source. You choose the tier for each guardrail in the Amazon Bedrock console or API. Existing guardrails keep the Classic tier until you change them. Pricing is the same for both tiers. To use the Standard tier, your guardrail must use cross-Region inference with a guardrail profile.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Standard tier for contextual grounding checks is available in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (N. California), US West (Oregon), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (Ireland), Europe (Milan), Europe (Paris), Europe (Spain), Europe (Stockholm), and Asia Pacific (Tokyo). To learn more, see the Amazon Bedrock Guardrails documentation on &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/guardrails-contextual-grounding-check.html"&gt;contextual grounding checks&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/guardrails-tiers.html"&gt;safeguard tiers&lt;/a&gt;, or visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/guardrails"&gt;Amazon Bedrock Guardrails product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock Advanced Prompt Optimization now supports prompt shortening, an optional setting that reduces the length of your prompt while maintaining or improving your evaluation scores. Advanced Prompt Optimization optimizes prompts for any model on Amazon Bedrock against your own evaluation criteria. Until now, optimization focused on quality alone. Optimized prompts were often longer than the original. Longer prompts raise input token costs and can increase time to first token, which matters at scale and in latency-sensitive applications.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When you turn on prompt shortening for a prompt template in your input dataset, the optimizer reduces the prompt's length. The optimized prompt is returned only when it is shorter than your original prompt and scores at least as high on your evaluation. Otherwise your original prompt is returned, so the setting never leaves you with a longer or lower-scoring prompt. Results include a compression ratio and the original and optimized token counts, in the output file and on the console results page. For example, a compression ratio of 0.7 means the optimized prompt uses 30% fewer tokens. Prompt shortening works with any evaluation method, including steering criteria, LLM-as-a-judge rubrics, and custom Lambda evaluators.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Prompt shortening is available in all AWS Regions where Advanced Prompt Optimization is available. To get started, enable prompt shortening for a prompt template in your input dataset and create an optimization job as usual. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/advanced-prompt-optimization-shorten.html"&gt;Shorten prompts&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Bedrock User Guide, the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/pricing/"&gt;Amazon Bedrock pricing page&lt;/a&gt;, and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/"&gt;Amazon Bedrock product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transform now supports more types of existing AWS environments when building landing zones for your migration, including AWS Control Tower landing zones and landing zones built with AWS Organizations. AWS Transform runs a foundation gap analysis of your current environment against AWS best practices and expands your landing zone on top of what's already in your target accounts, so your team can start migrating sooner.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;AWS Transform also now supports an Infrastructure as Code (IaC)-only mode for building landing zones for your migration. In this mode, AWS Transform analyzes your organization, generates recommendations, and produces IaC tailored to your migration plan in AWS CDK, Terraform, or Landing Zone Accelerator (LZA) format. IaC-only mode is designed for organizations that prefer not to grant AWS Transform direct access to their management account. AWS Transform can now connect through a delegated administrator (DA) account instead. It also benefits customers who manage their own deployment pipeline and don't need the agent to deploy automatically.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The AWS Transform for migrations landing zone agent automates landing zone design end to end, including foundation setup, organizational unit (OU) and account structure, service control policies (SCPs), and workload accounts aligned to your migration planning and waves. You can deploy directly or generate IaC for your own pipeline.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These new capabilities are available in &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/latest/userguide/transform-vmware-connect-target-account.html#transform-vmware-cta-supported-regions"&gt;all target AWS Regions supported by AWS Transform for migrations&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, please visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/latest/userguide/transform-vmware-landing-zone.html"&gt;AWS Transform User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, Amazon CloudWatch announces support for including alarm tags in action payloads and Amazon EventBridge events, enabling customers to route and filter alarm notifications by tag without additional lookups. When enabled on an alarm, CloudWatch adds the alarm tags to the messages it sends to Amazon SNS, AWS Lambda, and Amazon EventBridge each time the alarm changes state. This option is designed for customers who route many alarms through a shared notification pipeline and need to identify the owning team, environment, or service directly from the message.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With tags in actions payloads, customers can avoid the extra work of correlating tag metadata with alarm events after the fact. For example, a team that routes hundreds of alarms through a single SNS topic to a Lambda function previously had to call ListTagsForResource for each event to find the owning team before paging. With this feature, the tags arrive in the notification, so the function can route directly from the message. The setting is off by default and configurable per alarm, and customers can include all tags, only selected tag keys, or all tags except selected keys. It is supported for metric, composite, and log alarms.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Tags in actions and events for CloudWatch Alarms are available in all AWS Regions where Amazon CloudWatch is available except Middle East (UAE), and Middle East (Bahrain).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/alarm-tags-in-notifications.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Alarm tags in action payloads&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon CloudWatch User Guide. To learn more about Amazon CloudWatch Alarms, visit the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/"&gt;Amazon CloudWatch&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; product page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/"&gt;Amazon ElastiCache&lt;/a&gt; Serverless for Valkey now supports online migration from self-managed Valkey and Redis OSS on Amazon EC2 with zero downtime. Zero downtime means zero Errors returned to the client during migration, measured the same way the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/sla/"&gt;ElastiCache SLA&lt;/a&gt; measures availability. Your application continues reading and writing through cutover, and you do not need third-party replication tools.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Online migration runs in three steps. You prepare the migration, start the data sync, and complete the cutover. ElastiCache gives you a migration endpoint your application can point to when you prepare the migration, and data replicates continuously from your source once you start the sync. You choose when writes move to the serverless cache, with a manual cutover or an automatic one once your data is in sync. ElastiCache records each step as an event on the cache’s Events tab, with the reason for any failed step.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Online migration connects to your source using encryption in transit (TLS) and authentication, and reads credentials from AWS Secrets Manager, so your existing security controls stay in place. Online migration supports self-managed caches in cluster mode on Amazon EC2 running Redis OSS 4.0 through 7.2 or Valkey 7.2 through 8.1, and you can migrate them straight to Valkey 9.0.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Migration usage is free for the first 180 days per AWS account, starting when the account first prepares a migration. After that, you pay per node-hour for the migration nodes that ElastiCache runs to replicate your data, for as long as a migration is active. The ElastiCache Serverless cache is billed separately at standard data storage and ECPU rates, including during the free period. For pricing details, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/pricing/"&gt;Amazon ElastiCache pricing&lt;/a&gt;. Online migration is available in all commercial AWS Regions, the China Regions, and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, create or select an empty ElastiCache Serverless cache to migrate into, then start the migration from the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/elasticache/"&gt;ElastiCache console&lt;/a&gt; or the AWS CLI. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/migration-to-serverless.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS announces that Amazon CloudWatch Omni is now available in eight additional AWS Regions. CloudWatch Omni is an AI-powered observability experience, organized around your teams and the applications they run, that helps you observe and troubleshoot your applications and agents in one place. It combines the interoperability of OpenTelemetry with the scale and reliability of CloudWatch, and meets you where you work — a standalone web experience with single sign-on (SSO), or a local IDE extension.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With CloudWatch Omni, You create spaces in your central accounts to see telemetry across your AWS accounts, Regions, and other clouds, including Azure and GCP. Omni discovers services, maps dependencies, and surfaces golden metrics automatically and you can interact with telemetry through chat, point-and-click, or your own tool via Agent Toolkit for AWS to find signals, build dynamic views, and reach root cause powered by AWS DevOps Agent. A dedicated agent observability experience adds an evaluation-driven workflow for frameworks including LangGraph, CrewAI, OpenAI Agents SDK, Vercel AI SDK, and Strands.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this expansion, CloudWatch Omni is now available in Canada (Central), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (London), and South America (São Paulo), in addition to US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), and Europe (Ireland).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, create your Omni space from the CloudWatch console, configure SSO, and sign in. Agent developers can install the free &lt;a href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=AmazonWebServices.amazon-cloudwatch-omni" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;CloudWatch Omni extension for VS Code&lt;/a&gt;, Cursor, and Kiro. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/omni/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon CloudWatch Omni product page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/cloudwatch-omni.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/omni/pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;CloudWatch Omni pricing page.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;EC2 Image Builder now supports using existing instances from an Auto Scaling Group (ASG) for building images, enabling faster builds by eliminating the time spent in launching and bootstrapping new instances and reusing already running EC2 instances.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this feature, you can provide an ASG in your Image Builder infrastructure configuration. Instead of launching a new instance at the build time, Image Builder selects an already-running instance from this ASG, applies your build components, and creates the image. The ASG continuously replenishes the pool as instances are used for builds, ensuring instances are always available for subsequent pipeline runs.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is fully opt-in and does not affect existing pipelines. Pipelines without an ASG configured continue to use the default launch path with no behavior change.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Regions at no additional cost. To configure ASG integration in your Image Builder pipelines, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/imagebuilder/latest/userguide/infra-config-parent-instance.html"&gt;EC2 Image Builder documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon GuardDuty now supports optional detection rules for Runtime Monitoring. You can enable more than 50 Runtime Monitoring rules that help detect process, file, and network activity that might be routine in one environment but a sign of compromise in another, such as modifying sudoers files or disabling audit logging.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The new rules provide detections for Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon EKS, and Amazon ECS on both EC2 and AWS Fargate, and span 13 finding types mapped to MITRE ATT&amp;amp;CK® tactics including persistence, privilege escalation, execution, defense evasion, discovery, exfiltration, and command and control. This release introduces four new finding types: CommandAndControl:Runtime/SuspiciousCommand, CredentialAccess:Runtime/SensitiveFileRead, Discovery:Runtime/SensitiveFileRead, and Exfiltration:Runtime/SuspiciousCommand.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit the GuardDuty console. You can enable rules in dry-run mode to evaluate detection efficacy before enabling live detection.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Custom Detection Rules for Runtime Monitoring is available in all AWS commercial Regions and the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/custom-detection-rules.html"&gt;Amazon GuardDuty Custom Detection Rules&lt;/a&gt;. To receive programmatic updates on new Amazon GuardDuty features and threat detections, subscribe to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/guardduty_sns.html"&gt;Amazon GuardDuty SNS topic&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock now supports SpaceXAI Grok 4.7, a frontier model built for coding, agentic tasks, and knowledge work in AWS GovCloud (US).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Grok 4.7 builds on Grok 4.6 with better mixed-document handling, more dependable repo-scale coding with planning and error recovery, and enhanced browser-use agents for form fills and portal navigation. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Get started with Grok 4.7 in the&lt;a href="https://console.amazonaws-us-gov.com/bedrock/"&gt; Amazon Bedrock console&lt;/a&gt; or programmatically through supported Amazon Bedrock APIs. For information about supported&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/models-region-compatibility.html"&gt; AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt;, endpoints, APIs, features, inference profiles and pricing, see the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-card-xai-grok-4-7.html"&gt;Amazon Bedrock documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, read the launch &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/grok-4-7-is-now-available-on-amazon-bedrock/"&gt;blog post&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Elemental MediaConnect Router now supports high bitrate modes with two new tiers: 250 Mbps and 500 Mbps. This allows professional broadcasters that need higher bitrates to transport high-value content without compromising quality or reliability.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This enhancement enables industry-standard workflows such as VSF TR-07 compliant transport stream distribution for broadcast contribution, content distribution, and remote production. High bitrate mode supports up to two redundant sources for reliability and includes data plane optimizations that reduce jitter for improved performance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/mediaconnect/"&gt;AWS Elemental MediaConnect product page&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/mediaconnect/pricing/"&gt;MediaConnect pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/sqlserver/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for SQL Server&lt;/a&gt; now supports creating DB snapshots directly on read replica instances. Previously, you could create snapshots only on standalone and primary DB instances. You can now snapshot a read replica without promoting it first, giving you more flexibility in your backup and disaster recovery (DR) workflows.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, enabling Multi-AZ on a promoted read replica meant waiting for an initial full snapshot to finish before the conversion could begin. On larger storage configurations—such as io2 volumes up to 64 TiB—that first snapshot could take a long time, making DR timelines hard to predict. Now, Amazon RDS for SQL Server maintains snapshot data on the read replica throughout its lifecycle, so the first snapshot after promotion completes faster. This shortens the time to enable Multi-AZ and helps you plan DR exercises with more predictable recovery timelines. You can take manual snapshots of a read replica, turn on automated backups by setting a backup retention period greater than zero, and retain automated backups when you delete the replica; however, point-in-time recovery (PITR) from a read replica isn't supported.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature works for both in-region and cross-region read replicas. It's available for SQL Server Enterprise Edition on all SQL Server versions that support read replicas, in all AWS Regions where Amazon RDS for SQL Server read replicas are available, including the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/SQLServer.ReadReplicas.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Working with read replicas for Microsoft SQL Server&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing details, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/sqlserver/pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon RDS for SQL Server Pricing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Partner Central now gives AWS Partners more visibility into how the Solution Matching Engine (SME) contributes to their co-sell pipeline. The Solution Matching Engine performance section in Partner Analytics helps partners understand how SME performs for their company and AWS Marketplace listings.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Partners can view recommendations, SME-attributed opportunities, their share of AWS-originated opportunities, stall rate, and pipeline attributed to SME. They can filter by recommendation type or AWS Marketplace listing and explore top customer profiles by Region, industry, and segment. These insights help partners assess their visibility and decide where to improve their listings and opportunity information.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;All partners eligible for &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/partners/programs/ace/"&gt;APN Customer Engagements (ACE)&lt;/a&gt; can find the section on the Opportunity pipeline analysis page under Partner Analytics in &lt;a title="Link to AWS Partner Central" href="https://aws.amazon.com/partners/partner-central/"&gt;AWS Partner Central&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;To learn more about SME and how to strengthen your visibility with AWS sellers, read &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/apn/solution-matching-engine-your-gateway-to-aws-sellers/"&gt;Solution Matching Engine: Your Gateway to AWS Sellers&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon CloudWatch log centralization is now available in AWS GovCloud (US) and 13 additional commercial Regions. CloudWatch log centralization copies log data from multiple AWS accounts and Regions into a single destination account, integrating with AWS Organizations for efficient aggregation without the need for custom solutions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, organizations in the AWS GovCloud (US) partition and commercial partition can each centralize logs across their accounts and Regions for centralized monitoring. You can scope centralization rules to your entire organization, specific organizational units, or selected accounts and copy logs into a single account while preserving source context through enriched system fields.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;CloudWatch log centralization is now additionally available in Africa (Cape Town), Asia Pacific (Hong Kong), Asia Pacific (Hyderabad), Asia Pacific (Jakarta), Asia Pacific (Malaysia), Asia Pacific (Melbourne), Canada West (Calgary), Europe (Milan), Europe (Spain), Europe (Zurich), Israel (Tel Aviv), Middle East (Bahrain), Middle East (UAE), AWS GovCloud (US-East), and AWS GovCloud (US-West). For a complete list of supported Regions, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, create a centralization rule in the Amazon CloudWatch console, or by using the AWS CLI or AWS SDKs. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/"&gt;Log centralization&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon CloudWatch Logs User Guide. Customers can centralize one copy of logs for free; additional copies are charged at $0.05/GB. For details, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/pricing/"&gt;Amazon CloudWatch pricing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon EMR Serverless now supports IPv6 and dual-stack connectivity, expanding beyond the IPv4-only connectivity that was previously available. You can now run your Apache Spark and Apache Hive jobs over IPv6 in your Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) by supplying dual-stack (IPv4 and IPv6) or IPv6-only subnets. This gives you greater flexibility in how your applications connect to data stores and services, while maintaining full backward compatibility with your existing IPv4 workloads.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;As Spark and Hive workloads scale to many workers, the IPv4 address space in your VPCs and subnets can become a constraint, and a growing number of organizations face compliance mandates that require IPv6 adoption. With dual-stack connectivity, your EMR Serverless applications accept both IPv4 and IPv6, making it easy to adopt IPv6 gradually while maintaining backward compatibility with your existing IPv4 workloads. With IPv6-only subnets, you can eliminate the need for IPv4 addresses entirely, overcoming IPv4 address exhaustion and meeting the networking standards and compliance requirements that call for IPv6, while your jobs reach IPv6 endpoints and services both inside and outside your VPC.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;IPv6 and dual-stack connectivity for Amazon EMR Serverless is available at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where Amazon EMR Serverless is available. IPv6 and dual-stack subnets are supported on all EMR Serverless release versions. To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-Serverless-UserGuide/ipv6-subnets.html"&gt;Configure IPv6 subnets for EMR Serverless applications&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-Serverless-UserGuide/vpc-access.html"&gt;Configuring VPC access for EMR Serverless applications&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Lambda durable functions are now available in the AWS GovCloud (US-West) and AWS GovCloud (US-East) Regions. Lambda durable functions enable developers to build reliable multi-step applications and AI workflows within the Lambda developer experience.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Lambda durable functions extend the Lambda programming model with new primitives in your event handler, such as "step" and "wait", allowing you to checkpoint progress, automatically recover from failures, and pause executions without incurring compute charges for on-demand functions. With availability in the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, government customers and regulated industries can orchestrate complex processes such as order workflows, user onboarding, and AI-assisted tasks while meeting stringent compliance and security requirements.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can activate durable functions for new Python (versions 3.13 and 3.14), Node.js (versions 22 and 24), Go (versions 1.24 or later), .Net (version 2.2), or Java (17+) based Lambda functions using the AWS Lambda API, AWS Management Console, or AWS SDK. You can also use infrastructure as code tools such as AWS CloudFormation, AWS Serverless Application Model (AWS SAM), and the AWS Cloud Development Kit (AWS CDK).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For more information on durable functions, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/lambda-durable-functions/"&gt;AWS Lambda durable functions product page&lt;/a&gt; or the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/"&gt;AWS Lambda Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;. To learn about pricing, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/pricing/"&gt;AWS Lambda pricing&lt;/a&gt;. For region availability, visit the &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region page&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;NVIDIA's Nemotron 3.5 Super VL is now available on Amazon SageMaker JumpStart, expanding the portfolio of foundation models available to AWS customers. This vision-language model from the Nemotron 3.5 family accepts images, video, and text as input and generates text, enabling customers to deploy multimodal AI solutions on AWS infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Nemotron 3.5 Super VL is designed for document intelligence, GUI understanding, video question answering, and multimodal agentic applications. It pairs a 120B-parameter hybrid Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) language backbone with 12B active parameters and a vision encoder, supporting context lengths of up to 1 million tokens. The model includes a built-in Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) head for speculative decoding to accelerate text generation, with reasoning configurable per request. It supports ten languages including English, French, Spanish, German, Japanese, Hindi, Korean, and Chinese. Post-trained with multimodal supervised fine-tuning on image, video, document, chart, and GUI data followed by GRPO reinforcement learning, it delivers structured JSON output, tool calling, and configurable reasoning for enterprise agent workflows. SageMaker JumpStart offers two variants: NVFP4 for optimized inference on G7 instances with NVIDIA RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell Server Edition GPUs, and BF16 reference weights on ml.p5en.48xlarge for customization including SFT, RL, distillation, and domain adaptation.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With SageMaker JumpStart, customers can deploy Nemotron 3.5 Super VL with just a few clicks to address their specific AI use cases.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started with these models, navigate to the SageMaker JumpStart model catalog in the SageMaker console or use the SageMaker Python SDK to deploy the models to your AWS account. For more information about deploying and using foundation models in SageMaker JumpStart, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/studio-jumpstart.html"&gt;Amazon SageMaker JumpStart documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Connect Customer now enables you to use flow modules as data requests in agentic CX designer, so you can build and apply reusable logic across your customer experience flows. You can run a flow module at a fixed step in a conversation with a Data request node, or give it to a Generative journey as a tool to call when the conversation needs it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, you can use flow modules to update customer profiles and cases, create tasks, look up data tables, send an SMS confirmation, or check hours of operation and staffing before escalating to a human agent. You can also retrieve data that sits inside a private network, such as core banking, policy, or claims systems. For example, a flow module can invoke an AWS Lambda function in your VPC to return an account balance or claim status in the middle of an interaction.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), Canada (Central), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Europe (Frankfurt), and Europe (London). To learn more, refer to &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/acxd-data-requests.html"&gt;Data requests&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/contact-flow-modules.html"&gt;Flow modules&lt;/a&gt; in the Connect Customer documentation. To learn more about Amazon Connect Customer, an agentic AI solution that helps enterprises deliver exceptional customer experiences, visit the Amazon Connect Customer &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/acxd.html"&gt;website&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announced the general availability of application code conversion from Azure SDK to AWS SDK in AWS Transform, giving you an accelerated path to migrate Microsoft Azure cloud-native .NET applications to AWS. The AWS Transform .NET modernization agent identifies Azure service code and converts it to equivalent AWS service code. For example, an Azure Functions serverless function that uses Azure Cosmos DB can be converted to an AWS Lambda function that uses Amazon DynamoDB.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Transform discovers the Azure SDK in your submitted source code and presents the conversion options in the assessment report and modernization plan. You can accept the options provided or adjust them based on your preferences before running the modernization. AWS Transform then converts the identified Azure service code to the equivalent AWS SDK calls, and validates that the changes are incorporated into the transformed code. The feature supports conversion of Azure Functions to AWS Lambda functions, Azure Blob Storage to Amazon S3, and Azure Queue Storage to Amazon SQS, with Azure Cosmos DB to Amazon DynamoDB conversion available in preview.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Transform is available in eight AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), Asia Pacific (Mumbai, Tokyo, Seoul, Sydney), Canada (Central), and Europe (Frankfurt, London).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/latest/userguide/dotnet-azure-to-aws.html"&gt;Convert cloud-native code to AWS&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Transform User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Braket is now in scope for FedRAMP Class C in AWS US Regions where Braket is available. The Federal Risk and Authorization Management Program (FedRAMP) is a US government-wide program that delivers a standard approach to the security assessment, authorization, and continuous monitoring of cloud products and services.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With FedRAMP Class C authorization, government customers and the organizations that serve them can use Amazon Braket to explore quantum computing within an authorized boundary. The AWS-operated components of Braket, including the API and SDK, job orchestration and management, compilation, and result storage in Amazon S3, are within the FedRAMP-certified boundary, while quantum workloads execute on third-party Quantum Hardware Providers (QHPs) outside that boundary. All data sent to QHPs meets AWS' privacy and security requirements including removal of all personally identifiable information, data encryption in transit and at rest, and adherence to AWS' standard data retention and deletion policies.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about FedRAMP, visit AWS FedRAMP services and the FedRAMP Marketplace. To learn more about Amazon Braket, visit the Braket product page and Getting Started on Braket resources.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Regional Availability: US East (N. Virginia), US West (N. California), US West (Oregon)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Web Search on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore is now available in four additional AWS Regions: US East (Ohio), US West (Oregon), Asia Pacific (Sydney), and AWS GovCloud (US-West). Web Search is a fully managed tool that provides AI agents with current grounding from the web, enabling more accurate and up-to-date responses for tasks such as research, summarization, and question answering.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this expansion, customers can build agents that retrieve web information closer to their applications and end users with lower latency. Customers with government workloads can now use Web Search in the AWS GovCloud (US-West) Region to meet compliance and data residency requirements.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about Web Search on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/gateway-target-connector-web-search-tool.html"&gt;Web Search tool documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To learn about pricing, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/agentcore/pricing/"&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore pricing&lt;/a&gt;. For Region availability, visit &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/agentcore-regions.html"&gt;Supported AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Studio now lets new customers sign up with their Google, GitHub, or Apple credentials and land inside a fully configured Studio environment - no traditional AWS account setup and no console navigation required.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, new customers who discovered a model on Hugging Face or an AWS product page had to complete a multi-step AWS account creation process, provide a credit card, navigate the AWS Console to find SageMaker AI, configure an environment, and set up IAM permissions before they could begin working. With earlier launches, we reduced environment setup to twenty seconds, pre-configured model customization permissions, and introduced deep links from Hugging Face and AWS product pages so customers could land directly inside Studio with a model pre-loaded. Now, with new sign-in options, customers complete a simplified sign-up using an existing identity, receive free credits as part of the AWS Free Tier with no credit card required in most cases, and arrive inside a ready-to-use SageMaker Studio environment where they can immediately fine-tune, evaluate, and deploy foundation models. Customers can invite team members by email, and collaborators sign in with their own email credentials to access the shared Studio environment, including shared models, notebooks, and endpoints without configuring IAM users or permissions. When a project grows beyond free credits, customers can upgrade to a paid plan and set a spend limit to stay within budget. Verified customers continue to receive default GPU access to G5, G6, G6e, and G4dn instances across endpoint deployments, training jobs, and notebooks without requesting quota increases.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Commercial Regions where Amazon SageMaker Studio is supported. To get started, visit any supported model on &lt;a href="https://huggingface.co/models"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Hugging Face&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and select "Customize and Deploy on SageMaker AI" or "Deploy on SageMaker AI," or click Get Started from the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/ai/studio/"&gt;&lt;u&gt;SageMaker Studio&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; page. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/sign-in-new.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;AWS sign-up documentation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/onboard-quick-start.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;SageMaker Studio setup guide&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon DynamoDB Streams now supports timestamp-based stream processing, enabling you to begin reading stream records from a specific point in time. Previously, you could only start from the oldest retained record (up to 24 hours old) or from new records written after you start consuming the stream. Now, you can supply a timestamp and begin processing at the first record written at or after that time using the AT_TIMESTAMP shard iterator type.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Timestamp-based stream processing can be used with the Amazon Kinesis Client Library (KCL) through the DynamoDB Streams Kinesis adapter. It helps you avoid reprocessing entire retained streams or missing records when resuming stream consumption, supporting common use cases such as recovery after an interruption, data replay for debugging and auditing by reading from a specific point in the past, skipping a backlog by resuming from a recent timestamp near the latest records, and synchronizing after a restore by beginning at the export timestamp following a point-in-time recovery (PITR) export.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Timestamp-based stream processing is available in all commercial AWS Regions, the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, and the China Regions. There is no additional charge for this feature beyond standard GetRecords API request charges. To get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Streams.Timestamp.html"&gt;Amazon DynamoDB Streams timestamp documentation&lt;/a&gt; in the DynamoDB developer guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces the general availability of the AWS Lambda Durable Execution SDK for Go, enabling Go developers to build resilient, long-running workflows using Lambda durable functions. With this SDK, developers can create multi-step applications like infrastructure automation, deployment rollouts with approval gates, and pipelines that call AI models and pause for human review directly in their Go code without implementing custom progress tracking.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Lambda durable functions extend Lambda's event-driven programming model with operations that checkpoint progress automatically and pause execution for up to an year when waiting on external events. The AWS Lambda Durable Execution SDK for Go provides a native Go experience for building with Lambda durable functions. It includes steps for progress tracking, callback integration for human and agent-in-the-loop workflows, durable invocation for reliable function chaining, and waits for efficient suspension.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The SDK works with the Go tools developers already use. Developers add it to a Go module with go get and deploy their function as a single static binary on the provided.al2023 runtime, like any other Go function on Lambda. Before deploying, they can locally test complete workflows on their own machine with the durabletest package, without requiring network access or AWS credentials, and use the durablelint analyzer to catch code that could break a workflow when it resumes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see the Lambda durable functions &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/durable-functions.html"&gt;developer guide&lt;/a&gt; and the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/aws-durable-execution-sdk-go"&gt;AWS Lambda Durable Execution SDK for Go on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;. For Regional availability and pricing details for Lambda durable functions, see the AWS Capabilities by Region &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities?refid=2abe6167-e3db-40c4-a9fa-b283e7b4d7c8"&gt;page&lt;/a&gt; and AWS Lambda &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/pricing/"&gt;Pricing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Meta's Muse Spark Open is now available on Amazon SageMaker JumpStart, expanding the portfolio of foundation models available to AWS customers. This coding-optimized open-weight model from Meta is purpose-built for agentic coding workflows, bringing multi-step reasoning and tool calling to enterprise software engineering on AWS infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Muse Spark Open is designed for long-running coding agents and multimodal engineering workflows. Built on a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture with 816B total parameters and 42B active per token, it delivers the capacity of a very large network while keeping per-token compute closer to that of a much smaller dense model. The model holds a 1M-token context window, enabling it to ingest an entire project—including dependencies, legacy code, and thousands of files—without splitting tasks into smaller pieces. It accepts text, images, documents, audio, and audio-video as input and generates text output. Trained on real developer workflows such as multi-file refactors and long debugging sessions, it plans, acts, observes results, and self-corrects over long horizons rather than making single tool calls. SageMaker JumpStart offers two variants: BF16 (Muse-Spark-Open-816B-A42B) and NVFP4 quantized (Muse-Spark-Open-816B-A42B-NVFP4), with the NVFP4 variant requiring roughly a quarter of the memory for cost-efficient deployment.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With SageMaker JumpStart, customers can deploy Muse Spark Open with just a few clicks, with the endpoint running in their own AWS account for full control over source code and prompts.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started with this model, navigate to the SageMaker JumpStart model catalog in the SageMaker console or use the SageMaker Python SDK to deploy the model to your AWS account. For more information about deploying and using foundation models in SageMaker JumpStart, see the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/studio-jumpstart.html"&gt;Amazon SageMaker JumpStart documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS HealthOmics now supports sharing VPCs across accounts, enabling your workflows to access resources in Amazon Virtual Private Clouds (VPCs) managed by other AWS accounts with AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM).&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/healthomics/"&gt;AWS HealthOmics&lt;/a&gt; is a HIPAA-eligible service that helps healthcare and life sciences customers accelerate scientific breakthroughs at scale with fully managed bioinformatics workflows.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With shared VPCs, customers can centralize networking administration by sharing subnets and security groups from a single VPC across multiple accounts in their organization, and HealthOmics then places each account's workflows directly into those shared subnets. With cross-account VPC sharing, multiple accounts run inside one centrally managed VPC, instead of duplicating endpoints across VPCs. Workflows in participating accounts can access data privately without separate VPC peering connections or duplicated VPC endpoints. Previously, customers with centralized networking had to create separate AWS PrivateLink VPC endpoints in each VPC and maintain individual route tables using VPC peering. Cross-account VPC sharing reduces infrastructure overhead and simplifies network management for organizations with centralized networking while allowing researchers and developers to run bioinformatics workflows in their own accounts.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Shared VPCs is available in all Regions where AWS HealthOmics is available: US East (N. Virginia, Ohio), US West (Oregon), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London), Israel (Tel Aviv), and Asia Pacific (Singapore, Seoul, Tokyo). To get started, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/omics/latest/dev/workflows-vpc-cross-account.html"&gt;AWS HealthOmics User Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Time Sync Service introduces support for microsecond-accurate time on 135 additional EC2 instance types—spanning 12 instance families—in all commercial regions. Built on Amazon's proven network infrastructure and the AWS Nitro System, microsecond-accurate time and nanosecond-precision hardware timestamps leverage the reference clocks running in the Nitro System directly, enabling customers to easily order application events, measure 1-way network latency, and increase distributed application transaction speed.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The newly supported families include eight Gen8 families (C8a, C8i, C8i-flex, C8id, M8i, M8i-flex, R8a, and X8i) and four Gen9 families (C9g, C9gd, M9g, and M9gd).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Starting today, customers can access microsecond-accurate time on these additional instance types by creating a Precision Time Placement Group (PTPG), a new placement strategy that allows customers to launch instances with Precision Time Protocol hardware clock (PHC) enabled. Customers that require both low network latency as well as precision time can associate a PTPG with their Cluster Placement Group (CPG), so that their low-latency workloads also benefit from microsecond-accurate time.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For more information, refer to the &lt;a title="Amazon Time Sync Documentation" href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/configure-ec2-ntp.html#enhanced-amazon-time-sync-service"&gt;Amazon Time Sync Service documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS Transform for full-stack Windows modernization adds support for cross-region deployment of converted Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL databases and is available in seven additional AWS Regions. Together these changes give customers more control over where their modernized databases are deployed after SQL Server modernization, supporting data residency, latency, and disaster recovery requirements.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers can upload SQL Server Database Design Language (DDL) files and set a target AWS Region in the transformation plan to convert the database and deploy the resulting Aurora PostgreSQL cluster to a different AWS Region. The same workflow makes dependent .NET applications PostgreSQL compatible, updating connection strings and &lt;a href="http://ADO.NET" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ADO.NET&lt;/a&gt; and Entity Framework data-access calls.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this expansion, AWS Transform for full-stack Windows modernization is now available in the following Regions:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;US East (N. Virginia), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (London), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Asia Pacific (Sydney), and Canada (Montreal).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, you can go to &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/transform/windows/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;u&gt;AWS Transform product page&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; or see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/latest/userguide/windows-full-stack.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;u&gt;AWS Transform for full-stack Windows documentation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/msk/features/msk-connect/"&gt;Amazon MSK Connect&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/schema-registry.html"&gt;AWS Glue Schema Registry&lt;/a&gt; are available in the AWS European Sovereign Cloud (Germany) region. Together with Amazon MSK Standard brokers, already available in the region, these launches give customers running streaming workloads on the AWS European Sovereign Cloud a more complete set of tools to build, connect, and govern their Apache Kafka applications.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;MSK Connect lets you run fully managed Kafka Connect clusters with Amazon MSK - deploy, monitor, and scale connectors that move data between Apache Kafka and external systems like databases, file systems, and search indices, with no infrastructure to provision, automatic scaling, and pay-as-you-go pricing. Complementing it, the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/glue/"&gt;AWS Glue Schema Registry&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;- a serverless, free feature of &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/glue/"&gt;AWS Glue&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;- lets you validate and control the evolution of streaming data using Apache Avro, JSON, and Protobuf schemas. Acting as a centralized repository for data format and structure across decoupled applications, it reduces data validation logic, cross-team coordination, and downstream failures, and integrates with C# and Java applications built for &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/msk/"&gt;Amazon MSK&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/"&gt;Amazon Kinesis Data Streams&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-analytics/"&gt;Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/"&gt;AWS Lambda&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-connect.html"&gt;Amazon MSK Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/schema-registry-gs.html"&gt;AWS Glue Schema Registry&lt;/a&gt; documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/postgresql/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL&lt;/a&gt; 19 Release Candidate 1 (RC1) is available in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/create-db-instance-in-preview-environment.html" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon RDS Database Preview Environment&lt;/a&gt;, allowing you to evaluate the pre-release of PostgreSQL 19 on Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;PostgreSQL 19 RC1 focuses on stability, with bug fixes and improvements from the Beta 4 testing period. This release improves error handling for complex text-search expressions, hardens parallel autovacuum, and improves reliability of GIN index maintenance. Refer to the PostgreSQL community announcement for more details.. Refer to the &lt;a href="https://www.postgresql.org/about/news/postgresql-19-rc-1-released-3409/" target="_blank"&gt;PostgreSQL community announcement&lt;/a&gt; for more details.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon RDS Database Preview Environment database instances are retained for a maximum period of 60 days and are automatically deleted after the retention period. Amazon RDS database snapshots that are created in the Preview Environment can only be used to create or restore database instances within the Preview Environment. You can use the PostgreSQL dump and load functionality to import or export your databases from the Preview Environment. Amazon RDS Database Preview Environment database instances are priced as per the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/postgresql/pricing/" target="_blank"&gt;pricing&lt;/a&gt; in the US East (Ohio) Region.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon CloudFront now supports end-to-end IPv6 delivery to origins inside your Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC), with the new dual-stack VPC origins. With VPC origins, customers keep their Application Load Balancers (ALB), Network Load Balancers (NLB), and EC2 instances in private subnets that only their CloudFront distributions can reach. With dual-stack support, customers can now send IPv6 traffic all the way to these private origins to meet their IPv6 architecture and regulatory requirements, without exposing origins to the internet.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, CloudFront supported dual-stack for public origins but connected to VPC origins over IPv4 only. Customers faced a tradeoff: move origins to public subnets for IPv6 and give up VPC origin security or keep them private and stay on IPv4. Now, with this launch, customers can configure their VPC origins as dual-stack and get end-to-end IPv6 without sacrificing security.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Dual-stack VPC origins are available in all AWS Commercial Regions where VPC origins are supported. The full list of supported Regions is available &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-vpc-origins.html#vpc-origins-supported-regions"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;. There is no additional cost for using dual-stack VPC origins. To learn more, visit &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-vpc-origins.html"&gt;CloudFront VPC origins.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/"&gt;Amazon Redshift&lt;/a&gt; long-term retention for system table data through native integration with &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/tables/"&gt;Amazon S3 Tables&lt;/a&gt; is now available in additional AWS Regions. With this feature, you can configure your Redshift system table data retention beyond the current 7-day limit to meet your compliance, auditing, and observability requirements. Once enabled, AWS automatically writes system table data to S3 Tables in &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/apache-iceberg/"&gt;Apache Iceberg format&lt;/a&gt; and manages partitioning, compaction, and retention.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers use Redshift system tables to monitor query performance, audit data warehouse activity, and meet compliance requirements. With this feature, system table data is replicated automatically, eliminating the need for custom extract transform load (ETL) pipelines and any resource contention with your production workloads. If you operate multiple data warehouses, you can consolidate their system table data into a single location for cross-warehouse observability and analysis. Because the data is stored in the open Apache Iceberg format, you can query it through Redshift, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/athena/"&gt;Amazon Athena&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://duckdb.org/"&gt;DuckDB&lt;/a&gt;, or any Iceberg-compatible engine, and build observability dashboards using AWS services or third-party tools without additional operations overhead. Additionally, the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/tree/main/skills/specialized-skills/system-table-skills"&gt;AWS Agent Toolkit&lt;/a&gt; provides skills for querying system table data to surface performance insights and optimization recommendations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available for Amazon Redshift Provisioned &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/features/rg/"&gt;RG&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/features/ra3/"&gt;RA3&lt;/a&gt; instances and Amazon Redshift &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/redshift-serverless/"&gt;Serverless&lt;/a&gt; in the following additional AWS Regions: Asia Pacific (New Zealand), Canada West (Calgary), AWS GovCloud (US-East), and AWS GovCloud (US-West). To learn more, visit our &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/system-table-s3-tables.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; or the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/long-term-system-tables-retention-in-amazon-redshift-with-amazon-s3-tables/"&gt;blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon S3 Metadata is now available in six additional AWS Regions: Asia Pacific (Hyderabad), Asia Pacific (Malaysia), Asia Pacific (New Zealand), Asia Pacific (Taipei), Asia Pacific (Thailand), and Mexico (Central).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;S3 Metadata is a fast and easy way to instantly discover and understand your S3 data with automated, easily queried metadata that updates in near real time. This helps you curate, identify, and use your S3 data for business analytics, real-time inference applications, and more. S3 Metadata automatically populates metadata for both new and existing objects, providing you with a comprehensive, queryable view of your data. Annotations extend S3 Metadata, letting you attach rich business context directly to your objects in JSON, XML, or YAML, with up to 1 GB per object. To query annotations at scale, you can surface them in S3 Metadata annotation tables in these Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, S3 Metadata is now generally available in 38 &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/metadata-tables-restrictions.html#metadata-tables-regions" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing details, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/pricing/" target="_blank"&gt;S3 pricing page&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/metadata/" target="_blank"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/metadata-tables-overview.html" target="_blank"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/"&gt;AWS Security Hub CSPM&lt;/a&gt; now supports automated security checks that align with the Cyber Risk Institute (CRI) profile. The CRI Profile is a cybersecurity framework developed by the Cyber Risk Institute, a non-profit coalition of financial institutions and trade associations. The framework harmonizes cybersecurity and regulatory expectations that apply to the financial services sector into a common set of requirements, so that an organization can assess itself once and report against multiple regulations. AWS Security Hub CSPM provides 404 controls that support a subset of CRI Profile v2.2 requirements; the controls don't support requirements that require manual checks.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The new standard is now available in all AWS Regions where Security Hub CSPM is currently available, including the AWS GovCloud (US) and the China Regions. To quickly enable the standard across your AWS environment, we recommend that you use Security Hub central configuration. With this approach, you can enable the standard in all or only some of your organization's accounts and across all AWS Regions that are linked to Security Hub with a single action.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/standards-reference-cri-profile.html"&gt;CRI Profile v2.2&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Security Hub CSPM user guide. To receive notifications about new Security Hub CSPM features and controls subscribe to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-announcements.html"&gt;Security Hub SNS topic&lt;/a&gt;. You can also &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/cspm/pricing/"&gt;try Security Hub CSPM at no cost for 30 days&lt;/a&gt; with the AWS Free Tier offering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Managed Service for Prometheus collector, a fully-managed agentless collector for Prometheus metrics, is now available in Asia Pacific (New Zealand). Customers in New Zealand can now run their Prometheus-compatible monitoring workloads in-region, with lower latency for local workloads and the ability to keep their data in-country.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Managed Service for Prometheus collector discovers and collects Prometheus metrics from your container workloads without the need to run and operate collection agents yourself. Customers ingest the collected metrics into an Amazon Managed Service for Prometheus workspace, where they query and analyze the metrics. This fully-managed approach simplifies operational overhead by eliminating the need to maintain and scale collection infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about Amazon Managed Service for Prometheus collector, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prometheus/latest/userguide/AMP-collector.html"&gt;user guide&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/prometheus"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (MWAA) now integrates with the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/agent-toolkit/latest/userguide/what-is-agent-toolkit.html"&gt;Agent Toolkit for AWS&lt;/a&gt; to provide built-in AI agent skills that let you author, debug, and test MWAA workflows and upgrade MWAA environments using any AI coding assistant that supports agent skills.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The four skills are authoring-mwaa-workflow, debugging-mwaa-workflow, testing-mwaa-workflow, and upgrading-mwaa-environments. Together they let your AI assistant interact directly with MWAA using natural language, reducing the manual effort of building and maintaining data pipelines. For example, you can describe an ETL pipeline in plain language and the authoring skill generates a working DAG complete with task dependencies and error handling.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Amazon MWAA AI agent skills work with both Amazon MWAA Provisioned and Amazon MWAA Serverless environments, and are available in all AWS Regions where Amazon MWAA is available.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To get started, install the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/tree/main/plugins/aws-data-analytics#agent-skills"&gt;aws-data-analytics plugin&lt;/a&gt; and configure your AI coding assistant to use the MWAA agent skills. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/using-agent-skills.html"&gt;Using agent skills with Amazon MWAA&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon MWAA User Guide and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/agent-toolkit/"&gt;Agent Toolkit for AWS documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Oracle Database@AWS (ODB) now supports advertising aggregate routes and a default route (0.0.0.0/0) when configuring ODB peering between applications in an Amazon VPC and databases in an ODB network. This simplifies network operations as customers extend new VPCs or on-premises networks to reach their databases, without updating the advertised CIDRs for each network that needs connectivity to the ODB network.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, customers had to advertise each network reaching the ODB network as a separate CIDR, and could not advertise a broad aggregate range or a default route. With this launch, customers can advertise any CIDR range, so a single aggregate or default route can cover all networks reachable through the peered VPC, such as those connected through an AWS Transit Gateway, without a separate entry for each one.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available on ODB networks that support multiple ODB peering connections, in all AWS Regions where Oracle Database@AWS is available. To learn more, visit &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/featured-seller/oracle/" target="_blank"&gt;Oracle Database@AWS&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/odb/latest/UserGuide/what-is-odb.html" target="_blank"&gt;Oracle Database@AWS User Guide&lt;/a&gt;. To get started, subscribe through &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/pp/prodview-qks5dl3hr7nfw?applicationId=AWSMPContessa&amp;amp;ref_=beagle&amp;amp;sr=0-1" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Marketplace&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The next generation of &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/resilience-hub"&gt;AWS Resilience Hub&lt;/a&gt; now integrates with AWS DevOps agent allowing you to review failure mode assessment findings and implement mitigations to resolve findings and review resilience test results. AWS Resilience Hub is a central location in the AWS Management Console that helps platform engineering and site reliability teams assess and strengthen the resilience of their workloads running on AWS. It provides automated dependency discovery, generative AI-powered failure mode analysis, modular resilience policies, resilience testing, and organization-wide resilience posture reporting.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Resilience test investigations: When you run a resilience test, Resilience Hub now launches an AWS DevOps Agent investigation that observes the test and returns a structured analysis. You can uncover symptoms, root cause, and impact along with observed recovery actions, like ARC Region switch.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Interactive agent analysis from failure mode assessment findings: You can now start AWS DevOps Agent on demand task directly from a failure mode assessment finding in the Resilience Hub console, with the finding's context already in place. This allows for asking questions and investigating findings to create a mitigation plan.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/resilience-hub/"&gt;AWS Resilience Hub console&lt;/a&gt;, select a service and configure DevOps agent in the service configuration. Then launch the DevOps agent directly from a failure mode assessment finding or a resilience test. To learn more, see the product page or visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/resilience-hub/latest/userguide/next-gen-devops-agent-integration.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AWS Resilience Hub is available in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (Oregon), Canada (Central), Europe (Ireland), Europe (London), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (Paris), Europe (Stockholm), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Asia Pacific (Seoul), and South America (São Paulo).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon CloudWatch now supports conditional log routing and chained parsers via pipelines, giving you more control over how log data is transformed and which CloudWatch log group it's sent to. CloudWatch pipelines is a fully managed service that ingests, transforms, and routes telemetry to CloudWatch without managing infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Log pipelines often carry a mix of event types that belong in different places, and individual events can nest one log format inside another. With conditional log routing, you define routes that send each event to a different CloudWatch log group based on conditions evaluated against processed fields, so you can separate events by severity, source, or type and apply the right retention and access controls to each. With chained parsers, you can apply multiple parser processors in sequence, where later parsers consume fields produced by earlier ones. For example, you can parse a JSON envelope first, then apply a Grok parser to a field it extracts. Together, these capabilities let you structure diverse log formats and route them to the right log groups in a single pipeline.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Conditional routing and chained parsers are available in all commercial AWS Regions where CloudWatch pipelines is generally available. You can configure these capabilities using the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, or AWS SDKs. To get started, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/cloudwatch-pipelines.html"&gt;Amazon CloudWatch pipelines documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Studio now lets new customers sign up with their Google, GitHub, or Apple credentials and land inside a fully configured Studio environment - no traditional AWS account setup, no credit card, and no console navigation required.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Previously, new customers who discovered a model on Hugging Face or an AWS product page had to complete a multi-step AWS account creation process, provide a credit card, navigate the AWS Console to find SageMaker AI, configure an environment, and set up IAM permissions before they could begin working. With earlier launches, we reduced environment setup to twenty seconds, pre-configured model customization permissions, and introduced deep links from Hugging Face and AWS product pages so customers could land directly inside Studio with a model pre-loaded. Now, with social sign-in, new customers complete a simplified sign-up using an existing identity, receive free credits as part of the AWS Free Tier with no credit card required, and arrive inside a ready-to-use SageMaker Studio environment where they can immediately fine-tune, evaluate, and deploy foundation models. Customers can invite team members by email, and collaborators sign in with their own social credentials to access the shared Studio environment, including shared models, notebooks, and endpoints without configuring IAM users or permissions. When a project grows beyond free credits, customers can upgrade to a paid plan and set a spend limit to stay within budget. Verified customers continue to receive default GPU access to G5, G6, G6e, and G4dn instances across endpoint deployments, training jobs, and notebooks without requesting quota increases.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all AWS Commercial Regions where Amazon SageMaker Studio is supported. To get started, visit any supported model on &lt;a href="https://huggingface.co/models"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Hugging Face&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and select "Customize and Deploy on SageMaker AI" or "Deploy on SageMaker AI," or click Get Started from the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/ai/studio/"&gt;&lt;u&gt;SageMaker Studio&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; page. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/sign-in-new.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;AWS sign-up documentation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/onboard-quick-start.html"&gt;&lt;u&gt;SageMaker Studio setup guide&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can now use container images from registries such as Seqera (Wave), NVIDIA NGC, and Google Artifact Registry with AWS HealthOmics expanded support for third-party container registries. You can use the containerized tools your workflows already rely on, without manually migrating images into Amazon ECR or editing your workflow definitions.&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/healthomics/"&gt;AWS HealthOmics&lt;/a&gt; is a HIPAA-eligible service that helps healthcare and life sciences customers accelerate scientific breakthroughs at scale with fully managed bioinformatics workflows.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With expanded container registry support, you can now instruct HealthOmics to copy container images, on your behalf, from newly supported registries into your Amazon ECR when running your private workflows.&amp;nbsp;Previously, AWS HealthOmics could pull images only from registries supported through&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/08/aws-healthomics-third-party-container-registries-private-workflows/"&gt;Amazon ECR pull-through cache&lt;/a&gt; (PTC). Your private workflows can now access Amazon ECR pull-through cache registries and the newly supported third party container registries, so you do not need to pull, re-push, or rewrite image URIs yourself.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Expanded container registry support is now available in all AWS Regions where HealthOmics is available: US East (N. Virginia, Ohio), US West (Oregon), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London), Israel (Tel Aviv), and Asia Pacific (Singapore, Seoul, Tokyo). To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/omics/latest/dev/workflows-ecr.html"&gt;third-party container image support documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/"&gt;AWS Lambda&lt;/a&gt; recursive loop detection now automatically detects and stops recursive loops between Lambda Event Functions and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-custom-bus.html"&gt;Amazon EventBridge Custom event buses&lt;/a&gt;, helping protect you against unintended usage and unexpected charges from runaway workloads. &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/invocation-recursion.html"&gt;Lambda recursive loop detection&lt;/a&gt;, enabled by default on your AWS account, automatically identifies when a Lambda Event Function is invoked recursively by a supported AWS service and stops the loop.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can use EventBridge Custom event buses to invoke Lambda Event Functions, building automation workflows and scheduling recurring tasks without managing any infrastructure. However, a misconfiguration or code defect can cause EventBridge rules to trigger your Event Functions recursively, resulting in runaway usage. Now, recursive loop detection supports EventBridge Custom buses, automatically stopping such loops and sending you an AWS Health Dashboard notification with troubleshooting instructions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;EventBridge Custom event buses now support recursive loop detection in every commercial AWS Region where they are available. This feature is enabled by default on your account and requires no configuration. If your Event Function uses recursive loops intentionally, you can use the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/api/API_PutFunctionRecursionConfig.html"&gt;PutFunctionRecursionConfig API&lt;/a&gt; to turn off recursive loop detection for that function.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about recursive loop detection, visit &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/invocation-recursion.html"&gt;Lambda documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Application Recovery Controller (ARC) Region switch helps you orchestrate the failover of multi-Region applications to achieve a bounded recovery time during a Regional impairment. Today, Region switch adds four new capabilities: AWS Systems Manager Run Command execution block, payloads for custom action Lambda execution block, service quota parity checks, and wait for target group health feature for compute scaling actions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;AWS Systems Manager Run Command execution block.&lt;/b&gt; During failover, you often need to run commands on managed instances and VMs. This execution block lets you invoke Run Command actions on managed instances during failover, with plan evaluation verifying that command documents target valid instances and that IAM permissions are correctly configured.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Payloads for custom action Lambda execution block.&lt;/b&gt; ARC Region switch now lets you pass payloads to your custom action Lambda execution block, allowing reusability of your Lambda functions across recovery plans.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Service quota checks.&lt;/b&gt; Service quota limits are a common failure mode during recovery. Region switch now verifies quota parity between your primary and standby Region, and automatically submits quota limit increases, reducing the risk of insufficient service quotas during recovery.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Wait for target group health.&lt;/b&gt; Amazon ECS service scaling and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group execution blocks help you provision compute capacity during recovery, but provisioned capacity may not be ready to serve traffic. The optional wait for target group health feature verifies that your provisioned capacity is ready to serve traffic.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see the documentation for &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/r53recovery/latest/dg/ssm-send-command-block.html"&gt;AWS Systems Manager Run Command execution block&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/r53recovery/latest/dg/custom-action-lambda-block.html"&gt;custom action Lambda execution block&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/r53recovery/latest/dg/working-with-rs-service-quota-checks.html"&gt;Service quota checks&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; and the Wait for target group health for &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/r53recovery/latest/dg/ecs-service-scaling-block.html"&gt;Amazon ECS service scaling execution block&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/r53recovery/latest/dg/ec2-auto-scaling-block.html"&gt;Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group execution block&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/license-manager/"&gt;AWS License Manager&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; now supports in-place upgrades for EC2 Ubuntu LTS (Long Term Support) instances to Ubuntu Pro available on AWS Marketplace. This allows customers to strengthen their security posture and meet compliance requirements without rebuilding their fleet or migrating workloads to a new instance or AMI. With an Ubuntu Pro subscription, customers receive security patches for up to 10 years — extendable to 15 years with the Legacy add-on. This coverage extends beyond the base OS to thousands of additional packages, including widely used open-source applications such as NGINX, PostgreSQL, MongoDB, and Redis. Ubuntu Pro also supports Kernel Livepatch, which applies critical kernel security fixes without rebooting to minimize downtime.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, customers, who have an existing Canonical Ubuntu Pro subscription through AWS Marketplace, can upgrade directly during the conversion workflow. Customers who do not yet have a subscription can subscribe to Canonical Ubuntu Pro from within the AWS License Manager console. The new feature is available in &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;all commercial regions&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; where AWS License Manager is available.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, navigate to the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://us-east-1.console.aws.amazon.com/license-manager/home?region=us-east-1#/"&gt;AWS License Manager console&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt; and the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/license-manager/latest/userguide/license-asset-groups-overview.html"&gt;AWS License Manager User Guide&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS HealthOmics introduces the resource fallback directive for tasks in your Nextflow workflows, enabling you to define an ordered list of preferred accelerator types, including an option to fallback to CPU instances. Researchers and bioinformaticians can use this directive to reduce time spent diagnosing and resubmitting runs due to accelerator constraints and keep production workflows running. &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/healthomics/"&gt;AWS HealthOmics&lt;/a&gt; is a HIPAA-eligible service that helps healthcare and life sciences customers accelerate scientific breakthroughs at scale with fully managed bioinformatics workflows. &lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With resource fallback for Nextflow workflows, you can prioritize your preferred accelerator for your task. When your preferred accelerator is unavailable, HealthOmics automatically moves through your specified alternatives in the fallback without resubmission. Each accelerator profile has a configurable timeout, giving you control over how long HealthOmics searches for that accelerator before moving to the next. Shorter timeouts help you move quickly through the fallback order, while longer timeouts increase the probability of reserving your preferred accelerator. You can also include a CPU profile as a final fallback to increase the likelihood of your task reserving an instance. &lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can now use resource fallback for Nextflow workflows in all AWS HealthOmics Regions: US East (N. Virginia, Ohio), US West (Oregon), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London), Israel (Tel Aviv), and Asia Pacific (Seoul, Singapore, Tokyo). To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/omics/latest/dev/advanced-resource-configuration.html"&gt;advanced resource configuration&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/omics/latest/dev/workflow-definition-nextflow.html#workflow-nextflow-directive-fallback"&gt;Nextflow specifics&lt;/a&gt; documentation. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Backup now supports Amazon FSx for Lustre, Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP, Amazon FSx for OpenZFS, and Amazon FSx for Windows File Server in the Asia Pacific (New Zealand) Region.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, you can use AWS Backup to centrally automate and manage data protection for your Amazon FSx file systems in the Asia Pacific (New Zealand) Region, alongside your other supported AWS resources. You can create policy-based backup plans to schedule backups and apply retention rules, store backups in encrypted vaults, and restore file systems when needed. This helps you meet business continuity, disaster recovery, and regulatory compliance requirements from a single, centralized service.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit the AWS Backup console, AWS Command Line Interface (CLI), or AWS SDKs. For a complete list of supported Regions and features, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/backup-feature-availability.html#features-by-region"&gt;AWS Backup documentation&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more about AWS Backup, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/backup/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/backup/pricing/"&gt;pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For blockchain node operators running Ethereum or Solana, AWS is announcing Blockchain Snapshots on AWS, an initiative that publishes pre-synced public blockchain node chain data in &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/"&gt;Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)&lt;/a&gt; so you can bootstrap a node from a snapshot instead of peer-to-peer syncing from the network. These&amp;nbsp;snapshots are hosted on Amazon S3 within AWS infrastructure, enabling customers running nodes on AWS benefit from reduced latency and optimized data transfer. Blockchain Snapshots on AWS republishes each network's authoritative snapshot in a format optimized for parallel extraction across multiple cores, reducing setup time, that an &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/"&gt;Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)&lt;/a&gt; instance can use.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Snapshots cover Ethereum and Solana, are available at no charge, and refresh on the same cadence as the upstream provider. Use them to add capacity to a remote procedure call (RPC) fleet, recover a failed node, or re-sync a corrupted database.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can use Blockchain Snapshots on AWS in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), Europe (Ireland), and Asia Pacific (Tokyo). Access requires an Amazon EC2 instance in the same Region with an Amazon S3 gateway VPC endpoint.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://awslabs.github.io/blockchain-snapshots/"&gt;Blockchain Snapshots on AWS documentation&lt;/a&gt; to access setup instructions and download the latest snapshots.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AWS Transfer Family console now supports SVG files for logos and ICO files for favicons in web apps, with upload limits increased to 2 MB. This gives you more flexibility to use existing branding assets when customizing your web app.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, the console accepted only JPG and PNG files and limited logo uploads to 50 KB and favicon uploads to 20 KB. The console automatically resizes images and converts them to JPG or PNG before saving them. Existing API upload limits and format requirements remain unchanged.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These capabilities are available in all AWS Regions where Transfer Family web apps are offered. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transfer/latest/userguide/web-app-tutorial.html" target="_blank"&gt;Transfer Family web apps tutorial&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transfer Family now lets you store SFTP server host keys in AWS Secrets Manager in your own AWS account. This gives you greater control over your server identity and helps simplify server migrations and failover.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, Transfer Family stored host keys within the service, so you could not retrieve the associated private key material. With this launch, you can choose Secrets Manager as the storage type when creating a server or importing a host key. You can then retrieve the private key material and reuse the same host key across servers and AWS Regions. For example, you can use it on a standby server for failover or on a replacement server during migration, without requiring clients to trust a new host key. AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies let you control who can retrieve the stored keys. You can also migrate existing host keys to Secrets Manager, which may require additional steps; see the AWS Transfer Family User Guide for details.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Transfer Family is available. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transfer/latest/userguide/configuring-servers-change-host-key.html" target="_blank"&gt;Store SFTP server host keys in AWS Secrets Manager&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Transfer Family User Guide. To get started, go to the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/transfer/home" target="_blank"&gt;AWS Transfer Family console&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon S3, including S3 Tables and S3 Vectors, is now available on the Vercel Marketplace and v0 by Vercel. Developers can create and connect S3 resources in seconds without leaving their development environment, getting the durability, scalability, and performance of S3 through Vercel's streamlined provisioning workflow.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Millions of customers rely on S3 for low-cost, high-performance storage to power web and AI applications, but until now provisioning it from Vercel required navigating to the AWS Management Console. Now, S3 integrates directly into the Vercel developer experience, automatically handling resource provisioning and connection configuration. You can choose from S3 general purpose buckets for virtually any use case, S3 Tables for fully managed Apache Iceberg tables optimized for analytics workloads, or S3 Vectors to natively store and query vectors at scale.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;New AWS accounts created through Vercel include $100 in credits valid for up to six months, usable across Amazon S3, Amazon OpenSearch Serverless, and AWS Database services. Existing AWS customers can link their account to use S3 with their Vercel projects.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon S3 on Vercel is now available in all AWS Regions where Vercel is available. To get started, read the &lt;a href="http://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/amazon-s3-vercel-marketplace-v0" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS News Blog&lt;/a&gt;, visit &lt;a href="https://vercel.com/marketplace/aws" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS on Vercel Marketplace&lt;/a&gt;, or launch &lt;a href="https://v0.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;v0 by Vercel&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS Security Hub adds resource criticality to exposure prioritization, helping central security teams focus on the exposures that threaten their most business-critical resources.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can set criticality with an automation rule, for example marking every compute resource in a specific account as Very High. The rule applies to resources already in your inventory and to new resources as Security Hub discovers them. Security Hub also infers criticality from resource tags and resource names, with no configuration required, and records which source determined the value. Criticality appears as a column on the Resources page for sorting, filtering, and grouping, and on the Impact assessment tab of an exposure finding. Resource criticality is included with Security Hub Essentials at no additional cost and is always on for all customers.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Resource criticality is included with Security Hub Essentials at no additional cost and requires no new enablement. To learn more, see&amp;nbsp; &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/what-is-securityhub-v2.html"&gt;AWS Security Hub User Guide&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/"&gt;AWS Security Hub product page&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;For a list of AWS Regions where Security Hub is available, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/"&gt;AWS Region table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) announces LiveTaskCount, a new ECS service metric that reports how many tasks are actively running in your service, available to all customers at no additional charge. Additionally, the existing RunningTaskCount metric in Amazon CloudWatch Container Insights no longer incurs a charge.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;LiveTaskCount reports the number of actively running ECS tasks across all desired states (ACTIVATING, RUNNING, and DEACTIVATING), including tasks still running while transitioning to a stopped state, for example, tasks undergoing connection draining during a scale-in event. This comprehensive state coverage makes LiveTaskCount well suited for autoscaling policies and capacity planning where a complete view of active, metric-generating tasks is required. It is available at no charge as part of ECS service utilization metrics and does not require opting in to CloudWatch Container Insights. The existing CloudWatch Container Insights RunningTaskCount metric tracks tasks where both the current and desired state are RUNNING, and continues to serve as a health and stability signal for customers who depend on it. With today's update, RunningTaskCount is now available at no additional charge for all CloudWatch Container Insights users.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These updates are available in all AWS Regions where Amazon ECS is available. No action is required, and the pricing change for RunningTaskCount takes effect beginning October 1, 2026, and applies automatically. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/developerguide/service_utilization.html#cw_live_task_count"&gt;ECS CloudWatch metrics&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECS/latest/developerguide/cloudwatch-container-insights.html"&gt;ECS Container Insights metrics&lt;/a&gt;. For a pricing example, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/pricing/"&gt;Amazon CloudWatch pricing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, AWS Lambda durable functions increases the maximum number of durable operations per execution from 3,000 to 30,000, and the maximum checkpoint payload size from 256 KB to 1 MB. Higher operation limits let you safely do more work in a single execution including expanding iterative loops, polling for longer time periods, and building more complex rollback paths that run when a step fails. At 1 MB, checkpoint payloads match the maximum payload size of asynchronous Lambda invocations, and integration services such as Amazon SQS, and Amazon EventBridge, so a workflow can persist a full event from any of these sources in a single operation.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/durable-functions.html"&gt;AWS Lambda durable functions&lt;/a&gt; helps you build resilient multi-step applications and AI workflows that execute for up to one year, with built-in checkpointing, automatic retries, and waits that suspend execution without incurring compute charges. Every durable operation counts toward the per-execution limit, including each step attempt, each poll of a wait-for-condition, and each branch of a parallel or map operation. With 30,000 operations, a single execution has more room to coordinate complex, multi-step workflows, iterate across thousands of items, or poll an external system for weeks, without exceeding checkpoint limits.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An agentic application can reason over thousands of model and tool calls, and with 1 MB checkpoints it persists larger conversation histories, model responses, and outputs from tools directly in the checkpoint instead of writing to external storage. For state larger than 1 MB, the JavaScript and Python SDKs include a &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/durable-execution/sdk-reference/state/serialization/#filesystem-serdes"&gt;FileSystem serializer&lt;/a&gt; that writes the payload to an Amazon EFS or Amazon S3 Files mount and checkpoints a pointer instead.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;The new limits apply in all AWS Regions where AWS Lambda durable functions is available. To learn more about these service limits, see the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/gettingstarted-limits.html"&gt;AWS Lambda developer guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon DCV 2026.0 is now available, adding support for RHEL 10, Rocky Linux 10, CentOS 10, and Ubuntu 26.04 along with a new macOS client and updated Windows and Linux clients. Amazon DCV is a high-performance remote display protocol that enables customers to securely access remote desktops and application sessions across Windows, Linux, and macOS.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon DCV 2026.0 includes the following key features and improvements:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Support for RHEL 10, Rocky Linux 10, CentOS 10, and Ubuntu 26.04, with Linux hosts and sessions running on the modern Wayland display server&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;A new macOS client with gamepad support, and updated Windows and Linux clients&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;URL redirection on Windows hosts, so configured links opened in a remote session launch in the local browser on the client device&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Printer preferences redirection on Windows, giving remote sessions access to client-side printer settings&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Performance improvements, including H.264 frame slice support and slice-based multithreaded decoding on the Linux and macOS clients for lower latency&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Support for new macOS server versions on Amazon EC2&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For more information about the new features and enhancements in Amazon DCV 2026.0, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dcv/latest/adminguide/doc-history-release-notes.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;release notes&lt;/a&gt; or visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/hpc/dcv/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon DCV webpage&lt;/a&gt; to learn more and get started.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Deadline Cloud now lets users who have access to specific queues, fleets, or jobs, rather than an entire farm, find the resources they can reach directly from the Deadline Cloud monitor. Deadline Cloud is a fully managed service that helps teams run compute-intensive workloads for visual effects, animation, product design, simulation, and gaming.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, a user with access to only one queue had to get the farm ID from an administrator before they could reach their work. With this launch, the Deadline Cloud monitor displays to users queues they have access to, the parent IDs needed to reach them, and their level of access, covering both direct and group-inherited permissions. Teams now can share a single monitor entry point (URL) across different permission levels without additional coordination.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Resource discovery is available in all AWS Regions where Deadline Cloud is available. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/deadline-cloud/latest/userguide/view-member-resource-access.html"&gt;AWS Deadline Cloud user guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Location Service now offers a complete, interactive API Playground that lets developers explore and test every Places, Routes, and Maps API visually without writing code. With this release, all 14 Amazon Location Service Places, Maps and Routes APIs are available in an interactive API Playground, where developers can make real API calls, see results rendered on an interactive map, adjust parameters, and copy ready-to-use request snippets directly into their applications.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The API Playground covers Amazon Location Service's Places, Routes, and Maps APIs: search and geocoding (Geocode, ReverseGeocode, Autocomplete, Suggest, SearchText, SearchNearby, and GetPlace), route planning and optimization (CalculateRoutes, CalculateRouteMatrix, CalculateIsolines, OptimizeWaypoints, and SnapToRoads), and mapping (Dynamic Map and GetStaticMap). Customers can simulate validating delivery addresses, planning truck-safe multi-stop routes, visualizing drive-time service areas, or embedding maps into their apps — with no SDK setup or test scripts required.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The API Playground is available in the following AWS Regions: US East (Ohio), US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Malaysia), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Canada (Central), Europe (Frankfurt), Europe (Ireland), Europe (London), Europe (Spain), Europe (Stockholm), and South America (São Paulo).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/location/api-playground"&gt;Amazon Location Service API Playground&lt;/a&gt; in the AWS Console, or learn more in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/location/latest/developerguide/api-playground.html"&gt;Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Memory now lets you set a time-to-live (TTL) on long-term memory records, so they are automatically deleted after a retention period you define. By default, long-term memory records do not expire. With this launch, you can configure a TTL to meet data-retention requirements, control storage growth, and ensure that stale insights age out of a memory over time.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can configure retention on the resource or specific records. Configuring or removing a TTL requires the new bedrock-agentcore:UpdateMemoryRecordExpirySpecification permission, giving you independent control over who can set record retention.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/long-term-record-ttl.html"&gt;Set a time-to-live for long-term memory records&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Developer Guide. This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Memory is supported.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your coding agent can select, train, optimize and deploy models on Amazon SageMaker AI while working across the rest of your AWS environment in a single natural-language workflow. The AWS AI/ML skill is now a core skill in the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/tree/main"&gt;Agent Toolkit for AWS&lt;/a&gt;, so it works alongside the toolkit's other AWS skills like EC2, Bedrock, and more.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We released the new AWS AI/ML skill on the Agent Toolkit for AWS. The toolkit is now the single, supported integration point, replacing the previous AWS Labs skills and plugins, with a consistent and easy install and update path. The skill is also pre-installed into SageMaker Studio JupyterLab for use with Kiro or your coding agent of choice.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Moreover, we extended the SageMaker AI capability areas supported by skills.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/new-agent-skill-amazon-sagemaker-optimized-generative-ai-inference-for-your-coding-agent/"&gt;Inference optimization&lt;/a&gt;: optimize deployed models for latency, throughput, and cost&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/content/3BRdZnv6dY63soyl4qn7FJMQdqd/agentic-model-customization-simplifying-fine-tuning-with-ai-guided-workflows-in-amazon-sagemaker-ai"&gt;Model customization&lt;/a&gt;: end-to-end guided fine-tuning workflows, from use case definition through training, evaluation and deployment&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Serverful training for both traditional ML and generative AI&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;MLflow: experiment tracking and model lifecycle management&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;JumpStart model deployment and model recommendations: deploy pre-built JumpStart models and get guided recommendations on the right model for the use case&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Processing jobs: data preparation and ETL on managed compute&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The AWS AI/ML skill is available at no additional charge; you pay only for the AWS resources your agents use. To get started, install the skill from the &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/tree/main"&gt;Agent Toolkit for AWS&lt;/a&gt; on GitHub, or use it pre-installed in SageMaker Studio JupyterLab. To learn more about &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/studio-updated-jl-coding-assistant.html"&gt;using a coding assistant with your SageMaker AI workflows&lt;/a&gt;, see the Amazon SageMaker AI documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS DataSync Enhanced mode now supports Amazon FSx for OpenZFS as a source or destination location, NFS version 4.2 for on-premises NFS servers and FSx for OpenZFS file systems, and a new ObjectMetadata option for object storage transfers. Previously, customers transferring data to or from FSx for OpenZFS were limited to Basic mode.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;NFS 4.2 lets you transfer data from NFS servers on the latest version without reconfiguring them to an older protocol version. The ObjectMetadata option gives you control over whether an object's user metadata is copied to the destination, which helps when destination metadata must stay intact, such as buckets served by AWS Storage Gateway, or when source metadata keys are not compatible with the destination, such as Azure Blob Storage. Existing tasks remain unaffected and continue to run as scheduled.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These capabilities are available for Enhanced mode tasks in all AWS Regions where AWS DataSync Enhanced mode is supported. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/what-is-datasync.html"&gt;AWS DataSync documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Route 53 Domains now supports Premium Domain Names. Customers can now search for, register, transfer, renew, and restore registry premium names directly through Route 53.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Most domain registries set aside a selection of "premium" domains - names that are short, memorable, or in high demand. Previously, Route 53 Domains did not support these premium names, which meant customers who wanted them had to register and manage them through other registrars, fragmenting their portfolios across multiple providers. With this launch, customers can now discover and acquire premium domains directly in Route 53 Domains, consolidate their existing premium domains from other registrars, and manage premium and standard domains together through a single, unified experience. Finding and registering a premium domain works just like it does for standard domains. Premium domains appear automatically in domain search and availability results, with pricing shown upfront, so customers know the cost before they commit.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about Amazon Route 53 Domains and start registering premium domains, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/route53/domains/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon Route 53 Domains page&lt;/a&gt;. For a complete list of supported top-level domains, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/registrar-tld-list.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Route 53 TLD list&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/sqlserver/" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for SQL Server &lt;/a&gt;now supports the latest Cumulative Updates (CU) for Microsoft SQL Server. This release includes support for Microsoft SQL Server 2025 CU8 &lt;a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/servicing/sql/sql-server-2025/cumulative-update/kb5104822-cu8" target="_blank"&gt;KB5104822&lt;/a&gt; (RDS version 17.00.4075.5.v1).
 &lt;br&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
 For additional information on the improvements and fixes included in these updates, see Microsoft documentation for KB5104822. We recommend that you upgrade your Amazon RDS for SQL Server instances to apply these updates using Amazon RDS Management Console, or by using the AWS SDK or CLI. You can learn more about upgrading your database instance in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_UpgradeDBInstance.SQLServer.html" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon RDS SQL Server User Guide for upgrading your RDS Microsoft SQL Server DB engine&lt;/a&gt;.
 &lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, you can deliver web applications built on AWS Lambda Web Functions through CloudFront's global edge network. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With CloudFront in front of a Lambda Web Function, you get TLS termination at the edge for lower latency and faster connections, caching for better performance, and custom domains with managed TLS certificates. No changes to application code are needed. CloudFront Origin Access Control (OAC) restricts your Lambda Web Function so it's reachable only through CloudFront, so unintended requests that bypass your security protections do not reach your origin. With OAC in place, CloudFront's edge network protects your Lambda Web Function from network-layer DDoS attacks, and you can attach AWS WAF for additional application-layer security. For applications that authenticate their own users, OAC ensures that original credentials reach the application unchanged, so existing sign-in flows keep working.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;CloudFront support for AWS Lambda Web Functions is available worldwide, except in the CloudFront China region. To get started, use the CloudFront Console, SDK, CLI, or CloudFormation to configure your Lambda Web Function as a CloudFront origin. For more information, refer to the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-restricting-access-to-lambda-web.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;CloudFront Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt;. There are no additional fees associated with this feature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) G7 instances powered by NVIDIA RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell Server Edition GPUs are now available in Europe (Frankfurt) and Europe (Stockholm) Regions. G7 instances deliver up to 4.6x AI inference performance and up to 2.1 graphics performance compared to G6 instances. G7 instances also deliver faster performance for GPU-accelerated data analytics workloads.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers can use G7 instances for deploying AI models for language translation, video and image analysis, speech recognition and recommender systems. They also accelerate graphics workloads such as creating and rendering real-time, cinematic-quality graphics and game streaming. Additionally, G7 instances support video transcoding, spatial computing, and data analytics workloads. G7 instances feature up to 8 NVIDIA RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell Server Edition GPUs with 32 GB of memory per GPU and custom Intel Xeon 6 processors. They support up to 192 virtual CPUs (vCPUs) and up to 700 Gbps of Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA) networking bandwidth. They also support up to 768 GiB of system memory, and up to 7.6 TB of local NVMe SSD storage.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can use G7 instances for Amazon EC2 in the following AWS Regions: US West (Oregon), US East (N. Virginia, Ohio) and Europe (Spain, Frankfurt, Stockholm). You can purchase G7 instances as On-Demand Instances, Spot Instances, or as part of Savings Plans.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/"&gt;AWS Management Console&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cli/"&gt;AWS Command Line Interface&lt;/a&gt; (CLI), and AWS SDKs. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/instance-types/g7/"&gt;G7 instance page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Connect Customer can now fill in the step-by-step guides that agents complete after a contact, using information extracted from customer conversations, such as booking identifiers, claim numbers, and conversation summaries. Managers can set which information populates each form field. Agents can review and submit rather than manually typing in each field, saving time and improving data accuracy during after contact work.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Contact center agents spend time after each contact entering frequently used information into a step-by-step guide. For example, an insurance agent who finishes a claims call might record the customer’s policy number, a summary of the call, and a disposition code. Now, managers can define rules for which information to extract from the conversation and which fields to automatically fill in the guide. The agent can then check the information, make any necessary corrections, and submit the guide, shortening after contact work and reducing data entry errors.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can use step-by-step guides with information extraction in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), US West (Oregon), Asia Pacific (Seoul), Asia Pacific (Singapore), Asia Pacific (Sydney), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), Canada (Central), Europe (Frankfurt), and Europe (London). Contact summaries are also available in Africa (Cape Town).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more and get started, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/connect/latest/adminguide/integrate-views-conversational-analytics.html"&gt;step-by-step guides&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon Connect Customer administrator guide.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Lambda announces Lambda Web Functions, a new serverless compute primitive where your code handles HTTP requests directly. Your code listens on a port and handles requests as it would on any HTTP server. You use frameworks you already know, such as Express or Hono, with no adapters or custom configuration. This enables you to serve workloads such as AI inference endpoints, server-side rendered (SSR) web apps, streaming API endpoints, microservices, and webhooks. You pay for active CPU time and the memory your environment holds, not for idle CPU time your code spends waiting on databases or downstream services. With this launch, we are also renaming existing Lambda functions to Lambda Event Functions, a naming change only that requires no code changes or redeployments for your existing functions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Builders use Lambda to go from code to production quickly and without managing servers, across event-driven pipelines, request-response APIs, and data processing. As AI-driven development accelerates, they want to build locally and deploy with a familiar programming model and pay for workloads based on active CPU usage, while serving users with low latency. Lambda Web Functions delivers this. Today, Lambda Web Functions supports the Node.js runtime. A single warm execution environment can serve multiple concurrent requests, which reduces cold starts by orders of magnitude. You can stream results to clients incrementally with native HTTP streaming.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can create and configure Lambda Web Functions using the AWS Lambda API, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/agent-setup-guide.html"&gt;Agent Toolkit for AWS&lt;/a&gt;, AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, AWS CloudFormation, AWS SAM, AWS CDK, or the AWS SDKs. With multi-Region routing, you can deploy once and serve each request from the Region closest to the end user. Web Functions support &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/web-functions-networking.html"&gt;Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)&lt;/a&gt; connectivity, allowing your function to access resources that exist within a VPC.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Lambda Web Functions is available today in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (Oregon), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), and Europe (Ireland). For the current list, see the &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region&lt;/a&gt; page. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/web-functions.html"&gt;Lambda Web Functions developer guide&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing details, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/pricing/"&gt;AWS Lambda Pricing&lt;/a&gt;.  To see Lambda Web Functions in action, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/announcing-aws-lambda-web-functions-for-fast-consistent-web-app-performance-anywhere/"&gt;AWS News Blog announcement&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Announcing consumption-based pricing for short-term memory in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore memory now prices short-term memory (STM) based on the data your agents ingest, retrieve, and store, replacing the previous flat rate per event. AgentCore memory gives agents in-session context through short-term memory and persistent knowledge across sessions through long-term memory. With consumption-based pricing, developers building agents that write frequent, small events pay in proportion to what they use.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;STM is priced across three dimensions: ingestion at $1.00 per GB, retrieval at $0.20 per GB, and storage at $0.10 per GB-month. Ingestion and retrieval are billed per event with a 12 KB minimum and 64 KB maximum billable size, while storage is billed on each event's actual size. Most workloads see lower costs, with small-payload use cases seeing the largest reductions. You can manage storage costs by setting a shorter time to live (TTL) on your memory resources. Long-term memory pricing is unchanged.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Consumption-based pricing for AgentCore Memory short-term memory is available in all AWS Regions where AgentCore Memory is offered. To get started, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/agentcore/pricing/"&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Transfer Family now lets you configure custom ports on public SFTP servers and choose a communication mode for each port. You can support clients that require different ports or start SFTP connections differently on a single server.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Previously, public SFTP servers accepted connections only on port 22 and used server-first communication. With this launch, you can configure up to three ports (22, or any port from 2000 to 65535), allowing you to use the port numbers your business or network requires and offer multiple ports on one server. Some SFTP clients initiate communication themselves, and their connections can occasionally stall on a server-first port. For each port, you can now select client-first or server-first communication and direct clients to the port that matches their connection behavior. Using a port other than 22 can also reduce unwanted connection attempts from automated scanners that target port 22.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all AWS Regions where Transfer Family public SFTP servers are offered. To get started, visit the AWS Transfer Family console or use the AWS CLI or AWS SDKs. To learn more, visit Create an SFTP-enabled server in the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transfer/latest/userguide/create-server-sftp.html"&gt;AWS Transfer Family User Guide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Amazon S3 Express One Zone storage class is now available in 4 additional AWS Regions: Europe (London), Asia Pacific (Jakarta), Asia Pacific (Hong Kong), and Africa (Cape Town).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon S3 Express One Zone is a high-performance, single-Availability Zone storage class purpose-built to deliver consistent single-digit millisecond data access for your most frequently accessed data and latency-sensitive applications. S3 Express One Zone delivers data access speed up to 10x faster and request costs up to 80% lower than S3 Standard. It enables workloads such as machine learning training, interactive analytics, and key-value caching in AI search engines to achieve fast data access with high durability and availability.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this expansion, S3 Express One Zone is now available in &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/s3-express-Endpoints.html"&gt;19 AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt;. For pricing details, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/pricing/"&gt;S3 pricing page&lt;/a&gt;. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/express-one-zone/"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/s3-express-getting-started.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Secrets Manager now extends its managed external secrets capability to include Databricks and Twilio, enabling you to automatically rotate the credentials that your data pipelines and messaging applications use to call these services. You set up rotation directly from the AWS Management Console or API without writing or maintaining any custom rotation Lambda functions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Long-lived credentials often stay unrotated because rotation is hard to set up and hard to distribute safely to the applications that use the updated credential. With managed external secrets, you can use Secrets Manager to rotate the Databricks personal access tokens that your jobs and continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipelines use. You can also rotate the service principal OAuth client secrets that your automation workflows use. Each replacement personal access token retains the scopes of the token it replaces, so a narrowly scoped token stays narrowly scoped.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For Twilio, you can use Secrets Manager to rotate the API keys and Auth Tokens that your applications use to send messages and place calls. For both services, you store the credential in Secrets Manager as a managed external secret and set a rotation schedule. Secrets Manager always returns the current credential, so your applications pick up rotated values automatically, with no code changes required.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These integrations join existing managed external secrets support for BigID, Cisco Security Platform, Confluent Cloud, Datadog, GitLab, Jenkins, MongoDB Atlas, Netskope, Paddle, Salesforce, Snowflake, and SonarQube.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Databricks and Twilio managed external secrets are available in all AWS Regions where AWS Secrets Manager managed external secrets is supported. To learn more, visit the &amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/managed-external-secrets.html"&gt;AWS Secrets Manager managed external secrets documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/"&gt;Amazon ElastiCache&lt;/a&gt; for Valkey now features ElastiCache Insights, a pre-built dashboard in Amazon CloudWatch for your node-based clusters. ElastiCache Insights brings cluster health, memory, compute, network, command throughput, latency, keyspace, and errors into a single view sourced from a set of OpenTelemetry metrics that ElastiCache publishes automatically.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You can use ElastiCache Insights to check the health of your cache workloads across a Region at a glance, detect when a node nears its memory, network, or connection limits, and filter your view to a cluster, shard, node, or Availability Zone. You can also save a copy as the starting point for a custom dashboard tailored to your workload.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With detailed monitoring, you can choose metrics to publish every 15 seconds and view them on the Metrics tab or in your custom dashboard. &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/pricing/"&gt;CloudWatch pricing for OpenTelemetry metrics&lt;/a&gt; applies to ElastiCache detailed monitoring.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, open the Metrics tab for a cluster in the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/elasticache/"&gt;Amazon ElastiCache console&lt;/a&gt; and choose View in ElastiCache Insights. You can also choose ElastiCache Insights under Infrastructure Monitoring in the Amazon CloudWatch console.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;ElastiCache Insights is available for node-based Valkey clusters in all AWS Regions where Amazon CloudWatch supports OpenTelemetry metrics. To learn more about ElastiCache Insights, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/otel-metrics.html#otel-metrics.insights-dashboard"&gt;Monitoring ElastiCache with OpenTelemetry metrics&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon ElastiCache User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon EMR now validates data quality natively within Apache Spark jobs. Data engineering teams can catch quality issues inline before bad data reaches downstream systems, without building or maintaining separate validation infrastructure. Amazon EMR Data Quality, powered by AWS Glue, is available at no additional cost and uses the same declarative rules language (Data Quality Definition Language) and APIs as AWS Glue for a consistent experience across both engines, and works with Apache Iceberg, Apache Hudi, and Delta Lake across EC2, EKS, and Serverless deployment modes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this feature, you can author validation rules in your Spark scripts or reference rules stored in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, and then apply them at any stage of your pipeline i.e., after reading data, during transformation, or after processing. With 30+ rule types, including dynamic rules that compare against historical runs and ML-based anomaly detection for tracking drift over time without specifying fixed thresholds, Amazon EMR Data Quality provides the flexibility of choosing the validation framework that fits your needs. You can take actions like failing a pipeline, filtering invalid records, or routing them to quarantine based on the quality evaluation results. Results are produced at the dataset, rule, and row level, so that you can easily identify which records or rules are driving the quality failures.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Amazon EMR Data Quality is available on AWS runtime for Apache Spark (emr-spark-8.1.0) in all AWS Commercial, AWS GovCloud (US), and AWS China Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit Amazon EMR Data Quality &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-data-quality.html"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; and add DQDL rules for data quality validation to the Spark scripts of your EMR workloads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon OpenSearch Service now supports index-level encryption, letting you encrypt each index at rest with its own AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) customer managed key.&amp;nbsp;This helps you protect data with different ownership or sensitivity under separate keys on the same domain. For example, you can encrypt each tenant's data with its own key in a multi-tenant application.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Index-level encryption builds on the existing encryption at rest capability in Amazon OpenSearch Service. To enable it, set the encryption mode to Native and specify a customer managed AWS KMS key when you create a new domain using the AWS Management Console or the AWS CLI. You can then register additional keys with the domain and use them to encrypt individual indexes.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Index-level encryption is available for domains running OpenSearch version 3.3 or later in 14 AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia, Ohio), US West (Oregon), Canada (Central), Asia Pacific (Mumbai, Seoul, Singapore, Sydney, Tokyo), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London, Paris), and South America (São Paulo).&amp;nbsp;There is no additional charge for the feature. Standard AWS KMS and OpenSearch Service charges apply.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To get started, see &lt;a title=" Index-level encryption" href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/index-level-encryption.html" target="_blank"&gt;Index-level encryption&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon OpenSearch Service Developer Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, AWS customers in the United States can use a US bank account to sign up for AWS and pay for their AWS services through ACH direct debit. This feature provides a convenient and secure way to manage AWS payments directly from a USD bank account.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Customers can securely connect a personal or business US bank account and use it to pay AWS invoices. Previously, new AWS customers needed a credit or debit card to complete signing up for an account.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;During sign-up, customers can choose Bank Account on the AWS sign-up page, select their bank, and authenticate using their bank's mobile app or online banking credentials. This securely verifies ownership and links their bank account to their AWS account. By default, the account will be used to pay future AWS invoices.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Existing customers can add a bank account from the Payment preferences page in the AWS Billing and Cost Management console. They choose Add payment method, select Link your bank account, and follow the same bank-selection and authentication flow. Once verified, the bank account is available as a payment method to pay future AWS invoices..&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;ACH direct debit is available at no additional cost to AWS customers for USD bank accounts. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awsaccountbilling/latest/aboutv2/manage-ach-debit.html"&gt;Managing ACH direct debit&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS Security Hub announces support for exporting findings to Amazon S3 in CSV or JSON (OCSF) format. Security teams that need findings outside the console for use cases such as compliance reporting and audit evidence can now export findings from every findings page in the Security Hub console, including Threats, Exposure, Vulnerabilities, Posture Management, Sensitive Data, and All Findings, without building and maintaining their own extraction pipelines.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, you can start an export on demand from the findings page you are viewing and have Security Hub deliver the results to an S3 bucket in your account. You can choose CSV when you want to review or share findings in a spreadsheet or export in JSON if you need these findings in Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF) format.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Findings export is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Security Hub is available. To learn more, visit the AWS Security Hub User Guide at&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-v2-findings-export.html"&gt;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-v2-findings-export.html&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) M8id instances with up to 22.8 TB of local NVMe-based SSD block-level storage are now available in Asia Pacific (Malaysia) and Asia Pacific (Sydney). M8id instances are designed for workloads that require a balance of compute and memory resources along with high-speed, low-latency local block storage, including data logging, media processing, and medium-sized data stores.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Amazon EC2 M8id instances offer 3 times more vCPUs, memory and local storage compared to previous sixth-generation instances. These instances deliver up to 43% higher compute performance and 3.3 times more memory bandwidth compared to previous sixth-generation M6id instances. They also deliver up to 46% higher performance for I/O intensive database workloads, and up to 30% faster query results for I/O intensive real-time data analytics compared to previous sixth generation instances.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Additionally, customers can now adjust the network and Amazon EBS bandwidth on these instances by 25% using EC2 instance bandwidth weighting configuration, providing greater flexibility with the allocation of bandwidth resources to better optimize workloads. These instances offer Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA) networking on 24xlarge, 48xlarge, metal-24xl, and metal-48xl sizes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Customers can purchase these instances via Savings Plans, On-Demand instances, and Spot instances. For more information visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/instance-types/m8i/"&gt;Amazon EC2 M8id&lt;/a&gt; page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) C9g and C9gd instances are available in the Asia Pacific (Malaysia, Seoul, Singapore) regions. Amazon EC2 M9g and M9gd instances are available in the Asia Pacific (Malaysia) and Europe (Spain) regions. These instances are powered by AWS Graviton5 processors, the fifth generation of custom-designed CPUs, which deliver the best price performance for a broad range of workloads running on Amazon EC2.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/instance-types/c9g/"&gt;C9g and C9gd instances&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/instance-types/m9g/"&gt;M9g and M9gd instances&lt;/a&gt; deliver up to 25% better compute performance than AWS Graviton4-based C8g, C8gd, M8g, and M8gd instances. They are up to 30% faster for databases, up to 35% faster for web applications, and up to 35% faster for machine learning. These instances are built on the sixth-generation &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/nitro/"&gt;AWS Nitro System&lt;/a&gt; and are the first to feature the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/aws-nitro-isolation-engine-formally-verifying-the-hypervisor-in-the-aws-nitro-system/"&gt;Nitro Isolation Engine&lt;/a&gt;. The Isolation Engine uses formal verification to provide mathematical assurance that customer workloads are isolated from each other and from AWS operators, pioneering a new standard for mathematically proven cloud security.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;C9g instances are ideal for compute-intensive workloads such as high-performance computing (HPC), batch processing, gaming, video encoding, scientific modeling, distributed analytics, CPU-based machine learning (ML) inference, real-time analytics, and ad serving. M9g instances serve a broad range of general-purpose workloads, including application servers, microservices, gaming, caching, and containers. They also deliver the performance needed for agentic AI use cases like real-time reasoning, code generation, and multi-step orchestration. C9gd and M9gd instances add local NVMe-based SSD block-level storage for workloads that need high-speed, low-latency local storage for scratch space, temporary files, and caches.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;C9g, C9gd, M9g, and M9gd instances are available for purchase in these regions via Savings Plans, On-Demand, Spot Instances, Dedicated Instances, or Dedicated Hosts. &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/graviton/level-up-with-graviton/"&gt;Level up your compute with AWS Graviton&lt;/a&gt; and get started today with &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/instance-types/c9g/"&gt;C9g&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/instance-types/m9g/"&gt;M9g&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock now supports the reasoning.summary parameter for OpenAI models through the Responses API. This capability lets you request a human-readable summary of a model’s reasoning alongside its response, helping you understand its approach to complex tasks such as coding, analysis, and multi-step problem solving.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Reasoning summaries give developers additional context for evaluating responses, debugging applications, and showing users how a model approached a request. The summary is returned in the summary array of the reasoning output item, alongside the model’s answer.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available for all OpenAI models on Amazon Bedrock in all AWS Regions where OpenAI GPT models are available, including in-Region inference, geographic (GEO) cross-Region inference, and global cross-Region inference.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see the OpenAI documentation on r&lt;a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reasoning#reasoning-summaries"&gt;easoning summaries&lt;/a&gt;. To get started with OpenAI GPT models on Amazon Bedrock, see documentation &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/getting-started.html"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two new Anthropic models are now available in the Kiro IDE and CLI for the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Claude Opus 5.5 is Anthropic's most capable Opus model yet and a step up from Opus 5 on long-running agentic coding work. Opus 5.5 thinks adaptively on every request, deciding how much effort each task needs, and completes tasks with roughly 40% fewer tool calls and about half the tokens of Opus 5 in Kiro's internal benchmarking. Available with a 1M context window and 2.0x credit multiplier, down from Opus 5's 2.2x.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Claude Sonnet 5.5 is Anthropic's fastest Sonnet model to date and a natural upgrade for teams already building on Sonnet 5. Output is more than 30% faster, real-world coding and knowledge work approaches Opus 5.5 performance, and clearer writing makes it a stronger collaborator, giving developers a cost-performance dial between maximum capability and higher throughput. Available with a 1M context window and 1.3x credit multiplier.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Ensure your IDE or CLI is updated to the latest version, then restart it to access the new models from the model selector. For more details, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/govcloud-us/latest/UserGuide/govcloud-kiro.html"&gt;GovCloud documentation&lt;/a&gt;, the &lt;a href="https://kiro.dev/docs/enterprise/monitor-and-track/"&gt;monitoring and tracking guide&lt;/a&gt;, or contact your AWS account team. To learn more about Kiro, visit the &lt;a href="https://kiro.dev/"&gt;Kiro product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon S3 Vectors metadata pre-filtering is now available in the AWS GovCloud (US-East) and AWS GovCloud (US-West) Regions. Pre-filtering evaluates metadata filters before running similarity search, returning up to 5x more of the matching vectors when your filter is selective. S3 Vectors also adds a prefix match operator ($startsWith) for filtering on values like paths and URLs. Together, these improvements give your retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), agentic, and semantic-search applications more complete results when you filter, so your applications return more relevant answers.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;S3 Vectors provides native support to store and query vectors in Amazon S3, delivering purpose-built, cost-optimized vector storage and query at billion-vector scale. With this launch, indexes in new vector buckets in the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions use metadata pre-filtering by default, with no change to how you write vectors with PutVectors or run filtered queries with QueryVectors. To use pre-filtering on an existing index, update it in place with the UpdateIndexMode API. You can also compare pre-filtering against your current filtering on the same index, using a per-query parameter on QueryVectors, before you update.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Metadata pre-filtering is available at no additional cost in all commercial AWS Regions where Amazon S3 Vectors is available, and in the AWS China Regions. We are in the process of deploying this change and plan to complete the deployment in the coming days. To get started, use the AWS CLI, AWS SDKs, or the Amazon S3 console. To learn more, visit the Amazon S3 Vectors &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/s3-vectors-metadata-filtering.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/amazon-s3-vectors-now-supports-metadata-pre-filtering-for-higher-recall-on-filtered-searches" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS News blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://signin.aws.amazon.com/signup?request_type=register"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Lambda now supports OAuth authentication for &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kafka.html"&gt;self-managed Apache Kafka event source mappings&lt;/a&gt; (ESM), including&amp;nbsp;Kafka clusters that customers run themselves and managed offerings such as Confluent Cloud, Aiven, and Redpanda. With this launch, customers can authenticate their Lambda Kafka consumers through their enterprise identity provider, helping them meet the security and compliance requirements for their Kafka applications.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Customers building event-driven Kafka workloads for use cases such as payment processing, fraud detection, and real-time data pipelines use Kafka ESM to build serverless Kafka consumers. Kafka ESM provides automatic scaling, error handling, batching, and event filtering. Previously, Kafka ESM supported only SASL/PLAIN, SASL/SCRAM, and mutual TLS (mTLS) as&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/kafka-cluster-auth.html"&gt;authentication methods&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;so customers in regulated industries that require OAuth could not use Kafka ESM with their clusters. With OAuth support, customers can use their enterprise identity provider, such as &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cognito/"&gt;Amazon Cognito&lt;/a&gt; or Okta,&amp;nbsp;to authenticate their Kafka ESM and apply the same identity governance and access policies across their Kafka clusters and Lambda consumers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This capability is available in all&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities"&gt;AWS commercial Regions&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;where self-managed Kafka ESM is available. To use OAuth authentication, create a new Kafka ESM with your authentication configuration through the AWS Management Console, Lambda API, AWS CLI, AWS CloudFormation, or AWS SAM. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kafka.html"&gt;AWS Lambda developer guide&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/pricing/"&gt;AWS Lambda pricing&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting today, you can upload brand templates in Amazon Quick, allowing you to create presentations and documents that match your approved visual identity. Brand managers set up a template once and everyone gets on-brand output, without hunting for the right file in each chat.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Brand managers upload PowerPoint or Word templates, or pull them from a connected file store such as SharePoint. Quick extracts the colors, fonts, logos, layouts and brand rules from each file. A brand manager reviews and edits what Quick found before publishing it to the whole organization.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Quick also follows a template's rules as it creates new assets. If a request breaks a rule, Quick explains why and suggests an on-brand alternative. For each task, Quick picks the best-fit template based on guidance the brand manager writes. Users can still choose a different template.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Administrators decide who can create and publish brand templates using custom permissions, ensuring that only the proper members of brand, marketing, or other approved teams can set visual standards.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Brand assets are generally available today in the Amazon Quick desktop app. To learn more, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/brand-assets-desktop.html" target="_blank"&gt;Amazon Quick documentation,&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://portal.aws.amazon.com/billing/signup/service?app=AmazonQuickSuite&amp;amp;tier=free&amp;amp;funnel=boost&amp;amp;additionalState=ampDeviceId%3Da8b6426f-6802-4b96-8ebb-e39fff4fab5f%26ampSessionId%3D1791568256376#/validation&amp;amp;refid=0ea79374-057c-4897-84f0-5fe792905a8f"&gt;create an account for free&lt;/a&gt; and start using Quick in minutes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio now supports custom Tooling blueprints, giving domain administrators the ability to define the foundation of every project by using their own AWS CloudFormation templates. Administrators can tailor project environments to meet organization-specific requirements, such as IAM role names that comply with company naming standards or custom permissions boundaries instead of AWS managed policies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio administrators start by authoring a CloudFormation template that describes the project environment they need, then register it as a custom Tooling blueprint. The service validates the template at registration and again after each project deployment, confirming that the required resources exist before any team member uses the project. Templates can include any CloudFormation-supported resource, such as AWS Lake Formation grants, Amazon Athena workgroups, or VPC security groups, and work across multiple AWS accounts and Regions without per-project edits. At deployment time, the service automatically populates reserved template parameters, such as the project ID and domain ID, so administrators define the template once and apply it everywhere.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available in all &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/supported-regions.html"&gt;AWS Regions&lt;/a&gt; where Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio is available. To learn more, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/tooling-custom-blueprints.html"&gt;Custom blueprints as Tooling documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) C8gd and M8gd instances with up to 11.4 TB of local NVMe-based SSD block-level storage are now available in additional regions. C8gd instances are now available in Europe (Stockholm), Asia Pacific (Mumbai), and Asia Pacific (Seoul). M8gd instances are now available in Europe (Stockholm), US West (N. California), and Asia Pacific (Singapore). These instances are powered by AWS Graviton4 processors, delivering up to 30% better performance over Graviton3-based instances. They have up to 40% higher performance for I/O intensive database workloads, and up to 20% faster query results for I/O intensive real-time data analytics than comparable AWS Graviton3-based instances. These instances are built on the AWS Nitro System and are a great fit for applications that need access to high-speed, low latency local storage.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each instance is available in 12 different sizes. They provide up to 50 Gbps of network bandwidth and up to 40 Gbps of bandwidth to the Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS). Additionally, customers can now adjust the network and Amazon EBS bandwidth on these instances by 25% using EC2 instance bandwidth weighting configuration, providing greater flexibility with the allocation of bandwidth resources to better optimize workloads. These instances offer Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA) networking on 24xlarge, 48xlarge, metal-24xl, and metal-48xl sizes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;C8gd instances are ideal for compute-intensive workloads such as high-performance web servers, batch processing, distributed analytics, ad serving, video encoding, and gaming servers. M8gd instances are well-suited for balanced workloads including application servers, microservices, enterprise applications, and small to medium databases.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/instance-types/c8g/"&gt;Amazon C8gd Instances&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/instance-types/m8g/"&gt;Amazon M8gd Instances&lt;/a&gt;. To explore how to migrate your workloads to Graviton-based instances, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/graviton/fast-start/"&gt;AWS Graviton Fast Start program&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://github.com/aws/porting-advisor-for-graviton"&gt;Porting Advisor for Graviton&lt;/a&gt;. To get started, see the &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/ec2"&gt;AWS Management Console&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon Bedrock now supports TwelveLabs Pegasus 1.5, a video-to-text model that generates structured, time-coded metadata from video. Until now, finding a moment in a large video library meant knowing where to look before you could ask about it. Pegasus 1.5 reads an entire video and labels what happens and when, turning footage into data your applications can query.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You define what matters to your business, such as editorial segments, speaker changes, sports plays, or brand appearances, and Pegasus 1.5 finds those moments across the video and returns them with timestamps. It analyzes what it sees, hears, and reads on screen in a single pass, with no pre-indexing or ingestion pipeline to build first.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Pegasus 1.5 is accessible through the US and Global cross-Region inference profiles. To get started, visit the Amazon Bedrock product page, access the model in the Amazon Bedrock console, or see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-card-twelvelabs-pegasus-v1-5.html"&gt;Amazon Bedrock documentation&lt;/a&gt; for supported Regions, APIs, features, and pricing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces the availability of Ultrafast mode for GPT-6.1 Sol from OpenAI on Amazon Bedrock.&amp;nbsp;Ultrafast mode provides faster inference for workloads where speed matters most. The Amazon Bedrock inference engine delivers the performance, security, and reliability required for production workloads.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Use GPT-6.1 Sol&amp;nbsp;Ultrafast for latency-sensitive applications such as real-time coding assistants, interactive agents, and customer-facing experiences that require fast, high-quality responses. GPT-6.1 Sol helps developers build features, debug issues, investigate complex codebases, and complete multistep workflows. Established AWS controls help you secure workloads, govern access, and audit model invocation activity.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can get started in the &lt;a href="https://us-east-1.console.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/home?region=us-east-1#/"&gt;Amazon Bedrock console&lt;/a&gt; or programmatically through supported Amazon Bedrock APIs. For information about supported AWS Regions, endpoints, APIs, features, inference profiles, and pricing, see the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/model-card-openai-gpt-6-1-sol.html"&gt;Amazon Bedrock documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Cost Explorer, AWS Budgets, and AWS Cost Management Dashboards now let you analyze Amazon Bedrock costs by product attributes, a new dimension that breaks down Bedrock cost by model, model provider, inference type, and feature. In Cost Explorer and Dashboards, you can group and filter costs by these attributes. In Budgets, you can filter budgets by the same attributes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, finance and platform teams get a direct view of which models and workloads drive their Bedrock spend, and can act on it in the billing tools they already use. The attributes include model (such as Claude Sonnet 5 or Claude Haiku 4.5), model provider (such as Anthropic or Cohere), inference type (such as input tokens or output tokens), and feature (such as on-demand inference or reranker). You can combine product attributes with cost allocation tags on application inference profiles, projects, or workspaces to see which applications drive spend on each model, and with IAM principal tags to see which users or roles drive it. Group a month of Bedrock spend by model in Cost Explorer to find your most expensive models, build a dashboard widget that tracks cost per provider, or set a budget that alerts on spend for a single model.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Amazon Bedrock product attribute dimension is available today at no additional charge in all AWS Regions, excluding the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions and the China (Beijing) and China (Ningxia) Regions. To learn more, see the user guides for&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/ce-what-is.html"&gt;Cost Explorer&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/budgets-managing-costs.html"&gt;Budgets&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/dashboards.html"&gt;Dashboards&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/oracle/"&gt;Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for Oracle&lt;/a&gt; now lets you run a minor version upgrade precheck yourself, before you start an upgrade or before your scheduled maintenance window. RDS for Oracle now also emits a new event during patching that tells you when your DB instance is accepting connections again. With this event, you can bring your applications back while the remaining maintenance tasks are still in progress, which can reduce your application downtime during patching.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At the start of every minor version upgrade, RDS for Oracle checks for conditions that would prevent the upgrade from completing, such as insufficient free storage, invalid Oracle-maintained objects, or invalid triggers. If a check finds a problem, the upgrade stops, and you must fix the problem and schedule the upgrade again. Previously, these checks ran only as part of the upgrade itself. With the new rdsadmin.rdsadmin_precheck_tasks.precheck_minor_upgrade procedure, you can run the checks at any time. The precheck is read-only and doesn't affect your workload. It reports every issue it finds in a single log so you can fix them and run the precheck again until it passes before your maintenance window.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;During a minor version upgrade or an operating system update, RDS for Oracle now emits RDS-EVENT-0596 as soon as your DB instance is accepting connections. Any remaining maintenance tasks then complete while your database is online. You can subscribe to this event through Amazon RDS event notifications or Amazon EventBridge to automate application reconnection or notify your teams that the database is back online.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These capabilities are available in all AWS commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions where Amazon RDS for Oracle is available. To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_UpgradeDBInstance.Oracle.Precheck.html"&gt;Running minor version upgrade prechecks in Amazon RDS for Oracle&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_Events.Messages.html"&gt;Amazon RDS event categories and event messages&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/working-with-events.html"&gt;Monitoring Amazon RDS events&lt;/a&gt; in the Amazon RDS User Guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Network Firewall now supports wildcard matching in container attribute-based inspection filters for Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) and Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS). This enhancement allows you to define attribute filters using wildcard patterns to match multiple container workloads in a single rule, eliminating the need to create individual container associations for each variant.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With wildcard support, you can use patterns like app=payments-* to automatically cover all variants of an application, such as payments-api, payments-worker, and payments-cron. This simplifies firewall rule management for dynamic container environments where new application variants are frequently deployed, reducing operational overhead and ensuring consistent security coverage across your containerized workloads.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Wildcard support for container attribute filters is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Network Firewall container attribute-based inspection is supported. For a full list of supported Regions, see the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Region Table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To get started, visit AWS Network Firewall &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;product page&lt;/a&gt; and service &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/network-firewall/latest/developerguide/container-associations.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon GameLift Servers now supports CPU burstability for container fleets, giving game developers greater flexibility in how compute resources are allocated and consumed at runtime. Previously, container fleets relied on a fixed CPU value that simultaneously controlled both container packing density and imposed a hard runtime cap. This dual constraint forced developers to choose between efficient resource utilization and maintaining performance headroom for their game server workloads.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Game server workloads are inherently bursty, CPU demand stays low during most of a match but spikes sharply during physics calculations, AI processing, or collision detection. With CPU burstability, developers can now pack containers based on typical CPU needs while still allowing game server processes to burst into available idle instance CPU during peak demand moments. This opt-in feature is configured per container group definition, supports high-density deployments without sacrificing peak performance, and is included at no additional cost as part of standard container fleet pricing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CPU burstability for Amazon GameLift Servers container fleets is available in all AWS Regions where container fleets are supported, excluding China Regions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more about CPU burstability for Amazon GameLift Servers container fleets, visit the &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/gameliftservers/latest/developerguide/release-notes.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon GameLift Servers Developer Guide&lt;/a&gt; or explore the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/gamelift/servers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon GameLift Servers product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Amazon GuardDuty announces an expansion of RDS Protection that extends threat detection beyond login anomalies to identify data exfiltration and data destruction attacks targeting Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL and Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL databases. Available as RDS Protection for data activity, this new capability uses machine learning (ML)-based anomaly detection and integrated threat intelligence to continuously monitor database query patterns, helping you detect unusual data activity without requiring any agents, additional infrastructure, or database configuration changes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As attackers increasingly target databases to exfiltrate or destroy critical data after obtaining valid credentials through phishing, social engineering, or misconfiguration, their malicious database activity can appear indistinguishable from legitimate application behavior.&amp;nbsp; RDS Protection for data activity establishes behavioral baselines for normal query patterns enabling GuardDuty to detect threats that traditional tools miss.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can enable GuardDuty RDS Protection for data activity as an add-on to RDS Protection for login activity. With a single click, they can enable it for their entire organization in the GuardDuty console, or programmatically via APIs, SDKs, CLI, or AWS CloudFormation. A 30-day free trial is available per account per Region.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This feature is available today in all AWS commercial Regions and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, where Amazon GuardDuty RDS Protection is offered with support for Aurora PostgreSQL and Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. For supported versions visit GuardDuty RDS Protection &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/rds-protection.html#rds-pro-supported-db"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To learn more and start your free trial, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/guardduty"&gt;Amazon GuardDuty product page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="text-align: left;"&gt;Starting today, Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) C8gb instances, powered by the latest-generation AWS Graviton4 processors, are available in the US East (Ohio), US West (N. California), EU (Frankfurt, Ireland, Paris, Stockholm), and Africa (Cape Town) regions. The new instances provide up to 30% better compute performance than AWS Graviton3-based EC2 instances. At up to 300 Gbps EBS bandwidth and 1600K IOPS, C8gb instances offer higher EBS performance compared to same-sized equivalent Graviton4-based instances.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="text-align: left;"&gt;Take advantage of the higher block storage performance offered by these new EBS optimized EC2 instances to scale the performance and throughput of workloads such as high-performance file systems, while optimizing the cost of running your workloads.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="text-align: left;"&gt;For increased scalability, these instances offer instance sizes up to 48xlarge, up to 384 GiB of memory, up to 300 Gbps of EBS bandwidth, up to 400 Gbps of networking bandwidth. These instances support Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA) networking on the 16xlarge, 24xlarge, 48xlarge sizes, which enables lower latency and improved cluster performance for workloads deployed on tightly coupled clusters.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="text-align: left;"&gt;C8gb instances are available in the following AWS Regions: US East (N. Virginia, Ohio), US West (N.California, Oregon), EU (Frankfurt, Ireland, Paris, Stockholm), and Africa (Cape Town).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="text-align: left;"&gt;To learn more, see &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/instance-types/c8g/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon EC2 C8gb Instances&lt;/a&gt;. To begin your Graviton journey, visit the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/graviton/level-up-with-graviton/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Level up your compute with AWS Graviton page&lt;/a&gt;. To get started, see &lt;a href="https://console.aws.amazon.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Management Console&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/cli/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI)&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSJavaScriptSDK/latest/AWS/EC2.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS SDKs&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, AWS announces feature-level availability notifications and advanced filters for &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities/explore"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region&lt;/a&gt; in AWS Builder Center. You can now set availability notifications for AWS service, or to an individual feature of an AWS service, in the AWS Regions you are interested in, and receive an in-app notification and a weekly email digest covering all the capabilities that became available since you enabled availability notifications. Setting availability notifications for a service covers every feature within it, and an 'All regions' option covers every Region, including AWS Regions that launch in the future.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In addition to the feature level availability notifications , you can now filter API operations and CloudFormation resources by availability status and compare Regions to show all capabilities, only matching ones, or only differences. With over 19,000 API operations and more than 5,000 CloudFormation resource types, these filters quickly narrow a full listing down to the exact differences between Regions, working alongside search and existing display preferences.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Comparing capabilities in AWS Capabilities by Region requires no account. Setting up availability notifications requires a Builder ID, which is free. To get started, visit &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/build/capabilities/explore"&gt;AWS Capabilities by Region&lt;/a&gt; on AWS Builder Center. To learn more, explore the &lt;a href="https://builder.aws.com/faq#aws-capabilities-by-region"&gt;AWS Builder Center FAQ&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AWS Config now supports 77 additional AWS resource types across key services including Amazon EC2, Amazon S3 Files, and Amazon Q Business. This expansion provides greater coverage over your AWS environment, enabling you to more effectively discover, assess, audit, and remediate an even broader range of resources.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With this launch, if you have enabled recording for all resource types, then AWS Config will automatically track these new additions. The newly supported resource types are also available in Config rules and Config aggregators.&lt;/p&gt;
